:5},{"n":"Projects & Talent Pool","c":2}],"t":9},{"i":9,"n":"Credits, Plans & Billing","u":"billing","b":"Credits, profile unlocks, your packages, choosing a plan, and invoices.","ico":11,"sec":[{"n":"Credits","c":5},{"n":"Unlocking Profiles","c":3},{"n":"What You Have","c":4},{"n":"Choosing A Plan","c":5},{"n":"Payment & Invoices","c":6}],"t":23},{"i":10,"n":"Walk-In Campaigns","u":"walk-in","b":"Advertising a hiring day where candidates are interviewed on the spot.","ico":9,"sec":[{"n":"Running A Campaign","c":4}],"t":4},{"i":11,"n":"Team & Account Settings","u":"team-account","b":"Colleagues, roles, your own login details and alerts.","ico":7,"sec":[{"n":"Your Team","c":4},{"n":"Account & Alerts","c":3}],"t":7},{"i":12,"n":"Troubleshooting & Help","u":"troubleshooting","b":"Fixes for common problems, and how to reach a human.","ico":15,"sec":[{"n":"Common Problems","c":4},{"n":"Getting Help","c":2}],"t":6},{"i":13,"n":"Quick Reference","u":"quick-reference","b":"One-page tables: what costs what, every deadline, and what cannot be undone.","ico":17,"sec":[{"n":"Cheat Sheets","c":3}],"t":3}],"a":[{"u":"what-is-ajobthing-and-how-does-it-work-with-maukerja-and-ricebowl","t":"What is AJobThing, and how does it work with Maukerja and Ricebowl?","c":1,"s":"About AJobThing","b":"<p><strong>AJobThing is the employer side. Maukerja and Ricebowl are where jobseekers apply.</strong> You post one job ad here, it goes out to the jobseeker sites, and every application comes back to one list in your account.</p>\n<h2>Who uses what</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AJobThing</strong> &mdash; where you post jobs, manage applicants and message candidates.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://www.maukerja.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Maukerja</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"https://www.ricebowl.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ricebowl</a></strong> &mdash; the jobseeker sites where people search and apply.</li>\n<li><strong>EpiCareer</strong> &mdash; also part of the same group.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What this means in practice</h2>\n<p>You do not manage three separate postings. One ad is distributed for you, and applications from every source arrive in the same place, so there is a single list to work through &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied\">where you see everyone who applied</a>. The full list of destinations is in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-my-job-ads-appear\">where your job ads appear</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>A Maukerja or Ricebowl login will not work here.</strong> Those are jobseeker accounts. You need an AJobThing employer account &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-log-in-and-what-if-i-can-t\">how to log in</a>, which is the single most common reason a new employer cannot get in.</div>\n<p>New to the platform? <a href=\"#/a/what-should-i-do-before-posting-my-first-job\">What to do before posting your first job</a> is the right next step.</p>","r":["where-do-my-job-ads-appear","how-do-i-log-in-and-what-if-i-can-t"]},{"u":"where-do-my-job-ads-appear","t":"Where do my job ads appear?","c":1,"s":"About AJobThing","b":"<p><strong>One job ad goes to several places automatically</strong> &mdash; you do not post anywhere separately.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://www.maukerja.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Maukerja</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"https://www.ricebowl.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ricebowl</a></strong> &mdash; as soon as the ad is published.</li>\n<li><strong>EpiCareer</strong> and partner job sites.</li>\n<li>AJobThing also sends published jobs to <strong>Google Jobs</strong>, <strong>Facebook Jobs</strong> and <strong>LinkedIn Jobs</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Distribution starts once the ad is approved and turns Active, not when you submit it &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live\">why your job ad is Pending</a>.</p>\n<h2>To post on only one site</h2>\n<p>Ask your Account Manager. By default the ad goes to both Maukerja and Ricebowl.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>LinkedIn needs two extra things.</strong> Before you post, AJobThing tells you LinkedIn requires a public company name (not \"Confidential\") and a basic salary. Choose <strong>Edit Job</strong> to add them, or <strong>Continue Post Job</strong> without them. That means the <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-confidential-toggle\">Confidential toggle</a> and <a href=\"#/a/what-does-this-job-has-no-basic-salary-mean\">no basic salary</a> each rule LinkedIn out on their own.</div>\n<p>If the ad is Active but you cannot find it on a jobseeker site, work through <a href=\"#/a/my-ad-is-active-but-has-not-appeared-on-maukerja-or-ricebowl\">my ad is Active but has not appeared on Maukerja or Ricebowl</a>.</p>","r":["what-is-the-confidential-toggle","my-ad-is-active-but-has-not-appeared-on-maukerja-or-ricebowl"]},{"u":"how-do-i-log-in-and-what-if-i-can-t","t":"How do I log in, and what if I can't?","c":1,"s":"Signing In","b":"<p><strong>Log in with the email address or phone number on your employer account.</strong> Either one works.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Go to the AJobThing login page.</li>\n<li>Type your <strong>email address or phone number</strong> and click <strong>Next</strong>.</li>\n<li>Enter your password.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>You land on your <strong>Dashboard</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-numbers-on-my-dashboard-mean\">what the numbers on your Dashboard mean</a>.</p>\n<h2>If you cannot get in</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Check you are not using a Maukerja or Ricebowl jobseeker login.</strong> This is the most common cause. Those are jobseeker accounts and will not open an employer account &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-ajobthing-and-how-does-it-work-with-maukerja-and-ricebowl\">how AJobThing works with Maukerja and Ricebowl</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgot which email you used?</strong> Use <strong>Forget email or phone?</strong> on the login page.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrong password?</strong> See <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-reset-a-forgotten-password\">how to reset a forgotten password</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>No account yet?</strong> Use <strong>Create new Account</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Someone else set up the company account?</strong> Ask them to <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-team-member\">invite you as a team member</a> rather than sharing their login.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\">If the login page itself will not load or shows empty boxes, that is a browser problem rather than an account problem &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/a-page-is-stuck-loading-or-shows-empty-boxes\">a page is stuck loading</a>. If nothing here works, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">contact AJobThing</a> with the email or phone number you are trying.</div>","r":["how-do-i-reset-a-forgotten-password","how-do-i-change-my-name-email-contact-number-or-password"]},{"u":"how-do-i-reset-a-forgotten-password","t":"How do I reset a forgotten password?","c":1,"s":"Signing In","b":"<p><strong>Use Forgot Password on the login page &mdash; the reset link comes by email.</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Click <strong>Forgot Password</strong> on the login page.</li>\n<li>Enter the email address on your account and submit.</li>\n<li>Check your email for the reset link, and open <strong>Spam</strong> and <strong>Junk</strong> too &mdash; that is where it most often ends up.</li>\n<li>Follow the link and set a new password, then log in with it.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>If nothing arrives</h2>\n<p>Wait about 10 minutes and check spam again before trying a second time. If it still has not come through, the likeliest reasons are that the email address on the account is different from the one you tried, or your company mail filter is holding the message. Ask your IT or mail administrator to release it, then <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">contact AJobThing</a> with the email address you are using if you are still locked out.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Just registered?</strong> AJobThing emails you a password &mdash; check spam if you cannot find it, rather than assuming the account was not created.</div>\n<p>Once you are back in, you can set a password you will remember under Account Details &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-change-my-name-email-contact-number-or-password\">how to change your email or password</a>. If the problem is that you cannot remember which email or phone number the account uses, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-log-in-and-what-if-i-can-t\">the login article</a> covers the <strong>Forget email or phone?</strong> option.</p>","r":["how-do-i-log-in-and-what-if-i-can-t","can-two-people-share-one-login"]},{"u":"can-two-people-share-one-login","t":"Can two people share one login?","c":1,"s":"Signing In","b":"<p><strong>Don't share a login.</strong> Give each colleague their own account under <strong>Team Management</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-team-member\">how to invite a team member</a>.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>You lose the audit trail on spending.</strong> <strong>Credit History</strong> records a <strong>User Name</strong> against every credit spent, so a shared login makes it impossible to see who unlocked which profile. See <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">where you see your credit balance and history</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>You cannot tell who is active.</strong> The team list shows each person's <strong>Last Login</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Offboarding becomes all-or-nothing.</strong> You cannot remove one person's access without locking out everyone who uses that login &mdash; and the password change hits your whole team at once.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to do instead</h2>\n<p>Invite each person and give them the role that matches what they actually need to do &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-the-roles-and-what-can-each-one-do\">what the roles can each do</a>. Applicants and chats are shared across the account either way, so separate logins cost you nothing in visibility; see <a href=\"#/a/can-my-colleagues-see-the-same-applicants-and-chats\">can my colleagues see the same applicants and chats</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">If a colleague only needs the application emails, you do not need to give them an account at all &mdash; add them as an additional recipient on the job ad instead. See <a href=\"#/a/who-gets-told-when-someone-applies\">who gets told when someone applies</a>.</div>","r":["how-do-i-invite-a-team-member","what-are-the-roles-and-what-can-each-one-do"]},{"u":"what-should-i-do-before-posting-my-first-job","t":"What should I do before posting my first job?","c":1,"s":"First Steps","b":"<p><strong>Four things, in this order.</strong> Doing them before your first ad goes live is what makes the difference between an ad jobseekers trust and one they scroll past.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Complete your company profile.</strong> Logo, description, address, benefits. Jobseekers read it before applying &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">what your Company Page is and where jobseekers see it</a> and <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard\">the Profile % on your Dashboard</a>, which shows what is still missing.</li>\n<li><strong>Get your company verified.</strong> The blue tick tells jobseekers the company is real, which matters most for employers they have not heard of. See <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-get-my-company-verified\">how to get your company verified</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Check your job ad balance.</strong> Your Dashboard shows <strong>FREE JOB ADS</strong> and <strong>JOB AD PACKAGES</strong>. Decide upfront whether this role can be posted free &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/which-job-ads-are-free\">which job ads are free</a>, since the rule depends on how the title is written.</li>\n<li><strong>Decide who gets the applications.</strong> You can add up to five colleagues per job ad &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/who-gets-told-when-someone-applies\">who gets told when someone applies</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Then post</h2>\n<p>With those in place, work through <a href=\"#/a/what-do-i-need-to-prepare-before-posting-a-job-ad\">what to prepare before posting a job ad</a> and <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step\">how to post a job, step by step</a>. Your ad will not go live the moment you submit it &mdash; it goes to review first, so allow for that when planning a start date.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Get the job title right before you publish.</strong> It cannot be edited afterwards, and it is the field jobseekers search on. See <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">how to write a job title jobseekers will find</a>.</div>","r":["how-do-i-get-my-company-verified","which-job-ads-are-free"]},{"u":"what-do-the-numbers-on-my-dashboard-mean","t":"What do the numbers on my Dashboard mean?","c":1,"s":"First Steps","b":"<p><strong>The four numbers at the top of your Dashboard are your daily to-do list</strong> &mdash; each one points at something waiting for you.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Number</th><th>What it means</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Active job ads</strong></td><td>Ads live now, taking applications</td><td>Open <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page\">Job Ads &amp; Application</a> to review them</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>New applicants</strong></td><td>Applications you have not opened</td><td><a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied\">Open the applicant list</a> and work through them</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Unresponded chats</strong></td><td>Candidates waiting for your reply</td><td>Reply before <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-countdown-in-my-chat\">the chat countdown</a> runs down</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Credits</strong></td><td>Your credit balance</td><td><a href=\"#/a/what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on\">See what credits can be spent on</a></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Below them, <strong>Your job ad packages</strong> shows your <strong>FREE JOB ADS</strong> and <strong>JOB AD PACKAGES</strong> balances with their expiry dates &mdash; the fuller breakdown is in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">where you see your packages and balances</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Start your day here.</strong> If <strong>New applicants</strong> or <strong>Unresponded chats</strong> is not zero, open those first &mdash; candidates who wait usually accept somewhere else.</div>\n<h2>If a Dashboard number disagrees with another screen</h2>\n<p>They are usually counting different things rather than being wrong:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>New applicants</strong> here counts applications across <em>every</em> ad. The Applicants figure on a job card counts that one ad only.</li>\n<li><strong>Credits</strong> here is your total across all batches; <strong>Purchased Credits Details</strong> breaks the same total down batch by batch &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">your credit balance and history</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Package REMAINING</strong> in Billing is the balance on one package, not a total for your account &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">your packages and balances</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Total Ads</strong> on a job card includes the free similar versions of your ad, not just the one you posted &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-the-coloured-tags-on-my-job-cards\">the tags and numbers on your job cards</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Match each number to what it is actually counting and reload both screens before assuming something is broken. If one still looks wrong, screenshot both and <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-report-a-problem\">report it through Send Feedback</a>.</p>","r":["where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances","what-should-i-do-before-posting-my-first-job"]},{"u":"what-does-the-blue-verified-company-badge-mean","t":"What does the blue Verified Company badge mean?","c":2,"s":"Profile & Verification","b":"<p><strong>It tells jobseekers your company is real.</strong> In AJobThing's words, a verified account \"shows to jobseekers that your company is more trustworthy, and you will be able to get more applicants compared to an unverified company.\"</p>\n<h2>Where it appears</h2>\n<p>The tick sits next to your company name on <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">your Company Page</a> and on your job ads &mdash; the two places a jobseeker looks before deciding whether to apply.</p>\n<h2>Why it changes results</h2>\n<p>Jobseekers are cautious, and job scams have made them more so. When they have never heard of your company, the badge is the quickest reassurance they get that the employer exists and the ad is genuine. That matters most for smaller companies and for roles where applicants are asked to travel for an interview.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Your company tick and your login are separate things.</strong> Your own email and phone number are verified on their own under <strong>Account Details</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-change-my-name-email-contact-number-or-password\">how to change your email or password</a>. Having a verified email does not give the company a badge.</div>\n<p>Don't have the badge yet? See <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-get-my-company-verified\">how to get your company verified</a> &mdash; and note that a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-confidential-toggle\">Confidential job ad</a> hides your company name, so the badge does nothing for that ad.</p>","r":["how-do-i-get-my-company-verified","what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard","how-do-i-edit-my-company-details-and-what-can-t-i-change"]},{"u":"how-do-i-get-my-company-verified","t":"How do I get my company verified?","c":2,"s":"Profile & Verification","b":"<p><strong>Send your company verification documents to AJobThing.</strong> Verification is a manual check, so it is not something you can switch on yourself.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Gather your company registration documents.</li>\n<li>Submit them to AJobThing. If you cannot find where to upload them, ask your Account Manager or message <a href=\"https://wa.me/60189666610\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhatsApp +6018-966 6610</a> for the current link.</li>\n<li>Wait for the check &mdash; <strong>it takes up to 3 working days</strong>.</li>\n<li>Look for the blue tick next to your company name on your Company Page and job ads.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What to expect</h2>\n<p>Nothing about your account stops while you wait &mdash; you can post ads and receive applications as usual. The badge simply appears once the check clears. If the three working days have passed and the tick has not shown up, follow up with your Account Manager or <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">contact AJobThing</a> rather than resubmitting, and quote the date you sent the documents.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Do it before your first ad, not after.</strong> The badge is what <a href=\"#/a/what-does-the-blue-verified-company-badge-mean\">tells jobseekers the company is real</a>, so having it in place from day one means your earliest ads benefit too. Pairing it with a complete profile has the biggest effect &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard\">the Profile % on your Dashboard</a> and <a href=\"#/a/what-should-i-do-before-posting-my-first-job\">what to do before posting your first job</a>.</div>\n<p>Which documents are required depends on your company type, so ask AJobThing directly rather than assuming what is needed.</p>","r":["what-does-the-blue-verified-company-badge-mean","how-do-i-contact-ajobthing"]},{"u":"what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard","t":"What is the Profile % on my Dashboard?","c":2,"s":"Profile & Verification","b":"<p><strong>It shows how complete your public company profile is</strong> &mdash; logo, banner, description, photos, benefits, address and FAQ answers. The percentage is a checklist score, not a rating of your company.</p>\n<h2>How to raise it</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Click <strong>Improve</strong> in the side menu, or <strong>Show Checklist</strong> on your <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">Company Page</a>.</li>\n<li>Work through whatever is still missing.</li>\n<li>Preview the page as a jobseeker to check it reads well, not just that the score went up.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>AJobThing's note on that screen: \"Finish the last steps to rank higher on the leaderboard and attract more candidates.\"</p>\n<h2>Where to start if it is low</h2>\n<p>Not every item takes the same effort or gives the same return. The description, logo and benefits are what jobseekers actually read, so do those first. Then:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-add-photos-to-my-company-page\">Add photos</a> &mdash; quick, and they show what working there looks like.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-answer-the-questions-jobseekers-ask\">Answer the Company FAQ questions</a> &mdash; these also earn credits.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#/a/what-are-employee-stories\">Add Employee Stories</a> if you have willing staff.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\">A complete profile works hardest alongside <a href=\"#/a/what-does-the-blue-verified-company-badge-mean\">the Verified Company badge</a>. Together they are the two things that reassure a jobseeker who has never heard of your company &mdash; and a thin profile is one of the causes listed in <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">why a job ad is not getting applicants</a>.</div>","r":["what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it","what-does-the-blue-verified-company-badge-mean","how-do-i-get-my-company-verified"]},{"u":"how-do-i-edit-my-company-details-and-what-can-t-i-change","t":"How do I edit my company details, and what can't I change?","c":2,"s":"Profile & Verification","b":"<p><strong>Open Company Branding &rarr; Company Page.</strong> Almost everything on your public profile is editable there, whenever you like.</p>\n<h2>What you can edit</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Logo, banner, company size and industry</li>\n<li>Your description, in English, Mandarin and Bahasa Malaysia</li>\n<li><strong>Business Information</strong> &mdash; website, company type, SSM number, social media, GST number. <strong>Company Type</strong> includes <strong>Recruitment Agency</strong>, so set it to match what your company actually is.</li>\n<li>Benefits, address and YouTube link</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to edit</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Go to <strong>Company Branding &rarr; Company Page</strong>.</li>\n<li>Update the fields you need and save.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Preview Profile</strong> to check how the change looks to jobseekers on each site &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">what your Company Page is</a>.</li>\n<li>Check <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard\">your Profile %</a> afterwards to see whether anything is still outstanding.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Two things you cannot change yourself:</strong> your company name, and a job ad title after it is published. <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">Contact AJobThing support</a> for both. That is why the <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">job title is worth getting right</a> before you publish &mdash; see also <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad\">how to edit a live job ad</a> for what can still be changed on an ad that is already running.</div>\n<p>Editing your own name, email or password is separate and lives under Account Details &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-change-my-name-email-contact-number-or-password\">how to change your email or password</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad","what-does-the-blue-verified-company-badge-mean","how-do-i-get-my-company-verified"]},{"u":"what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it","t":"What is my Company Page and where do jobseekers see it?","c":2,"s":"Your Company Page","b":"<p><strong>It is your public profile on Maukerja and Ricebowl.</strong> A jobseeker can open it from your job ad before deciding whether to apply &mdash; so it is doing work on every ad you run.</p>\n<h2>What jobseekers see</h2>\n<p>Seven tabs: <strong>Profile</strong>, <strong>Jobs</strong>, <strong>Walk-In</strong>, <strong>Life</strong>, <strong>Photos</strong>, <strong>Q&amp;A</strong> and <strong>Benefits</strong>. Each one is filled by something you control &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-add-photos-to-my-company-page\">Company Photos</a> fills Photos, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-answer-the-questions-jobseekers-ask\">your FAQ answers</a> fill Q&amp;A, <a href=\"#/a/what-are-employee-stories\">Employee Stories</a> fill Life, and any <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-walk-in-campaign\">Walk-In campaign</a> appears under Walk-In.</p>\n<h2>What to do with it</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Fill the description, benefits and logo first &mdash; the parts of the Profile tab jobseekers read.</li>\n<li>Add photos and FAQ answers so the other tabs are not empty.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Preview Profile for: Maukerja / Ricebowl</strong> to see each version exactly as jobseekers do.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Check it before you leave it.</strong> The two sites show your page separately, so a section that looks complete on one can be empty on the other &mdash; preview both. <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard\">Your Profile %</a> tells you what is still missing.</div>\n<p>An empty page next to a well-filled competitor costs you applications, which is why it appears in <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">why a job ad is not getting applicants</a>. See it as jobseekers do on <a href=\"https://www.maukerja.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Maukerja</a> or <a href=\"https://www.ricebowl.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ricebowl</a>. One caveat: a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-confidential-toggle\">Confidential ad</a> does not link to your page at all.</p>","r":["how-do-i-add-photos-to-my-company-page","what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard"]},{"u":"how-do-i-add-photos-to-my-company-page","t":"How do I add photos to my Company Page?","c":2,"s":"Your Company Page","b":"<p><strong>Add them under Company Photos on your Company Page</strong> &mdash; and note that Maukerja and Ricebowl each keep their own set.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Scroll to <strong>Company Photos</strong> on your <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">Company Page</a>.</li>\n<li>Pick the <strong>MAUKERJA</strong> or <strong>RICEBOWL</strong> tab.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Add Photo</strong>, upload, then choose a category such as Office Environment or Team Building and write a short description.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Copy to Ricebowl</strong> instead of uploading twice.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Limit: 20 photos per platform.</strong></p>\n<h2>Which photos are worth uploading</h2>\n<p>Jobseekers are trying to picture the actual workplace, so real photos of your premises, team and equipment do more than stock imagery or a poster of your logo. A handful of clear, current photos beats twenty dark or repetitive ones &mdash; and if you have moved office, replace the old ones rather than adding to them.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Caption everything.</strong> \"Our KL office\" tells a jobseeker more than a photo with no description. Preview the page on both sites afterwards to confirm each set looks right, and check whether photos were one of the items holding back <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard\">your Profile %</a>.</div>\n<p>Photos sit under the <strong>Photos</strong> tab of your public page; for staff quotes use <a href=\"#/a/what-are-employee-stories\">Employee Stories</a> instead, which fill the <strong>Life</strong> tab.</p>","r":["what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it","how-do-i-get-free-resumes-from-my-company-page","how-do-i-answer-the-questions-jobseekers-ask"]},{"u":"how-do-i-get-free-resumes-from-my-company-page","t":"How do I get free resumes from my Company Page?","c":2,"s":"Your Company Page","b":"<p><strong>Turn on Free Resume on your Company Page.</strong> AJobThing describes it as: \"Receive FREE resume from company page to My Talent Pool.\"</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Keep the <strong>Free Resume</strong> toggle on.</li>\n<li>Check the <strong>Email alert</strong> address below it is one somebody actually reads.</li>\n<li>Change it with <strong>Edit email</strong> if it is wrong.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What happens once it is on</h2>\n<p>Jobseekers can send you their resume straight from <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">your Company Page</a>, without applying to a specific job. Those resumes land in <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-talent-pool\">My Talent Pool</a> rather than in a job's applicant list, so check there as well as in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied\">Manage Applicants</a> &mdash; otherwise they build up unnoticed.</p>\n<p>Each Brand Page has its own toggle and its own alert email, so if you run several brands, switch it on for each one you want resumes for.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>It works best when your page is worth visiting.</strong> People send speculative resumes to companies whose page tells them something &mdash; so a filled-in description, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-add-photos-to-my-company-page\">photos</a> and <a href=\"#/a/what-does-the-blue-verified-company-badge-mean\">the Verified badge</a> feed directly into how many you get.</div>\n<p>These resumes are candidate data like any other, so the handling and retention rules in <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a> apply from the moment they arrive.</p>","v":[{"i":"DEzZG_5UImk","t":"Get free resumes from your Company Page","d":"0:27","w":"The Free Resume toggle on the Company Profile page."}],"r":["what-is-my-talent-pool","what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it","how-do-i-add-photos-to-my-company-page"]},{"u":"how-do-i-answer-the-questions-jobseekers-ask","t":"How do I answer the questions jobseekers ask?","c":2,"s":"Your Company Page","b":"<p><strong>Open Company Branding &rarr; Company FAQ.</strong> These are real questions jobseekers asked about your company, grouped by topic such as Pay &amp; Perks and Working Hours &amp; Flexibility.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Filter by <strong>All</strong>, <strong>Answered</strong> or <strong>Not Answered</strong> to see what is outstanding.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Answer</strong> and publish.</li>\n<li>To change one later, click <strong>Edit Answer</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Your answers appear under the <strong>Q&amp;A</strong> tab of <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">your public page</a>, where jobseekers read them before applying.</p>\n<h2>How to answer well</h2>\n<p>Answer specifically and honestly &mdash; these questions get asked because the job ad did not settle them. \"Working hours are 9am to 6pm, Monday to Friday, with occasional Saturday shifts during peak season\" prevents applications from people who cannot do it, which saves everyone time. A vague answer is worse than none, because it reads as evasive on the very topics candidates care about.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Answering also earns credits.</strong> Each answer earns you <strong>2 credits</strong>, and there are <strong>9 questions</strong> in total &mdash; 18 credits for finishing the set, on top of a better page. See <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-get-more-credits\">how to get more credits</a> and <a href=\"#/a/what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on\">what you can spend them on</a>. Check your <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">credit history</a> to confirm they arrived.</div>\n<p>Answered questions also count towards <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard\">your Profile %</a>, so this is one of the quickest items on that checklist to clear.</p>","r":["how-do-i-get-more-credits","what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it","how-do-i-add-photos-to-my-company-page"]},{"u":"what-are-employee-stories","t":"What are Employee Stories?","c":2,"s":"Your Company Page","b":"<p><strong>Short quotes from your own staff, shown on your Company Page.</strong> They fill the <strong>Life</strong> tab and give jobseekers something no job ad can &mdash; a view of the place from the people already working there.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <strong>Employee Stories</strong>.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Create A Story</strong>, or use <strong>Invite To Share Employee Stories!</strong> to ask staff to write their own.</li>\n<li>Manage them under <strong>Published</strong>, <strong>Draft</strong> and <strong>Deleted</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each story shows its topics and how many <strong>Views</strong> and <strong>Likes</strong> it has, so you can see which ones jobseekers actually read and ask for more of that kind.</p>\n<h2>Getting stories worth publishing</h2>\n<p>Invitations work better than writing on staff's behalf &mdash; a story in someone's own words reads as genuine, and a polished corporate paragraph does not. Ask people from the roles you hire for most, since a warehouse applicant is reassured by a warehouse colleague, not by a manager.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Delete really deletes.</strong> If you only want a story off the page for now &mdash; while you check with the person, or because they have left &mdash; move it to <strong>Draft</strong> instead.</div>\n<p>Stories are one of the items behind <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard\">your Profile %</a>, and they sit alongside <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-add-photos-to-my-company-page\">Company Photos</a> and <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-answer-the-questions-jobseekers-ask\">your FAQ answers</a> as the parts of <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">your Company Page</a> that make it worth reading.</p>","r":["what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it","how-do-i-add-photos-to-my-company-page","how-do-i-get-free-resumes-from-my-company-page"]},{"u":"what-do-i-need-to-prepare-before-posting-a-job-ad","t":"What do I need to prepare before posting a job ad?","c":3,"s":"Before You Post","b":"<p><strong>Gather eight things before you open the form.</strong> The job form marks these fields compulsory, so it will not let you continue until each one is filled &mdash; having them ready turns a stop-start job posting into a five-minute task.</p>\n<h2>Your checklist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Job title</strong> &mdash; the one jobseekers actually search for. It cannot be edited after the ad publishes, so decide it now. See <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">how to write a job title jobseekers will find</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Which brand the job is under</strong>, if your account has more than one.</li>\n<li><strong>Specialization and Employment Type</strong> &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/specialization-and-employment-type-how-do-i-pick\">how to pick these two</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The work address</strong>, or a decision that the role is 100% remote.</li>\n<li><strong>Years of experience, minimum education and required languages.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Salary range</strong>, or a decision that <a href=\"#/a/what-does-this-job-has-no-basic-salary-mean\">the role has no basic salary</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Number of vacancies and position level.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Emails of colleagues</strong> who should also receive the applications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two decisions are worth settling before you start, because both are hard to change later: the exact job title, and whether this ad should come out of your free or paid balance &mdash; check <a href=\"#/a/which-job-ads-are-free\">whether this ad will be free or use a paid job ad</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Already have a job description in a file?</strong> Skip the typing. Use <strong>Import from File / URL</strong> at the top of the form to <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-import-a-job-from-a-file-or-url\">pull the details straight out of your document</a>.</div>","v":[{"i":"08MWng67usg","t":"How to post a job ad in AJobThing","d":"1:58","w":"The full Post a New Job form, from job details to Continue."}],"r":["how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step","how-do-i-import-a-job-from-a-file-or-url"]},{"u":"which-job-ads-are-free","t":"Which job ads are free, and will this one use a paid job ad?","c":3,"s":"Before You Post","b":"<p><strong>Six job types are free &mdash; but the job title must be written a certain way, not just the type selected.</strong> Both halves of the rule have to be met, so a genuine internship posted as \"Marketing Assistant\" still draws from your paid balance.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it needs</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Internship</td><td>Title starts with \"Internship for\" + type Internship</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Part-time</td><td>Title starts with \"Part-time\" + type Part-time</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Freelance</td><td>Title starts with \"Freelance\" + type Freelance</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Volunteer</td><td>Title starts with \"Volunteer\" + type Volunteer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>High-pay job</td><td>Salary RM8,000 or more</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Singapore job</td><td>Location Singapore + salary in SGD</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Anything else &mdash; an ordinary full-time role under RM8,000 in Malaysia, for example &mdash; uses your paid job ad balance.</p>\n<h2>How to post a free ad, and confirm it was free</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>On your Dashboard, look at <strong>FREE JOB ADS</strong> and click <strong>ELIGIBILITY</strong> to see the six types.</li>\n<li>Write the title exactly as the table requires. <em>Internship for Marketing</em> works. <em>Marketing Intern</em> does not.</li>\n<li>Set the matching <a href=\"#/a/specialization-and-employment-type-how-do-i-pick\">Employment Type</a>. For a high-pay or Singapore ad, make sure the <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-salary\">salary</a> is entered &mdash; an ad with <a href=\"#/a/what-does-this-job-has-no-basic-salary-mean\">no basic salary</a> cannot meet those two conditions.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Post Job FREE</strong> on your Dashboard.</li>\n<li>Check the <strong>FREE JOB ADS</strong> number again afterwards. If it dropped, the free balance was used. If it did not, the ad came out of your paid balance.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>The title cannot be edited after publishing</strong>, so a mis-worded title cannot be corrected into a free ad later &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">how to write a job title jobseekers will find</a>.</div>\n<p>Your free balance carries its own expiry date, shown next to it on the Dashboard. Both balances sit side by side, so you can see which one an ad will draw from &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-know-how-many-job-ads-i-have-left\">how many job ads you have left</a>.</p>","r":["what-do-i-need-to-prepare-before-posting-a-job-ad","how-do-i-know-how-many-job-ads-i-have-left"]},{"u":"how-do-i-know-how-many-job-ads-i-have-left","t":"How do I know how many job ads I have left?","c":3,"s":"Before You Post","b":"<p><strong>Two screens show your remaining job ads</strong> &mdash; your Dashboard for a quick look, and Billing for the full detail with expiry dates.</p>\n<h2>Quick check on the Dashboard</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open your Dashboard and find <strong>Your job ad packages</strong>.</li>\n<li>Read the <strong>FREE JOB ADS</strong> and <strong>JOB AD PACKAGES</strong> balances. Each one carries its own expiry date.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Full detail in Billing</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Go to <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\"><strong>Billing &rarr; Packages</strong></a>.</li>\n<li>Every package lists <strong>REMAINING</strong> as a fraction &mdash; for example 42/120 &mdash; plus its <strong>EXPIRY</strong> date and a <strong>STATUS</strong> countdown such as \"62 D Left\".</li>\n<li>Hover <strong>How it works?</strong> on a package card to break the fraction down: your total quota, how many ads are currently active, and how many are still available to post.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The two numbers can differ because active ads are already counted against your quota. If a balance looks lower than you expected, that is usually why &mdash; and if a package is close to expiring, read <a href=\"#/a/when-does-my-package-expire-and-what-happens-to-unused-job-ads\">what happens to unused job ads</a> before the date passes.</p>","r":["where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances","what-do-i-need-to-prepare-before-posting-a-job-ad","which-job-ads-are-free"]},{"u":"how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step","t":"How do I post a job, step by step?","c":3,"s":"Filling In The Form","b":"<p><strong>Posting a job is one form, nine sections, then a preview.</strong> Nothing publishes until you approve the preview, so you can work through it without worrying about going live by accident.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Click <strong>Post Job</strong> in the top bar.</li>\n<li><strong>Job Details</strong> &mdash; job title, company/brand, specialization, employment type, primary role location, years of experience, required languages, minimum education. All compulsory. Your <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">job title</a> and <a href=\"#/a/specialization-and-employment-type-how-do-i-pick\">employment type</a> matter most here.</li>\n<li><strong>Location</strong> &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-job-location\">set the work address</a>, or confirm the role is fully remote.</li>\n<li><strong>Required Skills</strong> &mdash; type them, or use <strong>Suggest Skills with AI</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Salary &amp; Compensation</strong> &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-salary\">set a range</a>, or tick \"This job has no basic salary.\"</li>\n<li><strong>Job Description</strong> &mdash; requirements, responsibilities and benefits.</li>\n<li><strong>Extra Information</strong> &mdash; number of vacancies, position level, accept fresh graduates.</li>\n<li><strong>Notification Settings</strong> &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/who-gets-told-when-someone-applies\">who gets told when someone applies</a>.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Continue</strong>, or <strong>Save as Draft</strong> to finish later.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What happens next</h2>\n<p>You then see a <strong>Job Ad Preview</strong> of exactly what jobseekers will see, on desktop, tablet and mobile. Choose <strong>Accept &amp; Continue</strong> or <strong>Back</strong>. After you accept, the ad goes to Pending for review &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/what-happens-after-i-click-continue\">what happens after you click Continue</a> covers the checks AJobThing may ask you to clear first.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Hiring the same role again?</strong> Don't retype it. <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-copy-a-previous-job-ad\">Copy a previous ad</a> or <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-import-a-job-from-a-file-or-url\">import the job from a file or URL</a>.</div>","v":[{"i":"08MWng67usg","t":"How to post a job ad in AJobThing","d":"1:58","w":"The full Post a New Job form, from job details to Continue."}],"r":["what-happens-after-i-click-continue","how-do-i-import-a-job-from-a-file-or-url"]},{"u":"how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find","t":"How do I write a job title jobseekers will find?","c":3,"s":"Filling In The Form","b":"<p><strong>Use the plain job title people type into a search box, and nothing else.</strong> Jobseekers search by role name, so a title that matches how they search is the single biggest thing you control in whether your ad gets seen.</p>\n<h2>Three rules</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use the real job title.</strong> <em>Account Executive</em>, not <em>Finance Ninja</em>. Nobody searches for a ninja.</li>\n<li><strong>Leave out your company name and the location.</strong> Both already appear on the ad, and adding them to the title only pushes the role name out of view.</li>\n<li><strong>One job per ad.</strong> Not \"Sales / Admin / Driver\" &mdash; a combined title matches none of the three searches properly, and applicants cannot tell which role they are applying for. Post them separately.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">CANNOT BE UNDONE</strong> You cannot edit the title after publishing. Get it right the first time, or <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">contact AJobThing support</a> to change it. If you are unsure of the wording, <a href=\"#/a/can-i-save-a-job-ad-and-finish-later\">save the ad as a draft</a> and decide before you publish.</div>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>For a free ad, the wording is part of the eligibility rule.</strong> The title must start with the exact words &mdash; \"Internship for\", \"Part-time\", \"Freelance\" or \"Volunteer\". See <a href=\"#/a/which-job-ads-are-free\">which job ads are free</a>.</div>\n<p>If the ad is live and getting few applicants, the title is the first thing to review &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">why a job ad is not getting applicants</a> walks through the rest.</p>","r":["which-job-ads-are-free","how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad"]},{"u":"specialization-and-employment-type-how-do-i-pick","t":"Specialization and Employment Type: how do I pick?","c":3,"s":"Filling In The Form","b":"<p><strong>Specialization is the industry the role sits in; Employment Type is the kind of contract.</strong> They do different jobs on your ad, and Employment Type also decides whether the ad is free.</p>\n<h2>Specialization</h2>\n<p>This is the industry category jobseekers browse and filter by. AJobThing's guidance is simple: \"Choose specialization based on the Job Position.\" Pick it from the role itself, not from what your company does &mdash; an accountant at a logistics firm is an accounting role, not a logistics one. Getting this right matters beyond browsing, because it also feeds the matching behind <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended Candidates</a>.</p>\n<h2>Employment Type</h2>\n<p>The options are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Full-time</li>\n<li>Part-time</li>\n<li>Internship</li>\n<li>Freelance</li>\n<li>Contract</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose the one that matches the actual contract you are offering. If you are not sure between Contract and Full-time, go by how the role is engaged rather than how long you expect it to last.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Employment Type decides whether the ad is free.</strong> Internship, Part-time and Freelance ads only come out of your free balance when the type matches <em>and</em> <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">the title is written the required way</a>. Check <a href=\"#/a/which-job-ads-are-free\">which job ads are free</a> before you post, and <a href=\"#/a/which-job-ads-are-free\">confirm which balance the ad will use</a>.</div>","r":["which-job-ads-are-free","how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step","how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find"]},{"u":"how-do-i-set-the-job-location","t":"How do I set the job location?","c":3,"s":"Filling In The Form","b":"<p><strong>There are two location fields and they do different jobs</strong> &mdash; one describes how the role works, the other is the address shown on your ad.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary Role Location</strong>, in Job Details &mdash; the working arrangement: on-site, remote or hybrid.</li>\n<li><strong>Location</strong> &mdash; the actual work address that appears on the ad and that jobseekers filter by.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Setting the address</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Pick from your recent locations if you have posted from that address before.</li>\n<li>Or use <strong>Select From Map</strong>, or search for the address.</li>\n<li>If the search cannot find it &mdash; common for new buildings, industrial lots and small towns &mdash; use <strong>Can't find the location? Enter manually.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Set the address as precisely as you can. Jobseekers filter and sort by location, so an ad pinned to the wrong district reaches the wrong people. If your ad is live but attracting very few applicants, an inaccurate or overly broad location is one of the causes covered in <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">why a job ad is not getting applicants</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>For a fully remote role,</strong> AJobThing asks you to confirm: \"Please confirm that your role is a 100% Fully Remote / Work from Home position.\" Only confirm if it truly is &mdash; a hybrid role marked fully remote brings in applicants who cannot come to your office.</div>\n<p>Location is one field you <em>can</em> correct on a live ad, unlike the title &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad\">how to edit a live job ad</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step","how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find","specialization-and-employment-type-how-do-i-pick"]},{"u":"how-do-i-add-required-skills","t":"How do I add required skills?","c":3,"s":"Filling In The Form","b":"<p><strong>Add skills either from the AI suggestions or by typing your own.</strong> Both end up in the same <strong>Selected Skills</strong> list on your ad.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Click <strong>Suggest Skills with AI</strong> &mdash; AJobThing lists skills for your job title.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>+</strong> on the ones that genuinely apply to the role.</li>\n<li>Or type your own into <strong>Selected Skills</strong> and press Enter.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Why the list matters</h2>\n<p>Skills appear on your ad, and they feed the matching AJobThing uses to recommend candidates for the role. That cuts both ways: a short, accurate list attracts people who can do the job, while a padded list of every tool you can think of pulls in mismatched applicants. If <a href=\"#/a/why-are-my-recommended-candidates-a-poor-match\">your recommended candidates are a poor match</a>, the skills list is one of the first places to look.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">The counter next to the button &mdash; for example <strong>3/3 left today</strong> &mdash; is how many AI uses you have left today. <a href=\"#/a/what-does-3-3-left-today-mean\">What \"3/3 left today\" means</a> explains what to do when it runs out, and you can always type skills in yourself.</div>","v":[{"i":"tJay7sqRKEA","t":"Let AI suggest the right skills","d":"0:30","w":"Using Suggest Skills with AI on the Required Skills field."}],"r":["what-does-3-3-left-today-mean","how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step","how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find"]},{"u":"how-do-i-set-the-salary","t":"How do I set the salary?","c":3,"s":"Filling In The Form","b":"<p><strong>Set a currency, a Minimum and a Maximum, and a period such as Monthly.</strong> You can then add <strong>Commission Pay</strong>, <strong>Overtime Pay</strong> or <strong>Bonus</strong> on top if the role includes them.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Choose the currency and the period (for example Monthly).</li>\n<li>Enter the <strong>Minimum</strong> and <strong>Maximum</strong> you are genuinely willing to pay for the role.</li>\n<li>Tick any extras that apply &mdash; commission, overtime or bonus.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Not sure what to put?</h2>\n<p>Click <strong>Suggest Salary with AI</strong>. AJobThing shows a recommended range, a suggested bonus, and an <strong>Industry Insight</strong> comparison against other companies, so you can see whether your range is competitive before the ad goes live. Treat it as a reference point and adjust to your own budget.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>A real salary range gets more applicants.</strong> It is also one of the two things LinkedIn needs before your ad can show there &mdash; the other is a public company name, which the <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-confidential-toggle\">Confidential toggle</a> removes. See <a href=\"#/a/where-do-my-job-ads-appear\">where your job ads appear</a>.</div>\n<p>If the role truly has no fixed monthly wage, do not enter a placeholder figure &mdash; tick the no-basic-salary option instead and read <a href=\"#/a/what-does-this-job-has-no-basic-salary-mean\">what \"This job has no basic salary\" means</a> first, because it changes how your ad performs.</p>","v":[{"i":"m66y72L-wNQ","t":"Check salary benchmarks with AI","d":"0:21","w":"AI Recommended Compensation and the industry comparison."}],"r":["what-does-this-job-has-no-basic-salary-mean","where-do-my-job-ads-appear"]},{"u":"what-does-this-job-has-no-basic-salary-mean","t":"What does \"This job has no basic salary\" mean?","c":3,"s":"Filling In The Form","b":"<p><strong>It tells jobseekers the role pays no fixed monthly wage</strong> &mdash; for example a pure commission role. Tick it only when that is genuinely true, and no salary range will show on the ad.</p>\n<h2>What to expect after you tick it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fewer applicants.</strong> Ads without a salary usually attract less interest, because many jobseekers filter by pay or skip ads that do not show it.</li>\n<li><strong>No LinkedIn distribution.</strong> The ad cannot appear on LinkedIn, which requires basic salary information. See <a href=\"#/a/where-do-my-job-ads-appear\">where your job ads appear</a> for the channels that are still covered.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to do instead, where you can</h2>\n<p>If the role has any fixed component &mdash; a retainer, a base plus commission, an allowance treated as wage &mdash; it is not a no-basic-salary role. Enter the real range and add commission on top, as described in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-salary\">how to set the salary</a>. If it truly is commission-only, use the job description to spell out realistic earnings and how commission is calculated, so applicants can judge the role for themselves.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Expect a slower response than your other ads.</strong> If applications are thin, <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">why a job ad is not getting applicants</a> covers what else to check.</div>","v":[{"i":"m66y72L-wNQ","t":"Check salary benchmarks with AI","d":"0:21","w":"AI Recommended Compensation and the industry comparison."}],"r":["how-do-i-set-the-salary","where-do-my-job-ads-appear"]},{"u":"how-do-i-write-the-job-description","t":"How do I write the job description?","c":3,"s":"Filling In The Form","b":"<p><strong>The job description has three parts &mdash; Job Requirements, Job Responsibilities and Benefits</strong> &mdash; and each has tabs for <strong>English</strong>, <strong>Bahasa Malaysia</strong> and <strong>Mandarin</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Fastest way</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Click <strong>Generate with AI</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tick <strong>Use this</strong> on each part you want, edit anything you disagree with, then click <strong>Confirm</strong>. Nothing is saved until you confirm, so read it properly first.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Translate</strong> to carry your text into another language.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Filling in more than one language widens your reach, since jobseekers browse in the language they are comfortable with. If Mandarin is not actually required for the role, leave that requirement out &mdash; AJobThing will ask you to confirm it before the ad is accepted.</p>\n<h2>Make it worth reading</h2>\n<p>Keep each part to short bullets covering what the person will do, what you need from them, and what they get. Specific beats generic: \"handle daily bank reconciliation for two entities\" tells a candidate far more than \"handle accounting duties\", and it is what makes an ad stand out against a page of similar roles.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Use bullet points.</strong> If a section is one long paragraph, AJobThing will stop you and ask you to add bullets or numbers before you can continue.</div>\n<div class=\"tip\">Tick <strong>Save this Job Description as a Template</strong> to reuse it next time, and see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-ai-to-fill-in-my-job-ad\">how to use AI to fill in your job ad</a> to draft skills, salary and description together in one step.</div>","v":[{"i":"fNUYN4skGAw","t":"Write job descriptions in 3 languages, 1 click","d":"0:24","w":"The AI job description window with English, Bahasa Malaysia and Mandarin."}],"r":["how-do-i-use-ai-to-fill-in-my-job-ad","how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step","how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find"]},{"u":"what-is-the-confidential-toggle","t":"What is the Confidential toggle?","c":3,"s":"Filling In The Form","b":"<p><strong>Turn it on and your company name is hidden on the job ad.</strong> The role still appears and jobseekers can still apply &mdash; they just cannot see who is hiring.</p>\n<h2>When it is the right choice</h2>\n<p>Use it when naming your company would cause a problem: you are replacing someone who is still in the role, or the hire signals something you are not ready to announce. Outside those situations, a named ad performs better.</p>\n<h2>What you give up</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Your logo and Company Page disappear from the ad</strong> &mdash; so everything you built on <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">your Company Page</a> and the <a href=\"#/a/what-does-the-blue-verified-company-badge-mean\">Verified Company badge</a> stops working for you here. That usually means fewer applicants.</li>\n<li><strong>The ad cannot appear on LinkedIn</strong>, which requires a public company name. See <a href=\"#/a/where-do-my-job-ads-appear\">where your job ads appear</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Weigh it against the drop in applications.</strong> A confidential ad asks jobseekers to apply to a company they cannot check, so expect a lower response and plan to reach out to candidates yourself &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">searching for candidates</a> is a practical complement to a confidential ad.</div>","r":["where-do-my-job-ads-appear","how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step","how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find"]},{"u":"what-are-position-level-and-accept-fresh-graduates","t":"What are Position Level and Accept Fresh Graduates?","c":3,"s":"Filling In The Form","b":"<p><strong>Both sit in Extra Information and help jobseekers judge whether the role suits them</strong> before they apply &mdash; which is what keeps your applicant list relevant.</p>\n<h2>Position Level</h2>\n<p>AJobThing defines the levels like this:</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Position Level</th><th>AJobThing's definition</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Entry Level</td><td>No prior experience required / fresh graduates</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Junior Level</td><td>1-3 years of experience, some supervision needed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mid Level</td><td>3-5 years. Minimal supervision needed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Senior Level</td><td>5+ years of exp. Top skills &amp; guides others</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lead / Manager</td><td>Manages a team and/or projects</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Head / Director</td><td>Manages multiple teams or a department</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Accept Fresh Graduates</h2>\n<p>A simple on/off switch. Turn it on if you are genuinely open to someone with no work history, and off if the role needs prior experience &mdash; leaving it on for a role that does not suit a fresh graduate creates applications you will only have to reject.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Keep Position Level and Years of Experience consistent.</strong> A \"Senior Level\" role asking for 1-2 years confuses jobseekers and makes the ad look careless. If the two fields disagree, fix the one that does not match the role you are actually hiring for.</div>\n<p>These fields also inform who AJobThing puts in front of you &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended Candidates</a>. You can adjust them on a live ad; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad\">how to edit a live job ad</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step","how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find","specialization-and-employment-type-how-do-i-pick"]},{"u":"how-do-i-use-ai-to-fill-in-my-job-ad","t":"How do I use AI to fill in my job ad?","c":3,"s":"AI & Faster Ways","b":"<p><strong>Auto Generate All writes your skills, salary and job description in one click</strong> &mdash; then hands them to you as a draft to review before anything is saved.</p>\n<h2>Before it works</h2>\n<p>Fill in the job details and the <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-job-location\">location</a> first. Until you do, the button is greyed out and the form says: \"Please fill up the job details and location first.\" The AI works from your title, specialization and location, so the more accurate those are, the more usable the draft.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Complete Job Details and Location.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Auto Generate All</strong>.</li>\n<li>Read each generated part, tick <strong>Use this</strong> on the ones you want, and edit anything you disagree with.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Confirm</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>It overwrites what you already typed.</strong> AJobThing warns: \"This will replace any skills, salary, or description you've already entered with a fresh AI-generated draft.\" Choose <strong>No, Keep Mine</strong> to keep your own words.</div>\n<p>Treat the output as a first draft, not a finished ad. The <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-salary\">salary range</a> in particular needs to match your actual budget, and the description reads better once you add the specifics only you know about the role.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">If the button is unavailable because you have used your daily allowance, see <a href=\"#/a/what-does-3-3-left-today-mean\">what \"3/3 left today\" means</a> &mdash; or <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-the-job-description\">write the description yourself</a>, which is never limited. If the button stays greyed out even with the details filled in, check <a href=\"#/a/the-ai-button-is-greyed-out-or-says-i-ve-hit-my-limit\">the AI button troubleshooting article</a>.</div>","v":[{"i":"fNUYN4skGAw","t":"Write job descriptions in 3 languages, 1 click","d":"0:24","w":"The AI job description window with English, Bahasa Malaysia and Mandarin."},{"i":"tJay7sqRKEA","t":"Let AI suggest the right skills","d":"0:30","w":"Using Suggest Skills with AI on the Required Skills field."}],"r":["what-does-3-3-left-today-mean","how-do-i-write-the-job-description"]},{"u":"what-does-3-3-left-today-mean","t":"What does \"3/3 left today\" mean?","c":3,"s":"AI & Faster Ways","b":"<p><strong>It is how many AI uses you have left today for that button.</strong> Each AI helper keeps its own daily count, so using up your description generations does not stop you from suggesting skills or salary.</p>\n<h2>What you see when it runs out</h2>\n<p>AJobThing shows: \"You've used up your AI generation limit for today. No worries, you can still post this job right now by filling in the details yourself.\"</p>\n<h2>What to do next</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Click <strong>Fill in Manually</strong> and type the fields yourself &mdash; nothing about posting the job is blocked. <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-the-job-description\">Writing the job description</a> by hand has no limit.</li>\n<li>Or <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-copy-a-previous-job-ad\">copy a previous job ad</a> and edit it, which needs no AI at all.</li>\n<li>Or come back tomorrow, when the count resets.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Spend the last use where it saves you the most typing.</strong> If you have one generation left, the job description is usually worth more than a skills list you could type in seconds.</div>\n<p>If a button is greyed out and you have not used your allowance, that is a different problem &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/the-ai-button-is-greyed-out-or-says-i-ve-hit-my-limit\">the AI button is greyed out or says I've hit my limit</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-use-ai-to-fill-in-my-job-ad","how-do-i-copy-a-previous-job-ad","how-do-i-import-a-job-from-a-file-or-url"]},{"u":"how-do-i-copy-a-previous-job-ad","t":"How do I copy a previous job ad?","c":3,"s":"AI & Faster Ways","b":"<p><strong>Copy Previous Ad fills a new job form with the details of an ad you have already posted</strong> &mdash; the fastest way to re-open a role you hire for regularly.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>At the top of <strong>Post a New Job</strong>, click <strong>Copy Previous Ad</strong>.</li>\n<li>Search by job title or company.</li>\n<li>Pick the ad. The form fills with its details.</li>\n<li>Change what is different, then continue.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What to check before you post the copy</h2>\n<p>A copied ad brings the old details with it, and some of them go stale:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Salary</strong> &mdash; last year's range may no longer be competitive. See <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-salary\">how to set the salary</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Location</strong> &mdash; especially if you have moved branch or the role is now hybrid.</li>\n<li><strong>Number of vacancies and position level</strong> in Extra Information.</li>\n<li><strong>The title</strong>, which cannot be changed once the new ad publishes &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">how to write a job title jobseekers will find</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>A copy is a new ad, not a renewal.</strong> It draws from your job ad balance like any other post, so <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-know-how-many-job-ads-i-have-left\">check what you have left</a> first. If you only want to keep an existing ad running, <a href=\"#/a/can-i-pause-extend-or-renew-a-job-ad\">pausing, extending or renewing</a> is the cheaper route.</div>\n<p>Starting from a document instead of an old ad? Use <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-import-a-job-from-a-file-or-url\">import from a file or URL</a>.</p>","v":[{"i":"Fq7Z_O5k5vU","t":"Post jobs with just one image or PDF","d":"0:21","w":"Uploading a job description file and letting AI fill the form."}],"r":["how-do-i-import-a-job-from-a-file-or-url","how-do-i-use-ai-to-fill-in-my-job-ad","what-does-3-3-left-today-mean"]},{"u":"how-do-i-import-a-job-from-a-file-or-url","t":"How do I import a job from a file or URL?","c":3,"s":"AI & Faster Ways","b":"<p><strong>Import from File / URL reads an existing job description and fills the form for you</strong> &mdash; useful when the role is already written up in a document or posted somewhere else.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Click <strong>Import from File / URL</strong> at the top of the job form.</li>\n<li>Upload your job description, or paste a link to an existing job listing and click <strong>Extract</strong>.</li>\n<li>Wait for it to finish, then click <strong>Review My Job Ad Now</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Accepted files:</strong> PDF, Docx, Xls, Xlsx, Csv, or an image (Jpg, Jpeg, Png). Maximum 2MB. If your file is larger, save a lighter copy or paste the text into a fresh document before uploading.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Always check the result.</strong> AJobThing asks you to \"ensure the information auto-filled from your document is completely accurate before moving forward.\" Read the salary, location and title before you continue &mdash; extraction from a scanned image or a PDF with an unusual layout is where mistakes show up, and the <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">title cannot be edited after publishing</a>.</div>\n<p>There is a daily limit on imports, and the window tells you how many uses you have left. If you run out, you can still <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step\">fill the form in yourself</a> or <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-copy-a-previous-job-ad\">copy a previous ad</a> &mdash; neither is affected.</p>","v":[{"i":"Fq7Z_O5k5vU","t":"Post jobs with just one image or PDF","d":"0:21","w":"Uploading a job description file and letting AI fill the form."}],"r":["how-do-i-copy-a-previous-job-ad","how-do-i-use-ai-to-fill-in-my-job-ad","what-does-3-3-left-today-mean"]},{"u":"who-gets-told-when-someone-applies","t":"Who gets told when someone applies?","c":3,"s":"Alerts & Submitting","b":"<p><strong>Notification Settings, near the bottom of the job form, controls who hears about applications for this ad.</strong> Set it before you submit &mdash; it decides whether a good applicant is seen the same day or a week later.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Turn on <strong>Email for Notifications</strong> and check the address is one someone actually monitors.</li>\n<li>Turn on <strong>WhatsApp Alert</strong> for a message on your phone.</li>\n<li>Under <strong>Additional Recipients</strong>, add colleagues who should also get the email &mdash; up to five, each with a name, job title, email and contact number.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Colleagues you added before appear as one-click chips, so you do not have to retype their details on every ad.</p>\n<h2>What to expect</h2>\n<p>Alerts are a prompt, not the record. Every application still lands in your applicant list whether or not the email arrives &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied\">where you see everyone who applied</a>. If the emails are not reaching you, work through <a href=\"#/a/i-m-not-receiving-application-emails\">I'm not receiving application emails</a> rather than assuming nobody has applied.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">Account-wide defaults live in <strong>Settings</strong>, where you can also choose how often the emails come &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get\">how to choose which alerts you get</a>. Adding a shared HR mailbox as an additional recipient means applications are still seen when the main owner is on leave.</div>","r":["how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get","can-i-save-a-job-ad-and-finish-later","what-happens-after-i-click-continue"]},{"u":"can-i-save-a-job-ad-and-finish-later","t":"Can I save a job ad and finish later?","c":3,"s":"Alerts & Submitting","b":"<p><strong>Yes &mdash; click Save as Draft at any point in the job form.</strong> Nothing is published and nothing is charged.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The ad goes to the <strong>Draft</strong> tab in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page\"><strong>Job Ads &amp; Application</strong></a>.</li>\n<li>You get a notification: \"Draft for job &lt;title&gt; has been saved.\"</li>\n<li>Your job ad balance is untouched until you actually publish.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to pick it up again</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <strong>Job Ads &amp; Application</strong> and switch to the <strong>Draft</strong> tab &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-five-tabs-mean\">what the five tabs mean</a>.</li>\n<li>Open the draft and continue from where you stopped.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Continue</strong> when you are ready to publish.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Draft anything you are still deciding.</strong> The <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">job title cannot be edited after the ad goes live</a>, so if the wording, salary or location is still under discussion, a draft is safer than publishing and hoping to fix it later.</div>\n<p>A draft is not queued for review and jobseekers cannot see it. Nothing happens to it until you come back and publish, at which point it goes to Pending like any new ad &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-happens-after-i-click-continue\">what happens after you click Continue</a>.</p>","r":["what-happens-after-i-click-continue","who-gets-told-when-someone-applies"]},{"u":"what-happens-after-i-click-continue","t":"What happens after I click Continue?","c":3,"s":"Alerts & Submitting","b":"<p><strong>You do not go live straight away.</strong> AJobThing shows you a <strong>Job Ad Preview</strong> first &mdash; the job card and the full job details, exactly as jobseekers will see them, in desktop, tablet and mobile view and in each language you wrote.</p>\n<p>From there choose <strong>Accept &amp; Continue</strong> or <strong>Back</strong>. Use the preview properly: this is your last chance to fix the <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">job title</a>, which cannot be edited after publishing.</p>\n<h2>Checks AJobThing may ask you to clear first</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>To add bullet points if a section is one long paragraph &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-the-job-description\">how to write the job description</a>.</li>\n<li>To confirm whether Mandarin is really required.</li>\n<li>To add a public company name and <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-salary\">salary</a> so the ad can appear on LinkedIn.</li>\n<li>To confirm you want a second ad if one with the same title and location is already active &mdash; posting it anyway can lead to a <a href=\"#/a/job-ad-is-duplicate-what-does-this-mean\">\"Job Ad Is Duplicate\"</a> outcome.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What happens next</h2>\n<p>After you accept, the ad goes to <strong>Pending</strong> for review, and your job ad balance is used at that point. Jobseekers cannot see it yet. <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live\">Why your job ad is Pending, and when it will go live</a> covers what review involves; once it turns Active but does not show on the jobseeker sites, see <a href=\"#/a/my-ad-is-active-but-has-not-appeared-on-maukerja-or-ricebowl\">my ad is Active but has not appeared</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">Not ready to commit? <strong>Back</strong> returns you to the form, and <a href=\"#/a/can-i-save-a-job-ad-and-finish-later\">Save as Draft</a> keeps everything without publishing or charging.</div>","v":[{"i":"08MWng67usg","t":"How to post a job ad in AJobThing","d":"1:58","w":"The full Post a New Job form, from job details to Continue."}],"r":["why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live","who-gets-told-when-someone-applies","can-i-save-a-job-ad-and-finish-later"]},{"u":"why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live","t":"Why is my job ad Pending, and when will it go live?","c":4,"s":"Approval & Review","b":"<p><strong>Pending means AJobThing is reviewing your ad before it goes live.</strong> It is a normal step, not a problem with your account &mdash; every new ad passes through it.</p>\n<p>AJobThing's message: \"We will review your job ad and edit if required, and publish it within 24 hours.\"</p>\n<h2>See the exact reason</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open the <strong>Pending</strong> tab in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page\"><strong>Job Ads &amp; Application</strong></a>.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>View Pending Reason</strong> on the job card.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The window lists anything you need to fix. If it says \"No pending reasons found\", the ad is simply waiting in the queue and there is nothing for you to do. If it does list something, that is usually a <a href=\"#/a/what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad\">job ad rule</a> to clear or a <a href=\"#/a/job-ad-is-duplicate-what-does-this-mean\">duplicate</a> to resolve.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Urgent?</strong> The same window gives you a contact number to call. You can also message <a href=\"https://wa.me/60189666610\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhatsApp +6018-966 6610</a> or your Account Manager with the job title and the date you posted.</div>\n<p>While it is Pending you cannot edit the ad from the card &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-i-edit-a-job-ad-while-it-is-pending\">can I edit a job ad while it is Pending</a>. When it is approved you get a notification: \"We have published your job ad - &lt;title&gt;\", and the ad moves to <strong>Active</strong>. If it is Active but still not visible to jobseekers, see <a href=\"#/a/my-ad-is-active-but-has-not-appeared-on-maukerja-or-ricebowl\">my ad is Active but has not appeared</a>.</p>","r":["job-ad-is-duplicate-what-does-this-mean","what-are-the-coloured-tags-on-my-job-cards"]},{"u":"job-ad-is-duplicate-what-does-this-mean","t":"\"Job Ad Is Duplicate\" — what does this mean?","c":4,"s":"Approval & Review","b":"<p><strong>You are posting an ad with the same title and same location as one that is already active.</strong> AJobThing flags it before you continue so you do not end up with two competing ads for one role.</p>\n<blockquote>It looks like you have an active job ad with the exact same title and location. To prevent duplicates, this new job will be saved as Pending if you continue.</blockquote>\n<h2>Your choices</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Edit Job Details</strong> &mdash; change the title or location if it really is a different role, such as a different outlet or branch. Make the difference visible in the title, since that is what jobseekers read.</li>\n<li><strong>Ignore &amp; Continue</strong> &mdash; post anyway, and expect it to sit in <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live\">Pending</a> for review rather than going live immediately.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Usually there is a better fix</h2>\n<p>If you need more people for the same role in the same place, a second ad does not help &mdash; it splits your applicants across two lists and uses a second job ad from your balance. Raise <strong>Number of Vacancies</strong> on the existing ad instead; you can do that on a live ad, as covered in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad\">how to edit a live job ad</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Not getting enough applicants from the existing ad?</strong> Reposting the same role rarely fixes that. <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">Why a job ad is not getting applicants</a> covers the causes that actually matter, and <a href=\"#/a/what-is-boost-ads\">Boost Ads</a> is the built-in way to give one ad more visibility.</div>","r":["why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live","does-ajobthing-edit-my-job-ad-before-publishing","can-i-edit-a-job-ad-while-it-is-pending"]},{"u":"does-ajobthing-edit-my-job-ad-before-publishing","t":"Does AJobThing edit my job ad before publishing?","c":4,"s":"Approval & Review","b":"<p><strong>It can.</strong> The review message says AJobThing will \"review your job ad and edit if required, and publish it within 24 hours.\"</p>\n<h2>What kind of edits, and why</h2>\n<p>Edits are made so the ad meets AJobThing's job ad rules &mdash; for example removing wording that shows a preference for a gender, age group or race, which is not allowed. The full list is in <a href=\"#/a/what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad\">what content is not allowed in a job ad</a>, and <a href=\"#/a/can-i-state-age-gender-race-or-nationality-preferences\">can I state age, gender, race or nationality preferences</a> covers that specific point.</p>\n<h2>What to do about it</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Once the ad turns Active, open it from the <strong>Active</strong> tab and read it as a jobseeker would.</li>\n<li>Check the description, requirements and salary still say what you intended.</li>\n<li>If something needs changing, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad\">edit the live ad</a> &mdash; though note the job title cannot be edited after publishing.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Write it compliantly the first time and there is nothing to edit.</strong> Describe the skills and experience the job needs rather than the kind of person you imagine doing it &mdash; that keeps the ad within the rules and widens your applicant pool at the same time.</div>\n<p>If the ad is held rather than published, the <strong>View Pending Reason</strong> window tells you why &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live\">why your job ad is Pending</a>.</p>","r":["what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad","why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live","job-ad-is-duplicate-what-does-this-mean"]},{"u":"can-i-edit-a-job-ad-while-it-is-pending","t":"Can I edit a job ad while it is Pending?","c":4,"s":"Approval & Review","b":"<p><strong>Not from the job card.</strong> A Pending ad shows only one button &mdash; <strong>View Pending Reason</strong>. The usual card actions appear once the ad is Active.</p>\n<h2>What to do if it must change before it goes live</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open the <strong>Pending</strong> tab in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page\"><strong>Job Ads &amp; Application</strong></a> and click <strong>View Pending Reason</strong> &mdash; if the change you want is something the review has already flagged, it will be listed there.</li>\n<li>Note the contact number in that window. It is the fastest route if the ad is urgent.</li>\n<li>Message your Account Manager or <a href=\"https://wa.me/60189666610\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhatsApp +6018-966 6610</a> with the job title, the date you posted, and exactly what needs to change.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Give the correct wording in the message rather than asking for a call back &mdash; it saves a round trip while the ad is still in the queue.</p>\n<h2>Once the ad is Active</h2>\n<p>You can edit it from the job card yourself. Most fields are editable, but the job title is not &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad\">how to edit a live job ad</a> and <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">how to write a job title jobseekers will find</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Still deciding on details?</strong> Next time, <a href=\"#/a/can-i-save-a-job-ad-and-finish-later\">save the ad as a draft</a> instead of submitting it. A draft can be edited freely and uses none of your job ad balance until you publish.</div>","r":["how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad","why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live"]},{"u":"what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad","t":"What content is not allowed in a job ad?","c":4,"s":"Job Ad Rules","b":"<p><strong>Every ad must be one real job, described without preferences for who the person is.</strong> Ads that break these rules are removed, so it is worth checking your wording before you post.</p>\n<h2>Every ad must</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Advertise one job position per posting &mdash; not \"Sales / Admin / Driver\"</li>\n<li>Be a real job &mdash; not a service, a training course or a business opportunity</li>\n<li>Be in English (other languages optional)</li>\n<li>Not prefer a particular gender, age group or race &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-i-state-age-gender-race-or-nationality-preferences\">can I state age, gender, race or nationality preferences</a></li>\n<li>Not contain profanity or obscenity</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Never allowed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Scams or misleading job opportunities</li>\n<li>Human trafficking or exploitation</li>\n<li>Overseas jobs outside Malaysia and Singapore</li>\n<li>Online trading, cryptocurrency, forex, online gambling or any illegal business</li>\n<li>Asking jobseekers to pay anything to get the job &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-i-ask-candidates-to-pay-a-deposit-or-training-fee\">can I ask candidates to pay a deposit or training fee</a></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>The penalty is real.</strong> Non-compliant ads are \"removed immediately, without refund\", and AJobThing can suspend or terminate the account without prior notice.</div>\n<h2>How to stay inside the rules</h2>\n<p>Describe the job, not the person: state the skills, experience, languages and physical requirements the work genuinely needs, and leave out anything about who you picture doing it. <a href=\"#/a/does-ajobthing-edit-my-job-ad-before-publishing\">AJobThing may edit an ad</a> to bring it in line during review, and <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-the-job-description\">how to write the job description</a> shows the structure that keeps ads clear and compliant. If you are unsure whether specific wording is acceptable, ask before posting &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">contact AJobThing</a>.</p>","r":["can-i-state-age-gender-race-or-nationality-preferences","can-i-ask-candidates-to-pay-a-deposit-or-training-fee"]},{"u":"can-i-state-age-gender-race-or-nationality-preferences","t":"Can I state age, gender, race or nationality preferences?","c":4,"s":"Job Ad Rules","b":"<p><strong>No.</strong> AJobThing's rules say a job ad must \"not demonstrate preference towards a particular gender, age group, or race.\" Ads that break this are removed immediately, without refund.</p>\n<h2>What to write instead</h2>\n<p>State what the job actually requires, and let that do the filtering:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Instead of an age range</strong> &mdash; the years of experience the role needs, in the <a href=\"#/a/what-are-position-level-and-accept-fresh-graduates\">Position Level and experience fields</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Instead of a gender</strong> &mdash; the physical or practical requirements of the work, such as shift patterns or lifting, described plainly in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-the-job-description\">the job description</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Instead of a race or nationality</strong> &mdash; the specific language or certification the job requires.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Language requirements</h2>\n<p>If AJobThing detects a language requirement it asks you to confirm &mdash; for example: \"We have detected that your job ad may require applicants who know Mandarin language.\" Answer honestly.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Only say a language is required if the job genuinely cannot be done without it.</strong> A language requirement that is really about ethnicity is exactly the kind of ad that gets removed. If most of the work is in English and Mandarin is only occasionally useful, say it is an advantage rather than a requirement.</div>\n<p>The full list of ad rules is in <a href=\"#/a/what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad\">what content is not allowed in a job ad</a>, and AJobThing <a href=\"#/a/does-ajobthing-edit-my-job-ad-before-publishing\">may edit an ad</a> during review to bring it in line. The same restrictions apply in conversation with candidates &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-am-i-not-allowed-to-send-in-chat\">what you are not allowed to send in chat</a>. For how these rules interact with your own obligations as an employer, take your own legal advice.</p>","r":["what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad","can-i-ask-candidates-to-pay-a-deposit-or-training-fee"]},{"u":"can-i-ask-candidates-to-pay-a-deposit-or-training-fee","t":"Can I ask candidates to pay a deposit or training fee?","c":4,"s":"Job Ad Rules","b":"<p><strong>No. Never.</strong> AJobThing bans \"requesting payments from job seekers as a condition for employment.\" This applies everywhere &mdash; in the job ad, in chat, and at interview.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>The penalty:</strong> AJobThing can suspend or permanently terminate your services and account access without prior notice, and no refund is given.</div>\n<h2>What this covers</h2>\n<p>Any payment asked of a candidate as a condition of getting or keeping the job &mdash; a deposit, a training fee, a uniform charge, a processing or placement fee, or a payment for equipment before they start. It makes no difference whether you intend to refund it later, or describe it as refundable.</p>\n<h2>What to do instead</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Bear genuine onboarding costs as the employer</strong>, or arrange them through payroll after the person starts, in line with your own legal obligations.</li>\n<li><strong>Say so in the ad</strong> if uniforms or tools are provided &mdash; it reassures candidates who have encountered scams.</li>\n<li><strong>Brief anyone interviewing on your behalf</strong>, including anyone interviewing on your behalf, that this rule applies to them too.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The same applies to requests for bank details or identity documents during recruitment &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-am-i-not-allowed-to-send-in-chat\">what you are not allowed to send in chat</a>. The full ad rules are in <a href=\"#/a/what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad\">what content is not allowed in a job ad</a>. If you have seen this happening under your company's name, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-report-a-problem\">report it</a> straight away.</p>","r":["what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad","what-am-i-not-allowed-to-send-in-chat"]},{"u":"my-ad-is-active-but-has-not-appeared-on-maukerja-or-ricebowl","t":"My ad is Active but has not appeared on Maukerja or Ricebowl","c":4,"s":"If Your Ad Is Not Showing","b":"<p><strong>Almost always it is timing or a search mismatch, not a missing ad.</strong> Work through these in order before contacting support.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Confirm the ad really is Active.</strong> Check the <strong>Active</strong> tab in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page\">Job Ads &amp; Application</a>, not <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live\">Pending</a> &mdash; a Pending ad is not visible to jobseekers at all.</li>\n<li><strong>Give the jobseeker sites a little time after approval.</strong> Distribution happens after the ad turns Active, not the moment you submitted it.</li>\n<li><strong>Search for the exact job title</strong> on <a href=\"https://www.maukerja.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Maukerja</a> or <a href=\"https://www.ricebowl.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ricebowl</a>, not a shortened version. Also check the location filter matches the address on the ad &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-job-location\">how to set the job location</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Still missing?</strong> <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">Contact AJobThing</a> with the job title and the date you posted.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>If it is LinkedIn that is missing</h2>\n<p>LinkedIn needs a public company name (not \"Confidential\") and a basic salary. If your ad has either missing, it will not show there &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-confidential-toggle\">the Confidential toggle</a> and <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-salary\">how to set the salary</a>. <a href=\"#/a/where-do-my-job-ads-appear\">Where your job ads appear</a> lists every destination.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">AJobThing's Terms note it \"is not responsible for advertisements not published on partner platforms\" &mdash; the outside sites decide what they accept. Maukerja and Ricebowl are the two channels that carry every published ad, so judge your reach there first, and if applications are slow read <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">why a job ad is not getting applicants</a>.</div>","r":["where-do-my-job-ads-appear","how-do-i-contact-ajobthing"]},{"u":"how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page","t":"How do I use the Job Ads & Application page?","c":5,"s":"Job Manager","b":"<p>The <strong>Job Ads &amp; Application</strong> page is your central list of every job ad you have posted, with the numbers and actions for each one in a single view. Open it from <strong>Manage Job &rarr; Job Ads &amp; Application</strong>.</p> <h2>What the top counters show</h2> <p>Five counters run across the top: <strong>Active Job ads</strong>, <strong>Total Views</strong>, <strong>Total Applicants</strong>, <strong>Total Interview</strong> and <strong>Expiring soon</strong>. These are totals across all your ads, so they move as ads go live, get views, and applicants come in — a quick way to see whether anything needs attention before you open individual cards.</p> <h2>What is on each job card</h2> <p>Every card shows the job title, its current status, how many <a href=\"#/a/what-are-the-coloured-tags-on-my-job-cards\">Total Ads</a> it has, which package paid for it, and any Booster or Premium badge — see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-the-coloured-tags-on-my-job-cards\">what the coloured tags on a job card mean</a>. Below that sit Applicants, Recommendations, Jobseeker Questions and Views, plus the employment type, who posted it, the location and the expiry date.</p> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Open <strong>Manage Job &rarr; Job Ads &amp; Application</strong>.</li> <li>Check the counters at the top for a quick overview.</li> <li>Find the job card you need and review its numbers and tags.</li> <li>Use <strong>Mark Filled / Hired</strong>, <strong>Boost Ads</strong> or <strong>Manage Applicants</strong> directly on the card.</li> </ol> <p>Each card gives you three actions without leaving the page. Switching between the five tabs above the cards changes which ads you see — see <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-five-tabs-mean\">what the five tabs mean</a>.</p>","r":["what-do-the-five-tabs-mean","where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied"]},{"u":"what-do-the-five-tabs-mean","t":"What do the five tabs mean?","c":5,"s":"Job Manager","b":"<p>The five tabs above your job cards — <strong>Active</strong>, <strong>Pending</strong>, <strong>Draft</strong>, <strong>Filled</strong> and <strong>Expired</strong> — split your ads by status, and only one of them is visible to jobseekers.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Tab</th><th>Can jobseekers see it?</th><th>What you can do</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td><strong>Active</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>Manage applicants, boost, edit, mark filled</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>Pending</strong></td><td>No — under review</td><td>View Pending Reason only</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>Draft</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Finish and submit. Nothing charged</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>Filled</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Closed by you</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>Expired</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Its published period ended</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p><strong>Active</strong> is the only tab jobseekers ever see — these are the ads currently live on Maukerja and Ricebowl. If an ad is still under review, it sits in <strong>Pending</strong> until AJobThing approves it; see <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live\">why a job ad stays Pending and when it goes live</a>. An unfinished ad stays in <strong>Draft</strong> — nothing is charged until you submit it, so you can <a href=\"#/a/can-i-save-a-job-ad-and-finish-later\">save a job ad and finish it later</a>. <strong>Filled</strong> holds roles you closed yourself after hiring, and <strong>Expired</strong> holds ads whose published period simply ended.</p> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">GOOD TO KNOW</strong> Moving a job to Filled or Expired returns its capacity to your package on the next monthly refresh, so <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired\">closing a role as soon as you hire</a> is worth doing promptly.</div>","r":["how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired","how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page","what-are-the-coloured-tags-on-my-job-cards"]},{"u":"what-are-the-coloured-tags-on-my-job-cards","t":"What do the tags and numbers on my job cards mean?","c":5,"s":"Job Manager","b":"<p><strong>Each job card carries three things worth reading: coloured tags, a Total Ads count, and performance numbers.</strong> Together they tell you what the ad cost and what it is doing for you.</p>\n<h2>The coloured tags</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Free Job Ad</strong> &mdash; this one came out of your free balance, not a paid package. See <a href=\"#/a/which-job-ads-are-free\">which job ads are free</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Package names</strong> such as Growth Hire, Easy Hire Trial or AI Yearly Plan &mdash; shows exactly which package was used.</li>\n<li><strong>Booster</strong> or <strong>Booster Plus</strong> &mdash; a paid visibility add-on is active on this ad; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-boost-ads\">what Boost Ads does</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Premium</strong> &mdash; the ad is running in the premium format.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>\"N Total Ads\"</h2>\n<p>A Total Ads count higher than one is <em>not</em> extra job ads you paid for &mdash; it is your ad plus the free similar versions AJobThing creates for it.</p>\n<div class=\"note\"><strong class=\"lb\">FROM AJOBTHING</strong> AJobThing automatically creates and posts similar versions of your live job ads across its platforms (Maukerja, Ricebowl, EpiCareer) to increase your reach. These are free and do not count toward your monthly job ad limit.</div>\n<p>So <strong>6 Total Ads</strong> means one ad from your quota plus five free similar versions. How many free similar ads you get each month depends on <a href=\"#/a/what-do-i-get-in-every-plan\">your plan</a>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Free similar ads per month</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody><tr><td>Starter Hire</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Growth Hire</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>Pro Hire</td><td>80</td></tr></tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>\"N/A\" instead of a number</h2>\n<p>N/A means the ad is not live yet, so there is nothing to measure &mdash; it is not a fault. Ads in the <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-five-tabs-mean\">Pending tab</a> show N/A for Applicants, Recommendations and Jobseeker Questions, and 0 for Views, because jobseekers cannot see a job that has not been approved. Real counts appear once the ad turns Active; if it stays Pending, click <strong>View Pending Reason</strong> on the card &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live\">why a job ad is Pending</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">If a tag does not match what you expected, check <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">your packages and balances</a>. If a number disagrees with another screen, see <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-numbers-on-my-dashboard-mean\">numbers on different screens don't match</a>.</div>","r":["what-is-boost-ads","where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances"]},{"u":"how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad","t":"How do I edit a live job ad?","c":5,"s":"Editing & Expiry","b":"<p>Open the ad from <strong>Job Ads &amp; Application</strong> and edit it directly from the job card — you do not need to repost it.</p> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Open <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page\">Job Ads &amp; Application</a> and find the ad.</li> <li>Open the options on the job card.</li> <li>Edit the details you need to change and save.</li> </ol> <div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">CANNOT BE EDITED</strong> The job title cannot be changed this way. If you need a different title, contact AJobThing support — see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">how to contact AJobThing</a>. A strong title matters, so it is worth <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">getting it right before you post</a>.</div> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">DON'T REPOST</strong> Deleting and reposting to fix a small detail uses another job ad from your quota and leaves your existing applicants behind on the old ad. Editing keeps the same ad, the same link, and the same applicant list.</div>","r":["how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find","how-long-does-a-job-ad-stay-live","can-i-pause-extend-or-renew-a-job-ad"]},{"u":"how-long-does-a-job-ad-stay-live","t":"How long does a job ad stay live?","c":5,"s":"Editing & Expiry","b":"<p>By default a job ad stays live for <strong>30 days</strong>. AJobThing's Terms state: \"Job advertisements remain published for thirty (30) days unless a purchased promotion extends the period.\"</p> <h2>Auto-extend</h2> <p>Many job cards show <strong>Auto-extends</strong> and a date instead of a fixed end date. AJobThing explains this as: \"Your job ad is auto-extended after 30 days. It stays active, no need repost.\" If your card shows this, there is nothing for you to do — the ad keeps running until you close it yourself, for example by <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired\">marking it Filled once you hire</a>.</p> <p>For an ad without auto-extend, or if you want to stop one before its date, see <a href=\"#/a/can-i-pause-extend-or-renew-a-job-ad\">whether you can pause, extend or renew a job ad</a>.</p> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">KEEP AN EYE ON THIS</strong> The <strong>Expiring soon</strong> counter at the top of <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page\">Job Ads &amp; Application</a> shows what is close to ending, so you are not caught off guard.</div>","r":["can-i-pause-extend-or-renew-a-job-ad","how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad","where-did-the-applicants-from-my-expired-jobs-go"]},{"u":"can-i-pause-extend-or-renew-a-job-ad","t":"Can I pause, extend or renew a job ad?","c":5,"s":"Editing & Expiry","b":"<p>If your job card shows <strong>Auto-extends</strong>, the ad renews itself automatically — you do not need to do anything to keep it running; see <a href=\"#/a/how-long-does-a-job-ad-stay-live\">how long a job ad normally stays live</a>.</p> <div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">DO NOT REPOST TO RENEW</strong> Reposting creates a brand new ad, uses another job ad from your quota, and leaves your existing applicants behind on the old one — see <a href=\"#/a/where-did-the-applicants-from-my-expired-jobs-go\">where applicants from an expired job go</a>.</div> <p>If you are closing the role because you already hired, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired\">mark it Filled instead</a> of pausing it. For an ad with a fixed end date, or to pause one early, ask your Account Manager or message <a href=\"https://wa.me/60189666610\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhatsApp +6018-966 6610</a>.</p>","r":["how-long-does-a-job-ad-stay-live","how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad","where-did-the-applicants-from-my-expired-jobs-go"]},{"u":"where-did-the-applicants-from-my-expired-jobs-go","t":"Where did the applicants from my expired jobs go?","c":5,"s":"Editing & Expiry","b":"<p><strong>Nothing is deleted.</strong> Applicants from a job that has expired or been closed <strong>stay in your account</strong> &mdash; they are not removed just because the ad stopped being live.</p>\n<p>They land in <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-talent-pool\">My Talent Pool</a>, which collects every candidate you have already had contact with, so you can search and reach out to them again the next time you have a similar role open. See <a href=\"#/a/how-long-do-i-keep-access-to-applicants-after-a-job-expires\">how long you keep access to applicants</a>.</p>\n<h2>How to find them again</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <strong>My Talent Pool</strong> and search by role or skill.</li>\n<li>Check whether anyone you unlocked is still within their access window &mdash; that runs on <a href=\"#/a/how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last\">a separate 90 day clock</a> which is not affected by the job ad.</li>\n<li>Move the people you want to consider into a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a> for the new role.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">DO THIS BEFORE YOU CLOSE A ROLE</strong> Save the strong runners-up into a project before the job expires or you <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired\">mark it Filled</a>. That gives you the fastest shortlist the next time you are hiring for something similar.</div>\n<p>Keeping applications is not open-ended: once your recruitment purpose is complete you must delete or anonymise the data, and for candidates you did not hire that is within 6 months of the hiring decision &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a>.</p>","r":["what-is-my-talent-pool","what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one"]},{"u":"how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired","t":"How do I mark a job as Filled / Hired?","c":5,"s":"Closing & Boosting","b":"<p><strong>Click Mark Filled / Hired on the job card once you have hired for the role</strong> &mdash; it closes the ad in one step.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Find the ad in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page\">Job Ads &amp; Application</a>.</li>\n<li>Set the person you hired to <strong>Hired</strong> and give the others a clear status first &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant\">shortlist, reject or hire</a>.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Mark Filled / Hired</strong> on the job card.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What happens next</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The ad stops taking applications immediately.</li>\n<li>It moves from the Active tab to the <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-five-tabs-mean\">Filled tab</a>, where you can still open it and its applicants.</li>\n<li>The job ad capacity <a href=\"#/a/does-marking-a-job-filled-early-refund-my-job-ad\">returns to your package on the next monthly refresh</a>, not instantly.</li>\n<li>It also stops <a href=\"#/a/why-are-my-recommended-candidates-a-poor-match\">generating recommended candidates</a> for a role you have already filled.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">CLOSE IT AS SOON AS YOU HIRE</strong> Leaving a filled job open keeps it live, keeps using a slot, and keeps candidates applying for a role that no longer exists &mdash; which means rejections you could have avoided.</div>\n<p>Save the runners-up into a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a> before you close it, and see <a href=\"#/a/i-have-hired-someone-what-now\">I have hired someone, what now</a> for the full end-of-hire sequence. Applicants remain in your account afterwards &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/where-did-the-applicants-from-my-expired-jobs-go\">where the applicants from your expired jobs go</a>.</p>","r":["does-marking-a-job-filled-early-refund-my-job-ad","what-is-boost-ads","how-much-does-boosting-cost"]},{"u":"does-marking-a-job-filled-early-refund-my-job-ad","t":"Does marking a job Filled early refund my job ad?","c":5,"s":"Closing & Boosting","b":"<p><strong>Not instantly.</strong> Marking a job Filled moves it into the <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-five-tabs-mean\">Filled tab</a>, but that does not put the job ad straight back into your available balance.</p> <div class=\"note\"><strong class=\"lb\">FROM AJOBTHING</strong> \"When a job is set as Filled/Expired, that balance comes back on the next monthly refresh.\"</div> <p>So the slot returns when your package next refreshes, not the moment you <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired\">mark the job Filled</a>. It still pays to close a role as soon as you hire, since the count towards that refresh only starts once the ad is closed.</p> <p>You can see the exact breakdown — total quota, how many are active, how many are available — by hovering <strong>How it works?</strong> on a package card under <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">Billing &rarr; Packages</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired","what-is-boost-ads","how-much-does-boosting-cost"]},{"u":"what-is-boost-ads","t":"What is Boost Ads?","c":5,"s":"Closing & Boosting","b":"<p><strong>Boost Ads</strong> is a set of paid add-ons that make an ad you have already posted more visible, without changing the ad itself. AJobThing describes them as \"add-ons you can use on your job post to give it greater visibility and reach.\"</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Add-on</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td><strong>Feature</strong></td><td>Moves your job to the first page on <a href=\"https://www.maukerja.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Maukerja</a> and <a href=\"https://www.ricebowl.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ricebowl</a></td></tr> <tr><td><strong>Highlight</strong></td><td>Makes the job stand out visually in the listing</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>Bump Up</strong></td><td>Raises your job's placement in the job feed</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Click <strong>Boost Ads</strong> on the job card to open the menu — it gives you <strong>Bump Up</strong> and <strong>Edit Premium Job Ad</strong>. What each add-on costs is covered separately — see <a href=\"#/a/how-much-does-boosting-cost\">how much boosting costs</a>.</p> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">FIX THE AD FIRST</strong> A clear title, a real salary and a scannable description usually do more for applicant numbers than boosting a weak ad — if applicants are the real problem, start with <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">why your job ad is not getting applicants</a>.</div>","r":["how-much-does-boosting-cost","why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants"]},{"u":"how-much-does-boosting-cost","t":"How much does boosting cost?","c":5,"s":"Closing & Boosting","b":"<p>What <a href=\"#/a/what-is-boost-ads\">Boost Ads</a> costs depends on your plan — the hiring plans include an <strong>optional job ad boost</strong> as a paid monthly add-on, and the exact price varies by plan; check <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">your packages and balances</a> to see which plan you are on.</p> <div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">GET THE PRICE BEFORE YOU CONFIRM</strong> The Boost Ads menu does not show a price for <strong>Bump Up</strong> before you click it. Ask your Account Manager, or use <strong>Get Free Quotation</strong> under Shop, so you are not charged for something unexpected.</div> <p>Anything you spend on boosting appears in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">Billing &rarr; Credit History</a>, with the date, who spent it, the item and the job it was spent on — check there if a charge looks unfamiliar.</p>","r":["what-is-boost-ads","how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired","does-marking-a-job-filled-early-refund-my-job-ad"]},{"u":"why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants","t":"Why is my job ad not getting applicants?","c":5,"s":"Closing & Boosting","b":"<p>Work through this checklist in order — most low-applicant ads have one of these causes.</p> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li><strong>Check it is Active</strong>, not Pending or Expired.</li> <li><strong>Add a salary range.</strong> Ads without one get fewer applicants.</li> <li><strong>Check the title.</strong> Use <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-write-a-job-title-jobseekers-will-find\">the words jobseekers actually search for</a>.</li> <li><strong>Break up the description</strong> into bullet points so it is easy to scan.</li> <li><strong>Turn off Confidential</strong> if it is on — see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-confidential-toggle\">what the Confidential toggle hides</a>; your company name and logo help.</li> <li><strong>Verify your company</strong> and complete your Company Page.</li> <li><strong>Do not wait.</strong> Use Candidate Search or <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended Candidates</a> to approach people directly instead of waiting for applications.</li> </ol> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">ASK FOR HELP</strong> Your plan includes advice on this — AJobThing can tell you what to change to get more applicants, so ask your Account Manager rather than reposting the ad.</div>","r":["what-is-boost-ads","what-are-recommended-candidates"]},{"u":"where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied","t":"Where do I see everyone who applied?","c":6,"s":"Receiving Applications","b":"<p><strong>Click Manage Applicants on the job card in Job Ads &amp; Application.</strong> That page holds everyone who applied to that ad, whether or not you received the email alert.</p>\n<h2>How the page is organised</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Three tabs</strong> &mdash; Candidate Activity, Recommended, All Applicants. <strong>All Applicants</strong> is your complete list; <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended</a> is AJobThing's matching, not people who applied.</li>\n<li><strong>Status filters</strong> &mdash; No Status, Shortlisted, Hired, Rejected. These follow the statuses you set in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant\">shortlist, reject or hire</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Filter panel</strong> on the left &mdash; New, Viewed, Hidden, Bookmarked, and Recruiter Screened.</li>\n<li><strong>Select All</strong> and <strong>Sort by</strong> for acting on several people at once &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-filter-sort-or-search-my-applicants\">how to filter, sort or search your applicants</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to expect</h2>\n<p>If nobody has applied yet you will see: \"Congratulations, your job is now live! We'll notify you when you get new applicants.\" A quiet list after a few days is worth investigating &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">why a job ad is not getting applicants</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Work from New first.</strong> The <strong>New applicants</strong> figure on your <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-numbers-on-my-dashboard-mean\">Dashboard</a> counts applications you have not opened, so clearing that filter each day means nobody sits unread. Applicants stay in your account after the ad ends &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-long-do-i-keep-access-to-applicants-after-a-job-expires\">how long you keep access</a>.</div>","r":["how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant","how-do-i-filter-sort-or-search-my-applicants"]},{"u":"i-m-not-receiving-application-emails","t":"I'm not receiving application emails","c":6,"s":"Receiving Applications","b":"<p><strong>Work through these five checks in order.</strong> The cause is usually a setting or a mail filter, and applications are never lost in the meantime.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <strong>Settings &rarr; Notifications &amp; Alerts</strong> and check <strong>Receive on Email</strong> is ticked. Click <strong>Save</strong> &mdash; the setting does not apply until you save.</li>\n<li>Check the email frequency setting. A batched frequency is not a fault; it just means the email is not immediate.</li>\n<li>Check the address under <strong>Contact Information</strong> on the same page is correct and current &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-change-my-name-email-contact-number-or-password\">how to change your email</a>.</li>\n<li>Open the job ad's own <strong>Notification Settings</strong> and confirm the right people are listed &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/who-gets-told-when-someone-applies\">who gets told when someone applies</a>. A per-ad setting can differ from your account default.</li>\n<li>Search your <strong>Spam</strong> and <strong>Junk</strong> folders, and ask IT to allow AJobThing's sender address. Corporate mail filters are the most common culprit once the settings are right.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Applications are never lost.</strong> Even with no email at all, everyone who applied is in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied\">Manage Applicants</a>, and the <strong>New applicants</strong> count on your <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-numbers-on-my-dashboard-mean\">Dashboard</a> tells you there is something to open.</div>\n<p>While you sort the email out, turn on <strong>WhatsApp</strong> as a second channel so nothing waits &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get\">how you will be notified about new applicants</a>. If email is still not arriving after all five checks, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-report-a-problem\">report the problem</a> with the address you expect it to reach.</p>","r":["how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get","where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied"]},{"u":"why-is-the-applicant-s-name-partly-hidden","t":"Why is the applicant's name partly hidden?","c":6,"s":"Reviewing Applicants","b":"<p><strong>Candidates show as a first name and an initial with a locked photo until you have access to the full profile.</strong> Contact details are released through Chat rather than shown on the list.</p>\n<blockquote>Candidate resumes and contact details are now shared through Chat. Start a chat for 5 credits, and once the candidate accepts, you'll be able to view their details directly in the chat.</blockquote>\n<h2>What you can read before spending anything</h2>\n<p>Plenty &mdash; current job title, preferred location, years of experience, education, expected salary, languages, when they were last active, their skills, and the full <strong>Profile Summary</strong>, <strong>Work Experience</strong>, <strong>Skills</strong> and <strong>Education</strong> tabs. See <a href=\"#/a/what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile\">what is on a candidate's profile</a>.</p>\n<h2>How to decide who is worth a chat</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Read the free fields first, especially <strong>expected salary</strong> and <strong>last active</strong>.</li>\n<li>Check the work experience actually matches the role, not just the job title.</li>\n<li>Then start the chat &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged\">what a chat costs and when you are charged</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Use the free fields as your filter.</strong> Someone active this week whose salary expectation fits your range is worth the credits. Someone last active a long time ago at double your budget is not.</div>\n<p>The candidate has to accept before details appear, so a chat is a request rather than a purchase of contact information &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate\">how to start a new chat</a> covers what to write to get a reply. Credit costs shown on screen at the time are the ones that apply to your account; see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on\">what credits can be spent on</a>.</p>","r":["what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged","what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile"]},{"u":"how-do-i-filter-sort-or-search-my-applicants","t":"How do I filter, sort or search my applicants?","c":6,"s":"Reviewing Applicants","b":"<p><strong>Use the filter panel on the left of Manage Applicants, then the status pills across the top.</strong> Together they turn a long list into the handful of people you need to act on now.</p>\n<h2>The filter panel</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Job Ad &amp; Similar Jobs</strong> &mdash; narrow to one ad, or include the free similar versions.</li>\n<li><strong>New</strong> / <strong>Viewed</strong> &mdash; who you have not opened yet. The quickest way to make sure nobody is missed.</li>\n<li><strong>Bookmarked</strong> / <strong>Hidden</strong> &mdash; your own private sorting, invisible to candidates. See <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant\">shortlist, reject or hire</a> for when to use these instead of a status.</li>\n<li><strong>Recruiter Screened</strong> &mdash; under Featured Applicants.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Status pills and sorting</h2>\n<p>The pills &mdash; No Status, Shortlisted, Hired, Rejected &mdash; filter by where each person sits in your process, and <strong>Sort by</strong> changes the order of whatever is showing.</p>\n<h2>A workflow that keeps the list under control</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Filter to <strong>New</strong> and work only through those.</li>\n<li><strong>Bookmark</strong> anyone promising, <strong>Hide</strong> anyone clearly unsuitable &mdash; neither notifies the candidate.</li>\n<li>Come back to <strong>Bookmarked</strong> and set real statuses for the shortlist.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Select All</strong> when the same action applies to several people at once.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\">Add an <a href=\"#/a/can-i-add-private-notes-about-an-applicant\">Internal Comment</a> as you go, so your reasoning is still there when you or a colleague reopens the list &mdash; the whole team works from the same one. See <a href=\"#/a/can-my-colleagues-see-the-same-applicants-and-chats\">can my colleagues see the same applicants</a>.</div>","r":["how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant","why-is-the-applicant-s-name-partly-hidden","can-i-add-private-notes-about-an-applicant"]},{"u":"can-i-add-private-notes-about-an-applicant","t":"Can I add private notes about an applicant?","c":6,"s":"Reviewing Applicants","b":"<p><strong>Yes &mdash; use Internal Comment.</strong> AJobThing describes it as a way \"to leave comments or notes about the candidate, for your or your colleagues' future reference.\"</p>\n<h2>Who sees them</h2>\n<p>Your team can see the notes. <strong>Candidates cannot.</strong> Because applicants are shared across the account, a comment you write is visible to colleagues working the same list &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-my-colleagues-see-the-same-applicants-and-chats\">can my colleagues see the same applicants and chats</a>.</p>\n<h2>What to write</h2>\n<p>Notes are most useful when they record something the profile does not already say: what the candidate told you about notice period, why they were shortlisted or passed over, what to ask at the next interview. That is what stops a colleague repeating a screening call you already did.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Do</strong> keep them factual and job-related &mdash; skills, availability, interview outcome.</li>\n<li><strong>Don't</strong> record personal characteristics or anything you would not be comfortable showing the candidate.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>It is still written information about a real person.</strong> Internal notes form part of the candidate data you hold, so the handling rules in <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a> apply to them too, including deleting the data once your recruitment purpose is complete.</div>\n<p>Pair notes with the private filters &mdash; <strong>Bookmark</strong> and <strong>Hide</strong> &mdash; to keep your pipeline organised without notifying anyone: see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant\">shortlist, reject or hire an applicant</a>.</p>","r":["can-my-colleagues-see-the-same-applicants-and-chats","why-is-the-applicant-s-name-partly-hidden","how-do-i-filter-sort-or-search-my-applicants"]},{"u":"can-my-colleagues-see-the-same-applicants-and-chats","t":"Can my colleagues see the same applicants and chats?","c":6,"s":"Reviewing Applicants","b":"<p><strong>Yes, if they have the right access.</strong> Your account works from one shared pipeline rather than a separate one per person.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chats</strong> &mdash; \"Team members with the correct account access can view and manage candidate conversations.\" So a colleague can pick up a conversation you started.</li>\n<li><strong>Applicants</strong> &mdash; shared. Everyone works from the same list, and a status one person sets is what the others see.</li>\n<li><strong>Internal Comments</strong> &mdash; visible to your team, never to the candidate. See <a href=\"#/a/can-i-add-private-notes-about-an-applicant\">can I add private notes about an applicant</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to set it up</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Add colleagues under <strong>Team Management</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-team-member\">how to invite a team member</a>.</li>\n<li>Give each person the role that matches what they should be able to do &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-the-roles-and-what-can-each-one-do\">what the roles can each do</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What this means day to day</h2>\n<p>Shared visibility is what makes cover work: someone on leave does not block a candidate, and a hiring manager can read the chat before the interview. It also means two people can act on the same applicant, so agree who owns which ad, and use <a href=\"#/a/can-i-add-private-notes-about-an-applicant\">Internal Comments</a> to record decisions rather than keeping them in your own inbox.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">Because the list is shared anyway, there is no reason to hand your password around &mdash; and a shared login costs you the record of who spent which credits. See <a href=\"#/a/can-two-people-share-one-login\">can two people share one login</a>.</div>","r":["how-do-i-invite-a-team-member","can-two-people-share-one-login"]},{"u":"how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant","t":"How do I shortlist, reject or hire an applicant?","c":6,"s":"Your Pipeline","b":"<p><strong>Open the applicant in Manage Applicants and set the status.</strong> Three of the statuses reach the candidate, so it matters which one you pick.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied\"><strong>Manage Applicants</strong></a> and click the person.</li>\n<li>Set the status: <strong>Shortlisted</strong>, <strong>Rejected</strong> or <strong>Hired</strong>.</li>\n<li>Check which <a href=\"#/a/what-are-message-templates\">message template</a> is bound to that action first &mdash; that template is what the candidate receives.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Shortlist, Reject and Hire all notify the candidate automatically.</strong> Only set them when you mean it. There is no way to unsend the notification.</div>\n<h2>When you have not decided yet</h2>\n<p>Use the private tools instead &mdash; neither tells the candidate anything:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bookmark</strong> &mdash; save someone to come back to.</li>\n<li><strong>Hide</strong> &mdash; take them out of your working list without rejecting them.</li>\n<li><strong>KIV</strong> (\"Keep In View\") &mdash; a status that does not notify the jobseeker.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both Bookmarked and Hidden are filters in the panel on the left, so nothing is really removed &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-filter-sort-or-search-my-applicants\">how to filter, sort or search your applicants</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Close the loop at the end.</strong> Once you hire, give everyone else a clear Rejected status rather than leaving them waiting &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/i-have-hired-someone-what-now\">I have hired someone, what now</a>. Record your reasoning in an <a href=\"#/a/can-i-add-private-notes-about-an-applicant\">Internal Comment</a> so the next opening starts from a shortlist, not a blank page.</div>","r":["what-are-message-templates","can-i-add-private-notes-about-an-applicant"]},{"u":"what-are-message-templates","t":"What are message templates?","c":6,"s":"Your Pipeline","b":"<p><strong>Message templates are saved messages you reuse</strong> &mdash; so your team writes consistently and nobody retypes the same interview invitation or rejection note.</p>\n<h2>Bound or General</h2>\n<blockquote>You can bind this template to a particular action. For example, if you choose \"Hired\", then when you want to hire any applicant, we will use this template to send message to applicant. If you choose \"General\", this template will not be bind to any particular action and you may choose whenever you want to use it.</blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bound</strong> &mdash; you set the status, and this message goes out automatically. This is what a candidate receives when you <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant\">shortlist, reject or hire</a> them.</li>\n<li><strong>General</strong> &mdash; nothing automatic. You pick it when you want it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to use them well</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Bind the ones you send constantly &mdash; interview invite, rejection &mdash; so no candidate is left without a reply.</li>\n<li>Leave situational messages as <strong>General</strong>.</li>\n<li>Read a bound template before you use the status it is attached to. It is going out under your company's name, and once sent it cannot be recalled.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Keep bound templates short and neutral.</strong> They have to fit every candidate who gets that status, so anything role-specific belongs in a message you send yourself in <a href=\"#/a/what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message\">Chat</a> &mdash; that is also where <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-candidate-to-an-interview\">interview details</a> are best confirmed.</div>","r":["how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant","how-long-do-i-keep-access-to-applicants-after-a-job-expires"]},{"u":"how-long-do-i-keep-access-to-applicants-after-a-job-expires","t":"How long do I keep access to applicants after a job expires?","c":6,"s":"Your Pipeline","b":"<p><strong>Applicants are not removed when the ad ends</strong> &mdash; they stay in your account and in <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-talent-pool\">My Talent Pool</a>, so a closed ad does not cost you the people who applied to it. See <a href=\"#/a/where-did-the-applicants-from-my-expired-jobs-go\">where the applicants from your expired jobs go</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Unlocked contact details are a separate clock.</strong> A profile you unlocked carries a <strong>90 day</strong> unlock expiry, which runs independently of the job ad &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last\">how long an unlocked profile lasts</a> and <a href=\"#/a/what-s-the-difference-between-unlock-expiry-and-package-validity\">the difference between unlock expiry and package validity</a>.</div>\n<h2>What you should do before that clock runs out</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Save anyone worth keeping into a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a> while the ad is still fresh in your mind.</li>\n<li>Move the details you genuinely need for hiring into your own HR records.</li>\n<li>Set clear statuses on everyone else so the list is usable when you reopen the role.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Your obligation on the data</h2>\n<p>Under AJobThing's Terms you must delete or anonymise candidate data once your recruitment purpose is complete, unless the law requires you to keep it. Retention is your decision to make and document &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a> covers the rules, and if you are unsure how they apply to your own records, take your own legal advice.</p>","r":["how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last","what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details"]},{"u":"how-do-i-invite-a-candidate-to-an-interview","t":"How do I invite a candidate to an interview?","c":6,"s":"Interviews","b":"<p><strong>Send the invitation from inside the chat with the candidate.</strong> Keeping it in the conversation means their reply lands in the same place, so you can see who confirmed.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open the conversation in <a href=\"#/a/what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message\"><strong>Chat</strong></a>.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Interview</strong> in the conversation header.</li>\n<li>Fill in the details and send.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>You can also start from <strong>Manage Job &rarr; Interview Applicants</strong> and use <strong>Schedule</strong> next to a name &mdash; useful when you are booking several people in one sitting. See <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-track-everyone-at-the-interview-stage\">where you track everyone at the interview stage</a>.</p>\n<h2>What to include so people turn up</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Date, time and the full address, or the video link.</li>\n<li>Who they will meet and how long it will take.</li>\n<li>Anything to bring, and a number to reach you on the day.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Whether the candidate is notified depends on where you scheduled from.</strong> Read <a href=\"#/a/does-the-candidate-get-told-when-i-create-an-appointment\">does the candidate get told when I create an appointment</a> and confirm in your own words if there is any doubt &mdash; that is what prevents a no-show.</div>\n<p>If you send the same invitation often, save it as a <a href=\"#/a/what-are-message-templates\">message template</a> so the details are consistent every time.</p>","r":["does-the-candidate-get-told-when-i-create-an-appointment","where-do-i-track-everyone-at-the-interview-stage"]},{"u":"where-do-i-track-everyone-at-the-interview-stage","t":"Where do I track everyone at the interview stage?","c":6,"s":"Interviews","b":"<p><strong>Open Manage Job &rarr; Interview Applicants.</strong> The page tracks \"every candidate currently in the interview stage across all your job ads in one place\" &mdash; so you are not opening each ad in turn.</p>\n<h2>What the page gives you</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A table of the applicant, the job they applied for and when they applied.</li>\n<li>A <strong>Schedule</strong> action on each row, for <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-candidate-to-an-interview\">booking the interview</a>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>Calendar</strong> view, for seeing the week rather than the list.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open the page and scan for anyone with no appointment yet.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Schedule</strong> to book them, then confirm the details in chat &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/does-the-candidate-get-told-when-i-create-an-appointment\">does the candidate get told when I create an appointment</a>.</li>\n<li>Switch to <strong>Calendar</strong> to check the day is not overloaded before you offer more slots.</li>\n<li>After each interview, update the status in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant\">Manage Applicants</a> so the list stays accurate.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\">The <strong>Total Interview</strong> counter at the top of <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page\">Job Ads &amp; Application</a> has a <strong>View</strong> link straight to this page &mdash; a quick way to check nobody is sitting at the interview stage with nothing booked.</div>\n<p>Candidates only reach this page once you move them into the interview stage, so if it looks emptier than expected, check the statuses in your applicant list first.</p>","r":["how-do-i-invite-a-candidate-to-an-interview","does-the-candidate-get-told-when-i-create-an-appointment"]},{"u":"does-the-candidate-get-told-when-i-create-an-appointment","t":"Does the candidate get told when I create an appointment?","c":6,"s":"Interviews","b":"<p><strong>It depends where you schedule from</strong> &mdash; and AJobThing shows two different notes, so it is worth knowing which screen you are on.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>On <strong>Interview Applicants</strong>: \"The applicant is notified automatically by chat when you create this appointment.\"</li>\n<li>On the interview calendar tip in <strong>Job Ads &amp; Application</strong>: \"Scheduling here only updates your calendar. Please confirm with applicants separately.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Safest way</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Create the appointment &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-candidate-to-an-interview\">how to invite a candidate to an interview</a>.</li>\n<li>Send the candidate a chat message with the date, time and address in your own words.</li>\n<li>Wait for a reply that confirms it, and follow up if none comes.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Never assume a candidate has seen an appointment they have not replied to.</strong> Ten seconds of typing prevents a no-show, and a confirmed reply in the conversation is your record that they knew.</div>\n<p>Keeping the confirmation in <a href=\"#/a/what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message\">Chat</a> also means a colleague covering for you can see the arrangement &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-my-colleagues-see-the-same-applicants-and-chats\">can my colleagues see the same applicants and chats</a>. Track who has and has not confirmed from <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-track-everyone-at-the-interview-stage\">Interview Applicants</a>, and note that a chat can have a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-countdown-in-my-chat\">countdown</a>, so do not leave the confirmation to the last moment.</p>","r":["how-do-i-invite-a-candidate-to-an-interview","where-do-i-track-everyone-at-the-interview-stage"]},{"u":"i-have-hired-someone-what-now","t":"I have hired someone. What now?","c":6,"s":"After You Hire","b":"<p><strong>Three things: set the hire, close out everyone else, then close the ad.</strong> Doing them in that order means no candidate is left waiting and the ad stops collecting applications you no longer need.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Set the applicant to <strong>Hired</strong>. This notifies them &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant\">how to shortlist, reject or hire</a>.</li>\n<li>Give everyone else a clear <strong>Rejected</strong> status rather than leaving them waiting. A <a href=\"#/a/what-are-message-templates\">bound message template</a> handles the wording for you.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Mark Filled / Hired</strong> on the job card to close the ad &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired\">how to mark a job as Filled / Hired</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What happens after that</h2>\n<p>The job stops taking applications and moves to the <strong>Filled</strong> tab. The capacity returns to your package on the next monthly refresh &mdash; it is not an instant refund, so read <a href=\"#/a/does-marking-a-job-filled-early-refund-my-job-ad\">does marking a job Filled early refund my job ad</a> before you close it early.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Before you close it, save the strong runners-up into a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a>.</strong> They are your shortlist for the next opening, and it is far cheaper than sourcing from scratch. Applicants stay accessible after the ad ends &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-long-do-i-keep-access-to-applicants-after-a-job-expires\">how long you keep access</a> &mdash; but any unlocked contact details run on their own <a href=\"#/a/how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last\">90 day clock</a>.</div>\n<p>Once your recruitment purpose is complete, the retention rules in <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a> apply to the applications you keep.</p>","r":["how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired","what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one"]},{"u":"what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details","t":"What can I do with a candidate's details?","c":6,"s":"Candidate Data & Privacy","b":"<p><strong>Once you receive candidate data you become an independent data controller under the PDPA 2010</strong> and are responsible for your own compliance. The rules are in AJobThing's <a href=\"https://www.ajobthing.com/dpa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Data Processing Agreement</a>.</p>\n<h2>You may</h2>\n<p>Use it only \"to assess and process candidates for a genuine employment opportunity with you or your disclosed client\" &mdash; so if you are hiring on behalf of someone else, that client has to be disclosed.</p>\n<h2>You must not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Sell, rent, trade or share it beyond your recruitment staff and agents</li>\n<li>Use it for marketing or lead generation</li>\n<li>Use it to build or train any database, model or product</li>\n<li>Contact candidates for any other purpose &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-am-i-not-allowed-to-send-in-chat\">what you are not allowed to send in chat</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>You must also</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Protect it with practical security measures</li>\n<li>Delete or anonymise it once your recruitment purpose is complete &mdash; and for candidates you did not hire, <strong>within 6 months of the hiring decision</strong></li>\n<li>Honour candidate requests for access, correction or consent withdrawal</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Breach means suspension without refund</strong>, on top of your own liability under the PDPA. A <a href=\"#/a/do-i-need-consent-for-a-background-check\">background check needs separate consent</a>, and a data breach must be reported &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/report-a-data-breach\">report a data breach</a>.</div>\n<p>In practice this shapes how you keep records: what you hold in <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-talent-pool\">My Talent Pool</a>, in a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a>, in <a href=\"#/a/can-i-add-private-notes-about-an-applicant\">Internal Comments</a> and in your own HR files all falls under it &mdash; see also <a href=\"#/a/how-long-do-i-keep-access-to-applicants-after-a-job-expires\">how long you keep access to applicants</a>. Read the Data Processing Agreement itself for the binding wording, and take your own legal advice on what your company must retain.</p>","r":["do-i-need-consent-for-a-background-check","report-a-data-breach"]},{"u":"do-i-need-consent-for-a-background-check","t":"Do I need consent for a background check?","c":6,"s":"Candidate Data & Privacy","b":"<p><strong>Yes, separately and in writing.</strong> AJobThing is explicit:</p>\n<blockquote>Receipt of Candidate Data is not consent for background, reference, or verification checks. You are solely responsible for obtaining the candidate's separate, explicit consent before initiating any such check.</blockquote>\n<h2>What to do</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Ask the candidate first</strong>, before you contact a referee, a former employer or a screening provider.</li>\n<li><strong>Say what you will check and who with</strong>, so the consent covers what you actually do.</li>\n<li><strong>Get it in writing and keep their reply.</strong> Asking in <a href=\"#/a/what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message\">Chat</a> gives you a dated record in the conversation; an email reply works too.</li>\n<li><strong>Only then start the check.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>A candidate applying for your job has consented to being considered for it &mdash; nothing more. Contacting a current employer without asking can cost someone their job, which is why the consent has to be specific and given before you act. Note that this consent is a separate step from your wider obligations as a data controller; see <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a>, including honouring a later withdrawal of consent.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">Keep the consent with your own hiring records rather than only in a chat, since <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-countdown-in-my-chat\">conversations have a time limit</a> and <a href=\"#/a/how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last\">unlocked access expires</a>. What is legally sufficient for your company is a question for your own legal advice &mdash; AJobThing's requirement is the minimum, not the whole picture.</div>","r":["what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details","report-a-data-breach"]},{"u":"report-a-data-breach","t":"Report a data breach","c":6,"s":"Candidate Data & Privacy","b":"<p><strong>If you suffer a personal data breach affecting candidate data you obtained through AJobThing, you must notify them &mdash; within 48 hours of becoming aware of it.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Email:</strong> dataprivacy@ajobthing.com</li>\n<li><strong>Deadline:</strong> without undue delay, and no later than <strong>48 hours</strong> after you become aware of it</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to do, in order</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Contain it</strong> &mdash; stop the exposure, revoke access, secure the affected system or file.</li>\n<li><strong>Establish the scope</strong> &mdash; which candidates, which data, and when it happened.</li>\n<li><strong>Email dataprivacy@ajobthing.com</strong> inside the 48 hours with what you know so far. Do not wait for a complete investigation to make the notification.</li>\n<li><strong>Record what you did</strong> and keep that record.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>This duty continues even after your account is closed.</strong> The full terms are in AJobThing's <a href=\"https://www.ajobthing.com/dpa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Data Processing Agreement</a>.</div>\n<h2>Your own obligations are separate</h2>\n<p>Notifying AJobThing does not discharge what you may owe under the PDPA 2010 or to the affected candidates &mdash; you are an <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">independent data controller</a> for the data you hold. Take your own legal advice on what else you must do.</p>\n<p>Reducing the risk is largely about holding less: delete or anonymise data once the recruitment purpose is complete, keep candidate details out of loose spreadsheets and personal drives, and give each colleague their own login rather than <a href=\"#/a/can-two-people-share-one-login\">a shared one</a>. For problems with the platform rather than your own systems, see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-report-a-problem\">how to report a problem</a>.</p>","r":["what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details","do-i-need-consent-for-a-background-check"]},{"u":"what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message","t":"What is AJobThing Chat and who can I message?","c":7,"s":"Chat Basics","b":"<p><strong>Chat is one place to talk to candidates, instead of email and WhatsApp.</strong> The conversation stays attached to the candidate and the job, so your team can pick it up too.</p>\n<h2>Two kinds of conversation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Someone who applied to your job</strong> &mdash; open Chat from <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied\">your candidate list</a> and start typing. This uses 1 chat from your monthly total.</li>\n<li><strong>Someone you found yourself</strong> in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">Candidate Search</a> or <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended Candidates</a> &mdash; this costs 5 credits, and they must accept your chat request first.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Full details of what each one uses are in <a href=\"#/a/what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged\">what a chat costs and when you are charged</a>.</p>\n<h2>What you see in a conversation</h2>\n<p>The candidate's name, the job they applied for, whether they are <strong>Online</strong> or <strong>Offline</strong>, an <strong>Interview</strong> button for <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-candidate-to-an-interview\">sending an invitation</a>, and a countdown showing how long the conversation stays open &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-countdown-in-my-chat\">what the countdown means</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>The Online indicator is worth using.</strong> A message to someone who is online right now is far more likely to get an answer the same day, which matters when a countdown is running. Colleagues with the right access can see and manage these conversations too &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-my-colleagues-see-the-same-applicants-and-chats\">can my colleagues see the same applicants and chats</a>.</div>\n<p>Before you start, know what you can and cannot send &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-send-in-a-chat\">what you can send</a> and <a href=\"#/a/what-am-i-not-allowed-to-send-in-chat\">what you are not allowed to send</a>.</p>","v":[{"i":"2D5_9AYVjBQ","t":"Chat with candidates and hire 2x faster","d":"0:31","w":"The Chat workspace with the candidate profile beside the conversation."}],"r":["what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged","how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate"]},{"u":"how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate","t":"How do I start a new chat with a candidate?","c":7,"s":"Chat Basics","b":"<p><strong>Open the candidate and click Chat.</strong> How it proceeds depends on whether they applied to you or you found them.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open the candidate &mdash; from <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied\">Manage Applicants</a>, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">Candidate Search</a> or <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended Candidates</a>.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Chat</strong> or <strong>Start Chat</strong>.</li>\n<li>Type your message.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>If you found them yourself</h2>\n<p>They get a chat request with <strong>Accept &amp; Chat</strong> and <strong>Decline</strong> buttons. You see their resume and contact details only after they accept &mdash; which is why <a href=\"#/a/why-is-the-applicant-s-name-partly-hidden\">names and photos are partly hidden</a> until then.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>The 5 credits go when you send the request</strong> &mdash; not when they accept, and not when they reply. Read the free part of the profile first: <a href=\"#/a/what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile\">what is on a candidate's profile</a> shows how much you can assess for nothing, and <a href=\"#/a/what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged\">what a chat costs</a> covers the rest.</div>\n<h2>Write a first message that gets accepted</h2>\n<p>Say who you are, which role you are hiring for and why you contacted this person specifically. A generic opener is the most common reason a request is declined, and a declined request still spent the credits. Mentioning the salary range and location upfront saves a round of questions.</p>\n<p>Once the conversation is open, further messages in it cost nothing extra, so use it properly &mdash; and watch <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-countdown-in-my-chat\">the countdown</a>. If chat reports \"Not authorized\", see <a href=\"#/a/chat-says-not-authorized\">chat says \"Not authorized\"</a>.</p>","v":[{"i":"2D5_9AYVjBQ","t":"Chat with candidates and hire 2x faster","d":"0:31","w":"The Chat workspace with the candidate profile beside the conversation."}],"r":["what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged","what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile"]},{"u":"what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged","t":"What does a chat cost, and when am I charged?","c":7,"s":"Chat Basics","b":"<p><strong>Messaging your own applicants uses your monthly chat allowance; messaging candidates you found yourself uses credits.</strong> Either way you are charged per candidate, when the conversation starts.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Who you message</th><th>What it uses</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Someone who applied to your job</td><td>1 chat from your monthly total</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A candidate from <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">Candidate Search</a></td><td>5 credits</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended Candidate</a></td><td>5 credits</td></tr>\n<tr><td>An extra applicant once your monthly chats run out</td><td>5 credits</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>AJobThing's wording: \"Each time you start a conversation with a job applicant, it uses 1 chat from your monthly total. Your total resets automatically each month on your renewal date.\" And: \"1 chat costs 5 credits.\"</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Per candidate, not per message.</strong> Once a conversation is open, sending more messages in it does not cost more &mdash; so there is no reason to compress everything into one long message.</div>\n<h2>What this means for how you work</h2>\n<p>The cost falls at the moment you start the conversation, including for a candidate who never replies &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate\">how to start a new chat</a>. So the screening you do beforehand is what protects your balance: read the free profile fields, and start chats with the people you would genuinely interview.</p>\n<p>Your monthly chat total depends on your plan, and how many credits you hold is shown in your account &mdash; check <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">your credit balance and history</a> and <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">your packages</a> for the figures that apply to you, rather than assuming. See also <a href=\"#/a/what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on\">what credits can be spent on</a>.</p>","r":["what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on","where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history"]},{"u":"what-is-the-countdown-in-my-chat","t":"What is the countdown in my chat?","c":7,"s":"Chat Basics","b":"<p><strong>The small clock in the conversation header shows how long that conversation stays open</strong>, counting down in days.</p>\n<p>AJobThing's position: \"Chat availability may have a conversation time limit based on your account or plan. If a chat is no longer available, you may need to contact the candidate using other available methods.\"</p>\n<h2>What to do while it is running</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Reply to anything waiting &mdash; your Dashboard's <strong>Unresponded chats</strong> counter shows what is outstanding; see <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-numbers-on-my-dashboard-mean\">what the Dashboard numbers mean</a>.</li>\n<li>If you are close to hiring someone, get their email or phone number inside the chat while it is open.</li>\n<li>Send the <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-candidate-to-an-interview\">interview invitation</a> now rather than later, and confirm the details in writing &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/does-the-candidate-get-told-when-i-create-an-appointment\">does the candidate get told when I create an appointment</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Do not let a good conversation sit.</strong> When a chat closes you lose the easiest route back to that candidate, and starting again may cost you another chat or credits &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged\">what a chat costs</a>.</div>\n<p>The exact limit depends on your account or plan, so check what your own chat header shows rather than assuming a fixed number of days. Because the limit is per conversation, it is worth starting chats only with candidates you are ready to act on &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate\">how to start a new chat</a> &mdash; and <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-send-in-a-chat\">what you can send in a chat</a> covers getting documents across before it closes.</p>","r":["what-can-i-send-in-a-chat","what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message","how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate"]},{"u":"what-can-i-send-in-a-chat","t":"What can I send in a chat?","c":7,"s":"Rules & Limits","b":"<p><strong>Text, images and PDF documents.</strong> That covers most of what hiring needs &mdash; interview details, directions, or an offer letter.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Text messages</li>\n<li>Images &mdash; PNG, JPG, JPEG</li>\n<li>PDF documents</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Tools that save time</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AI smart replies</strong> &mdash; suggested responses based on the conversation. Read before sending; the suggestion does not know your role's specifics.</li>\n<li><strong>Bulk chat</strong> &mdash; send one update to many applicants at once. Useful for telling a group that interviews are next week, not for anything personal.</li>\n<li><strong>Message templates</strong> &mdash; for consistent wording across your team; see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-message-templates\">what message templates are</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to send, and when</h2>\n<p>Attachments are worth using for anything the candidate needs to keep: a map screenshot for the interview address, or the offer letter as a PDF. Send them while the conversation is open &mdash; <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-countdown-in-my-chat\">the countdown</a> limits how long you have.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Some things must never be sent or requested in chat</strong> &mdash; payment requests, bank details, identity card copies, or questions about age, gender, race, religion or marital status. See <a href=\"#/a/what-am-i-not-allowed-to-send-in-chat\">what you are not allowed to send in chat</a>.</div>\n<p>To book an interview, use the <strong>Interview</strong> button in the conversation header rather than only describing it in a message &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-candidate-to-an-interview\">how to invite a candidate to an interview</a>.</p>","r":["what-am-i-not-allowed-to-send-in-chat","what-are-message-templates"]},{"u":"what-am-i-not-allowed-to-send-in-chat","t":"What am I not allowed to send in chat?","c":7,"s":"Rules & Limits","b":"<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Never ask a jobseeker for money.</strong> Requesting payment as a condition of employment is banned in AJobThing's Terms and can get your account suspended without refund &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-i-ask-candidates-to-pay-a-deposit-or-training-fee\">can I ask candidates to pay a deposit or training fee</a>.</div>\n<h2>Also avoid</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Asking for bank details, identity card copies or deposits.</strong> If you need identity or bank information for payroll, collect it after the hire through your own HR process, not in a chat with a candidate.</li>\n<li><strong>Questions about age, gender, race, religion or marital status</strong> &mdash; the same rules that apply to your job ad apply here; see <a href=\"#/a/can-i-state-age-gender-race-or-nationality-preferences\">can I state age, gender, race or nationality preferences</a> and <a href=\"#/a/what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad\">what content is not allowed in a job ad</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Using candidate contact details for marketing</strong> or anything other than this job &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to ask instead</h2>\n<p>Everything you need to assess someone can be asked in job terms: availability and notice period rather than personal circumstances, ability to meet the role's requirements rather than age, language proficiency the job actually needs rather than background. That keeps the conversation compliant and gives you better information.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>A real employer never asks a candidate to pay for training, uniforms or processing before they start.</strong> If a candidate raises a message that looks like this from another party, they should report it &mdash; and you can <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-report-a-problem\">report a problem</a> to AJobThing. For anything involving a background check, consent comes first: see <a href=\"#/a/do-i-need-consent-for-a-background-check\">do I need consent for a background check</a>.</div>","r":["what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad","what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details"]},{"u":"will-i-be-told-when-a-candidate-replies","t":"Will I be told when a candidate replies?","c":7,"s":"Rules & Limits","b":"<p><strong>Yes.</strong> \"You will receive notifications when a candidate sends you a new message, so you can reply quickly and keep the hiring process moving.\"</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Desktop</strong> &mdash; while you are logged in to AJobThing.</li>\n<li><strong>Mobile app</strong> &mdash; on Android and iOS, which is what covers you when you are away from your desk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your Dashboard also shows an <strong>Unresponded chats</strong> counter, so nothing depends on you spotting a notification &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-numbers-on-my-dashboard-mean\">what the Dashboard numbers mean</a>.</p>\n<h2>If replies seem to be going unnoticed</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Check the counter on your Dashboard and open <a href=\"#/a/what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message\">Chat</a> directly &mdash; the messages are there whether or not the alert reached you.</li>\n<li>Review your channels in <strong>Settings &rarr; Notifications &amp; Alerts</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get\">how to choose which alerts you get</a>.</li>\n<li>Install the mobile app if candidates tend to reply after office hours.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Speed matters more here than anywhere else in the process.</strong> Conversations run on <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-countdown-in-my-chat\">a countdown</a>, and a candidate who has messaged you is actively job-hunting, so a same-day reply is often what decides whether they stay interested.</div>\n<p>Colleagues with the right access can also see and answer these conversations, which is what keeps replies moving when you are on leave &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-my-colleagues-see-the-same-applicants-and-chats\">can my colleagues see the same applicants and chats</a>. All in-platform notifications are collected in one list; see <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-all-my-notifications\">where you see all your notifications</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get","what-can-i-send-in-a-chat","what-am-i-not-allowed-to-send-in-chat"]},{"u":"what-are-recommended-candidates","t":"What are Recommended Candidates?","c":8,"s":"Recommended Candidates","b":"<p><strong>AJobThing matches people from its database to your open roles</strong>, so you have options before your first application arrives &mdash; useful in the first days of an ad, and for roles where applications are slow.</p>\n<h2>Where to find them</h2>\n<p>Open <strong>Recommended Candidates</strong> in the side menu. It shows candidates matched to your active jobs and refreshes daily, with tabs for <strong>Recommended</strong>, <strong>New today</strong> and <strong>Unlocked</strong>.</p>\n<h2>What each card shows</h2>\n<p>The role it was matched for, the candidate's first name and initial, their current job, years of experience, location, when they can start (for example <em>Immediately</em> or <em>After 1 month</em>), their expected salary and their skills. That is enough to screen before spending anything &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile\">what is on a candidate's profile</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>The button says <em>Chat for 5 credits</em>.</strong> Credits go when you start the chat, not when the candidate accepts or replies &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged\">what a chat costs and when you are charged</a>.</div>\n<h2>What to do with them</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Check <strong>New today</strong> daily &mdash; the list refreshes, so a good match can pass you by.</li>\n<li>Read expected salary and start date before anything else; those rule people out fastest.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate\">Start a chat</a> only with people you would genuinely interview.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Every plan includes 2 candidate suggestions a day; how many you receive beyond that depends on your plan, so check your own package details in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">Billing &rarr; Packages</a>. If the matches are wide of the mark, see <a href=\"#/a/why-are-my-recommended-candidates-a-poor-match\">why your recommended candidates are a poor match</a>, or <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">search for candidates yourself</a>.</p>","v":[{"i":"809PSJWz9b8","t":"Candidates delivered to your inbox","d":"0:17","w":"The Recommended tab and the actions on each candidate card."}],"r":["what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged","how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself"]},{"u":"why-are-my-recommended-candidates-a-poor-match","t":"Why are my recommended candidates a poor match?","c":8,"s":"Recommended Candidates","b":"<p><strong>The matching uses what is on your job ad.</strong> If the ad is vague, the matches will be too &mdash; so the fix is almost always in the ad, not in the recommendations.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Add proper Required Skills.</strong> This is what the matching reads most directly &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-add-required-skills\">how to add required skills</a>. A short, accurate list works better than a long aspirational one.</li>\n<li><strong>Check Specialization is the right industry</strong> for the role rather than for your company &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/specialization-and-employment-type-how-do-i-pick\">how to pick Specialization and Employment Type</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Check Years of Experience and Minimum Education are realistic.</strong> Asking for more than the job needs pulls in people whose expectations you cannot meet.</li>\n<li><strong>Set a salary range that matches the level you want</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-set-the-salary\">how to set the salary</a>. This is the field that most often explains mismatched expectations.</li>\n<li><strong>Check Position Level agrees with the experience you asked for</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-position-level-and-accept-fresh-graduates\">Position Level and Accept Fresh Graduates</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Close roles you have already filled</strong>, so they stop generating matches.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Most of these fields can be corrected on a live ad &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-edit-a-live-job-ad\">how to edit a live job ad</a>. Give the matching a day or two to reflect the change before judging it again.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">If a role is filled, click <strong>Mark Filled / Hired</strong>. That stops the recommendations for it &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired\">how to mark a job as Filled / Hired</a>. Meanwhile you can target people directly with <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">Candidate Search</a>, which uses your search terms rather than the ad.</div>","v":[{"i":"809PSJWz9b8","t":"Candidates delivered to your inbox","d":"0:17","w":"The Recommended tab and the actions on each candidate card."}],"r":["how-do-i-add-required-skills","how-do-i-mark-a-job-as-filled-hired"]},{"u":"how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself","t":"How do I search for candidates myself?","c":8,"s":"Candidate Search","b":"<p><strong>Use Talent Discovery &rarr; Candidate Search.</strong> Searching and reading profiles costs nothing, so this is where to build a shortlist before spending any credits.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <strong>Talent Discovery &rarr; Candidate Search</strong>.</li>\n<li>Type a job title, and a location if you want.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Search</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What you get back</h2>\n<p>Each result shows the candidate's first name and initial, their current job title and location, with two buttons: <strong>Save to Project</strong> and <strong>Chat</strong>. Click a name to read the full free profile &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile\">what is on a candidate's profile</a>.</p>\n<p>The tabs at the top are <strong>Search</strong>, <strong>Unlocked</strong> (candidates you already have access to) and <strong>My Projects</strong>. Recent searches sit under the search box so you can run one again in a click.</p>\n<h2>A workflow that keeps costs down</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Search broadly, then read profiles and <strong>Save to Project</strong> everyone plausible &mdash; both are free; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">what a Project is</a>.</li>\n<li>Compare the saved shortlist on expected salary and last active date.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate\">Start chats</a> with the best few only.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Searching and reading profiles is free.</strong> Only starting a chat or requesting a resume costs credits &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate\">what it costs to contact a candidate</a>. Check <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-talent-pool\">My Talent Pool</a> first too; someone who applied to you before may already be there.</div>\n<p>Few or no results? See <a href=\"#/a/why-do-i-get-very-few-or-zero-search-results\">why you get very few or zero search results</a>.</p>","v":[{"i":"_Het3yDcw44","t":"Profile views and resume downloads","d":"0:22","w":"Reading the signals on a candidate card in Candidate Search."}],"r":["what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile","what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate"]},{"u":"what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile","t":"What is on a candidate's profile?","c":8,"s":"Candidate Search","b":"<p><strong>Click a name to open Profile Details.</strong> Most of what you need to judge someone is visible before you spend a single credit.</p>\n<h2>Free to read</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>First name and initial, gender, age, current job title and employer</li>\n<li>Preferred location, years of experience, education, expected salary, languages</li>\n<li><strong>Last Active</strong> &mdash; when they last used the site</li>\n<li>Skill tags, plus tabs for <strong>Profile Summary</strong>, <strong>Work Experience</strong>, <strong>Skills</strong>, <strong>Education</strong> and <strong>Languages</strong></li>\n<li>A <strong>Portfolio</strong> section where the candidate added one &mdash; images, videos, websites and social media</li>\n<li><strong>Similar Profiles</strong> down the side, so you can line up comparable people</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to read it quickly</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Last Active</strong> first &mdash; someone who has not used the site in a long time is unlikely to reply.</li>\n<li><strong>Expected salary</strong> next. If it is well outside your range, nothing else matters.</li>\n<li>Then <strong>Work Experience</strong>, to check the substance matches the job title.</li>\n<li><strong>Portfolio</strong> where there is one, for roles where the work speaks for itself.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Last Active and expected salary tell you fastest whether this person is worth contacting.</strong> Full name, resume and contact details come only through <a href=\"#/a/what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged\">Chat</a> once the candidate accepts &mdash; which is <a href=\"#/a/why-is-the-applicant-s-name-partly-hidden\">why the name is partly hidden</a>.</div>\n<p>Use <strong>Save to Project</strong> to keep anyone promising while you compare &mdash; it is free; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">what a Project is</a>. Costs are set out in <a href=\"#/a/what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate\">what it costs to contact a candidate</a>.</p>","v":[{"i":"_Het3yDcw44","t":"Profile views and resume downloads","d":"0:22","w":"Reading the signals on a candidate card in Candidate Search."},{"i":"OC8j8DKu7TA","t":"Check portfolios and endorsements","d":"0:20","w":"The Portfolio section inside a candidate's Profile Details."}],"r":["what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate","how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself","why-do-i-get-very-few-or-zero-search-results"]},{"u":"what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate","t":"What does it cost to contact a candidate?","c":8,"s":"Candidate Search","b":"<p><strong>Contacting a candidate costs 5 credits; everything up to that point is free.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Start a chat with a candidate</td><td>5 credits</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Request Resume</strong> on a profile</td><td>5 credits</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Search, read a profile, Save to Project</td><td>Free</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>How you get their details: \"Start a chat for 5 credits, and once the candidate accepts, you'll be able to view their details directly in the chat.\" So a chat is a request the candidate can decline, not a purchase of contact information.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Credits go when you start the chat</strong> &mdash; even if the candidate declines or never replies. There is no refund for an unanswered chat, which is why screening first is worth the time.</div>\n<h2>How to spend fewer credits per hire</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Read the free profile fields, especially <strong>Last Active</strong> and expected salary &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile\">what is on a candidate's profile</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Save to Project</strong> while you compare, which costs nothing &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">what a Project is</a>.</li>\n<li>Check <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-talent-pool\">My Talent Pool</a> for someone you already have access to.</li>\n<li>Then start chats with the best few, writing a first message that earns a reply &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate\">how to start a new chat with a candidate</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Chats with people who applied to your own ad work differently &mdash; those use your monthly chat allowance; see <a href=\"#/a/what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged\">what a chat costs, and when am I charged</a> and <a href=\"#/a/what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on\">what credits can be spent on</a>.</p>","r":["what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on","what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile"]},{"u":"why-do-i-get-very-few-or-zero-search-results","t":"Why do I get very few or zero search results?","c":8,"s":"Candidate Search","b":"<p><strong>Almost always the search terms are too narrow, not the database too small.</strong> Work through these in order.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Use a common job title.</strong> Search \"Account Executive\", not your internal job grade &mdash; candidates describe themselves in market terms.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove the location</strong> and search Malaysia-wide first, then narrow down. A district filter on a small town can empty a good list.</li>\n<li><strong>Try a related title.</strong> \"Admin Assistant\" and \"Admin Clerk\" return different people, and so do \"Storekeeper\" and \"Warehouse Assistant\". Run two or three variants.</li>\n<li><strong>Check your spelling</strong>, including the English/Malay spelling you used.</li>\n<li><strong>Drop to the core word.</strong> \"Technician\" finds people that \"Senior Maintenance Technician (Mechanical)\" never will.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\">Open a candidate you like and look at <strong>Similar Profiles</strong> beside their details. It is often faster than another search &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile\">what is on a candidate's profile</a>.</div>\n<h2>If the pool is genuinely thin</h2>\n<p>Some roles and locations simply have few active candidates. In that case, let the job ad do the work alongside your searching: check <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">why a job ad is not getting applicants</a>, consider <a href=\"#/a/what-is-boost-ads\">Boost Ads</a> for more visibility, and for high-volume or walk-in style hiring look at <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-walk-in-campaign\">a Walk-In campaign</a>. Save what you do find into a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a> with <strong>AI Daily Recommendation</strong> on, so matching people are added over time instead of you repeating the same search.</p>\n<p>Searching costs nothing, so try several angles freely &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">how to search for candidates yourself</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself","what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile","what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate"]},{"u":"how-do-i-ask-a-candidate-for-their-resume","t":"How do I ask a candidate for their resume?","c":8,"s":"Candidate Search","b":"<p><strong>Open the candidate's Profile Details and click Request Resume.</strong> It costs <strong>5 credits</strong>, the same as starting a chat.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Find the candidate in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">Candidate Search</a> or <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended Candidates</a>.</li>\n<li>Click their name to open <strong>Profile Details</strong> and read the free sections first.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Request Resume</strong>.</li>\n<li>Watch your <a href=\"#/a/what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message\">Chat</a> inbox &mdash; resumes and contact details come through Chat, not by email.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What to expect</h2>\n<p>The candidate has to respond before you receive anything, so a request is not a guaranteed resume, and the credits are spent when you send it. Someone whose <strong>Last Active</strong> date is recent is far more likely to reply &mdash; check that before requesting. If nothing comes back after a few days, treat it as a no rather than requesting again.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Read the free part of the profile first.</strong> Profile Summary, Work Experience, Skills and Education are all visible before you spend anything &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile\">what is on a candidate's profile</a>. For many decisions that is enough, and starting a <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate\">chat</a> instead lets you ask your own questions for the same 5 credits.</div>\n<p>Costs and what else uses credits are set out in <a href=\"#/a/what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate\">what it costs to contact a candidate</a> and <a href=\"#/a/what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on\">what credits can be spent on</a>. Any resume you receive is candidate data you are responsible for &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a>.</p>","r":["what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate","how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself","what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile"]},{"u":"what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one","t":"What is a Project, and how do I use one?","c":8,"s":"Projects & Talent Pool","b":"<p><strong>A Project is a folder of saved candidates &mdash; one per role you are hiring.</strong> Saving is free, so it is where a shortlist should live before you spend credits.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <strong>Talent Discovery &rarr; My Projects</strong>.</li>\n<li>When you find someone in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">Candidate Search</a>, click <strong>Save to Project</strong>.</li>\n<li>Turn on <strong>AI Daily Recommendation</strong> on a project to have AJobThing keep adding matching people.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each project shows when it was created, how many candidates AI recommended and how many you saved yourself &mdash; so you can see whether the automatic additions are worth reviewing.</p>\n<h2>How to get the most out of them</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One project per role</strong>, named for the role, so a colleague can pick it up.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the runners-up.</strong> After you hire, the people you did not choose are your head start on the next opening &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/i-have-hired-someone-what-now\">I have hired someone, what now</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Leave AI Daily Recommendation on for roles you hire repeatedly</strong>, and review the additions weekly rather than searching again from scratch.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Saving is free.</strong> Build the shortlist first, then spend credits contacting only the best few &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate\">what it costs to contact a candidate</a>.</div>\n<p>A Project holds candidates you picked out; <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-talent-pool\">My Talent Pool</a> collects people you have already had contact with. Check both before paying to reach anyone new.</p>","r":["what-is-my-talent-pool","what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate"]},{"u":"what-is-my-talent-pool","t":"What is My Talent Pool?","c":8,"s":"Projects & Talent Pool","b":"<p><strong>My Talent Pool holds candidates you have already had contact with</strong>, kept in one place so you can find them again for your next role &mdash; without paying to reach them a second time.</p>\n<h2>How people get there</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Resumes sent to you through your Company Page, when <strong>Free Resume</strong> is on &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-get-free-resumes-from-my-company-page\">how to get free resumes from your Company Page</a>.</li>\n<li>Applicants from your job ads &mdash; they stay after the ad ends; see <a href=\"#/a/how-long-do-i-keep-access-to-applicants-after-a-job-expires\">how long you keep access to applicants</a>.</li>\n<li>Candidates whose profiles you unlocked &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-does-unlocking-a-candidate-profile-give-me\">what unlocking a candidate profile gives you</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Search your Talent Pool before you open <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">Candidate Search</a> for a new role.</li>\n<li>Check whether the person is still within an active unlock &mdash; access to unlocked details runs on <a href=\"#/a/how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last\">a 90 day clock</a>.</li>\n<li>Move anyone you are actively considering into a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a> for that role.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Check here before you buy new profiles.</strong> Your next hire may already be someone who applied to you last year &mdash; and reaching them again is the cheapest sourcing you will do.</div>\n<p>These are real people's details, so the retention rules apply to your Talent Pool as much as to a live application: see <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a>, which requires you to delete or anonymise data once your recruitment purpose is complete.</p>","v":[{"i":"DEzZG_5UImk","t":"Get free resumes from your Company Page","d":"0:27","w":"The Free Resume toggle on the Company Profile page."}],"r":["how-do-i-get-free-resumes-from-my-company-page","what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one"]},{"u":"what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on","t":"What are credits and what can I spend them on?","c":9,"s":"Credits","b":"<p><strong>Credits let you unlock applicant profiles and start conversations.</strong> They are separate from your job ads, and they are spent in units of 5.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Start a chat with a candidate from <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">Candidate Search</a></td><td>5 credits</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Start a chat with a <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended Candidate</a></td><td>5 credits</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Request Resume</strong> on a profile</td><td>5 credits</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chat with an extra applicant after your monthly chats run out</td><td>5 credits</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>What is free</h2>\n<p>Searching, opening a profile, reading work experience and education, <strong>Save to Project</strong>, saving a draft, previewing an ad, and editing your <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">Company Page</a>. So all the screening happens before any credit is spent &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile\">what is on a candidate's profile</a>.</p>\n<h2>How to make them last</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Shortlist for free in a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a> before contacting anyone.</li>\n<li>Use your monthly chat allowance on your own applicants first &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged\">what a chat costs and when you are charged</a>.</li>\n<li>Watch expiry dates, since batches lapse &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/do-my-credits-expire\">do my credits expire</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Credits and coins are the same balance under two names; job ads and candidate profiles are different balances entirely &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/credits-coins-job-ads-and-candidate-profiles-what-is-the-difference\">the difference between them</a>. Check your own balance and what you hold in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">credit balance and history</a>, and see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-get-more-credits\">how to get more credits</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-get-more-credits","where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history"]},{"u":"how-do-i-get-more-credits","t":"How do I get more credits?","c":9,"s":"Credits","b":"<p><strong>Some credits come free with things you are doing anyway; the rest you buy.</strong> Clear the free ones first.</p>\n<h2>Free ways</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Post a job ad.</strong> Every job ad comes with <strong>10 free credits</strong>, valid for 30 days from posting &mdash; so use them within that window; see <a href=\"#/a/do-my-credits-expire\">do my credits expire</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Answer your Company FAQ.</strong> Each published answer earns <strong>+2 credits</strong>, and nine questions is 18 credits &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-answer-the-questions-jobseekers-ask\">how to answer the questions jobseekers ask</a>. This also improves the page candidates read before applying.</li>\n<li><strong>Claim rewards</strong> when they appear on your Dashboard.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Paid</h2>\n<p>You can buy credits separately. Ask your Account Manager or use <strong>Get Free Quotation</strong> under Shop &mdash; prices and bundle sizes are shown there and depend on what your account is offered, so take the figures from the quotation rather than assuming.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Do the FAQ answers this week.</strong> They are the only credits you can earn without posting or paying, they take a few minutes each, and they make your <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">Company Page</a> more useful at the same time &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-is-the-profile-on-my-dashboard\">your Profile %</a>.</div>\n<p>Confirm anything you earn or buy has landed in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">Billing &rarr; Credit History</a>, and see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on\">what credits can be spent on</a> before you decide how many you need. If you are buying candidate profiles rather than credits, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-the-candidate-profile-packages-compare\">the candidate profile packages</a> are a different purchase.</p>","r":["how-do-i-answer-the-questions-jobseekers-ask","what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on"]},{"u":"where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history","t":"Where do I see my credit balance and history?","c":9,"s":"Credits","b":"<p><strong>Your balance is in the top bar; the full history is in Billing.</strong> Three views, each answering a different question.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Balance</strong> &mdash; the number beside the coin icon in the top bar. Click it to open <strong>Purchased Credits Details</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Each batch</strong> &mdash; that window lists the package name, remaining credits, purchase date and expiry date, and flags anything <strong>(Expiring soon)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Every transaction</strong> &mdash; <strong>Billing &rarr; Credit History</strong>, showing Date, User Name, Item, Credits and Job.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to use each one</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Check the <strong>top bar</strong> before starting chats, so you are not stopped halfway through a shortlist.</li>\n<li>Open <strong>Purchased Credits Details</strong> weekly to spot batches marked <strong>(Expiring soon)</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/do-my-credits-expire\">do my credits expire</a>.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Credit History</strong> when a balance looks wrong: the <strong>Item</strong> and <strong>Job</strong> columns show exactly what each spend went on.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>The User Name column matters.</strong> It shows who spent what &mdash; which is one reason to give each colleague their own login instead of sharing one; see <a href=\"#/a/can-two-people-share-one-login\">can two people share one login</a> and <a href=\"#/a/are-credits-shared-with-my-team\">are credits shared with my team</a>.</div>\n<p>Credits are only one of your balances &mdash; job ads and candidate profiles are tracked separately in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">Billing &rarr; Packages</a>; see <a href=\"#/a/credits-coins-job-ads-and-candidate-profiles-what-is-the-difference\">the difference between them</a>. If a figure here disagrees with another screen, check <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-numbers-on-my-dashboard-mean\">numbers on different screens don't match</a> before reporting it.</p>","r":["do-my-credits-expire","what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on","how-do-i-get-more-credits"]},{"u":"do-my-credits-expire","t":"Do my credits expire?","c":9,"s":"Credits","b":"<p><strong>Yes &mdash; each batch has its own expiry date.</strong> Credits are not one pool with one deadline, so it is worth knowing which batch is closest to lapsing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>10 free credits</strong> that come with a job ad are valid for <strong>30 days</strong> from posting.</li>\n<li>Purchased batches carry their own expiry date, shown in <strong>Purchased Credits Details</strong>.</li>\n<li>Batches close to expiring are marked <strong>(Expiring soon)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AJobThing emails you 7 days before credits expire, so make sure the address on your account is one you read &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-change-my-name-email-contact-number-or-password\">how to change your email</a> and <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get\">how to choose which alerts you get</a>.</p>\n<h2>What to do when a batch is expiring</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\"><strong>Purchased Credits Details</strong></a> and note which batch is closest to its date.</li>\n<li>Review the shortlist you already have in a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a> or <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-talent-pool\">Talent Pool</a>.</li>\n<li>Spend on candidates you were going to contact anyway &mdash; not on people you have not screened.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Expired credits are gone.</strong> There is no extension for an unused batch, so treat the free credits that arrive with a job ad as part of that hiring push rather than saving them for later.</div>\n<p>Candidate profile packages have their own separate clocks, which work differently &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-s-the-difference-between-unlock-expiry-and-package-validity\">the difference between unlock expiry and package validity</a>. For your other balances, see <a href=\"#/a/when-does-my-package-expire-and-what-happens-to-unused-job-ads\">when your package expires</a>.</p>","r":["where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history","what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on","how-do-i-get-more-credits"]},{"u":"credits-coins-job-ads-and-candidate-profiles-what-is-the-difference","t":"Credits, coins, job ads and candidate profiles — what is the difference?","c":9,"s":"Credits","b":"<p><strong>You hold several separate balances, and they cannot be swapped for each other.</strong> This table is the quickest way to tell them apart.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Balance</th><th>What it is for</th><th>Where to see it</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Credits</strong></td><td>Starting chats and requesting resumes, 5 at a time</td><td>Coin in the top bar</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Coins</strong></td><td>The same thing as credits, different word</td><td>Dashboard FAQ card</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Job ads</strong></td><td>Paid ads you can have live</td><td>JOB AD BALANCE on Packages</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Free job ads</strong></td><td>Only the <a href=\"#/a/which-job-ads-are-free\">six free job types</a></td><td>FREE JOB ADS on Packages</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Candidate profiles</strong></td><td><a href=\"#/a/what-does-unlocking-a-candidate-profile-give-me\">Unlocking full candidate profiles</a></td><td>CANDIDATE PROFILES on Packages</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Chats</strong></td><td>Monthly conversations with applicants</td><td>Your plan</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Why it matters in practice</h2>\n<p>Running out of one balance does not stop the others. You can have job ads left but no credits &mdash; in which case you can keep posting but cannot start new chats with sourced candidates. Equally, credits will not buy you another job ad. When something you expected to work is unavailable, check which balance the action actually draws on.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Credits and coins are one balance under two names.</strong> Job ads and candidate profiles are separate and cannot be swapped. See them together in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">Billing &rarr; Packages</a>, with credits in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">Credit History</a>.</div>\n<p>Each balance also has its own expiry behaviour &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/do-my-credits-expire\">do my credits expire</a> and <a href=\"#/a/when-does-my-package-expire-and-what-happens-to-unused-job-ads\">when your package expires</a>.</p>","r":["what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on","where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances"]},{"u":"what-does-unlocking-a-candidate-profile-give-me","t":"What does unlocking a candidate profile give me?","c":9,"s":"Unlocking Profiles","b":"<p><strong>Unlocking gives you the candidate's resume and contact details, plus the ability to chat with them and use Invite to Apply.</strong> Before that, you see the profile without identifying details &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/why-is-the-applicant-s-name-partly-hidden\">why the name is partly hidden</a>.</p>\n<h2>What every candidate profile package includes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Unlock from <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">Candidate Search</a></li>\n<li>Unlock from <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended Profiles</a></li>\n<li>Resume and contact details</li>\n<li><a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-talent-pool\">My Talent Pool</a> access</li>\n<li>Chat access</li>\n<li>Invite to Apply</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to do once a profile is unlocked</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Read the resume against the role before contacting them, so your first message is specific.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Invite to Apply</strong> or <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate\">start a chat</a> while their interest is fresh.</li>\n<li>Save what you need into your own records &mdash; access is not permanent.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Access runs on a clock.</strong> Every package lists a <strong>90 Days Profile Unlock Expiry</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last\">how long an unlocked profile lasts</a>, and <a href=\"#/a/what-s-the-difference-between-unlock-expiry-and-package-validity\">the difference between unlock expiry and package validity</a>, which is a separate deadline.</div>\n<p>Unlocking is paid from your candidate profile balance rather than credits &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/credits-coins-job-ads-and-candidate-profiles-what-is-the-difference\">the difference between the balances</a> and <a href=\"#/a/how-do-the-candidate-profile-packages-compare\">how the packages compare</a>. Once you hold someone's details, the rules in <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a> apply.</p>","r":["how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last","how-do-the-candidate-profile-packages-compare"]},{"u":"how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last","t":"How long does an unlocked profile last?","c":9,"s":"Unlocking Profiles","b":"<p><strong>90 days.</strong> Every candidate profile package lists a <strong>90 Days Profile Unlock Expiry</strong>, counted from when you unlock that person &mdash; so each candidate has their own end date.</p>\n<h2>What to do while access is live</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Contact the candidate early rather than late &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate\">how to start a new chat</a>.</li>\n<li>Move the details you genuinely need for hiring into your own HR records.</li>\n<li>Note where they sit in your process, using an <a href=\"#/a/can-i-add-private-notes-about-an-applicant\">Internal Comment</a> or a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>Do not treat an unlock as permanent.</strong> Save what you need into your own records while access is live, and check your unlock dates in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">Billing &rarr; Packages</a> rather than assuming there is time left.</div>\n<p>The 90 days is separate from how long you have to <em>use</em> the profiles you bought &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-s-the-difference-between-unlock-expiry-and-package-validity\">the difference between unlock expiry and package validity</a>, which is the distinction that catches people out at purchase time.</p>\n<p>Keeping data is not simply your choice: AJobThing's Terms require you to delete or anonymise candidate data once your recruitment purpose is finished, unless the law requires you to keep it. See <a href=\"#/a/what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details\">what you can do with a candidate's details</a>, and take your own advice on what your company must retain.</p>","r":["what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details","what-s-the-difference-between-unlock-expiry-and-package-validity"]},{"u":"what-s-the-difference-between-unlock-expiry-and-package-validity","t":"What's the difference between unlock expiry and package validity?","c":9,"s":"Unlocking Profiles","b":"<p><strong>Two different clocks &mdash; one on the package you bought, one on each person you unlock.</strong> Mixing them up is what leads to buying the wrong pack.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Package Validity</strong> &mdash; how long you have to <em>use</em> the profiles you bought. 10 days on the 20-profile pack, 3 months on the 80, 6 months on the 320.</li>\n<li><strong>Profile Unlock Expiry</strong> &mdash; how long access lasts <em>after</em> you unlock someone. 90 days on every package &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last\">how long an unlocked profile lasts</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>This matters when you buy.</strong> 20 profiles in 10 days is two a day. If your hiring is slower than that, the bigger pack is cheaper per profile you actually use &mdash; compare them in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-the-candidate-profile-packages-compare\">how the candidate profile packages compare</a>.</div>\n<h2>How to plan around both</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Estimate how many profiles you will realistically unlock per week, not per month.</li>\n<li>Pick the pack whose validity window matches that pace.</li>\n<li>Once unlocked, work each candidate within their 90 days &mdash; the package validity no longer applies to them.</li>\n<li>Check both on <strong>Billing &rarr; Packages</strong>: each card shows <strong>REMAINING</strong>, <strong>EXPIRY</strong> and a <strong>STATUS</strong> countdown &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">where you see your packages and balances</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Credits work on yet another schedule, batch by batch &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/do-my-credits-expire\">do my credits expire</a> &mdash; and job ad packages on their own; see <a href=\"#/a/when-does-my-package-expire-and-what-happens-to-unused-job-ads\">when your package expires</a>. If you are unsure which validity your account holds, take it from your package card rather than an assumption.</p>","r":["how-do-the-candidate-profile-packages-compare","what-does-unlocking-a-candidate-profile-give-me","how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last"]},{"u":"where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances","t":"Where do I see my packages and balances?","c":9,"s":"What You Have","b":"<p>Your packages and balances live under <strong>Billing &rarr; Packages</strong>, split into two tabs: <strong>Active Packages</strong> and <strong>Subscriptions</strong>.</p> <h2>Three balance cards</h2> <ul> <li><strong>JOB AD BALANCE</strong> &mdash; paid job ads you can have live</li> <li><strong>FREE JOB ADS</strong> &mdash; only for the six free job types</li> <li><strong>CANDIDATE PROFILES</strong> &mdash; for unlocking full profiles</li> </ul> <p>Each card tracks its own <strong>Next Expiry</strong> separately, and a balance on one card cannot be swapped into another &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-s-the-difference-between-active-packages-and-subscriptions\">what separates Active Packages from Subscriptions</a> if you are not sure which tab to check.</p> <p>Every package card shows <strong>REMAINING</strong> as a fraction (for example 42/120), an <strong>EXPIRY</strong> date and a <strong>STATUS</strong> countdown such as \"62 D Left\" &mdash; worth checking regularly, since <a href=\"#/a/when-does-my-package-expire-and-what-happens-to-unused-job-ads\">unused job ads do not roll over once a package expires</a>.</p> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">TIP</strong> Hover over <strong>How it works?</strong> on any card for a breakdown of your quota, active ads and available ads. If FREE JOB ADS shows zero, check <a href=\"#/a/which-job-ads-are-free\">which job ads qualify as free</a> before assuming you have to spend a paid ad.</div>","r":["when-does-my-package-expire-and-what-happens-to-unused-job-ads","what-s-the-difference-between-active-packages-and-subscriptions"]},{"u":"what-s-the-difference-between-active-packages-and-subscriptions","t":"What's the difference between Active Packages and Subscriptions?","c":9,"s":"What You Have","b":"<p><strong>Active Packages</strong> are what you have already bought and can still use, each shown with REMAINING, EXPIRY and STATUS. <strong>Subscriptions</strong> are anything that renews on its own and charges your card on a cycle. Both tabs sit under <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">Billing &rarr; Packages</a>.</p> <h2>What a subscription card shows</h2> <p>Its name and status, when it started, the price and billing frequency, the current billing period, <strong>NEXT CHARGE</strong>, <strong>CARD ON FILE</strong> (brand and last four digits) and <strong>LAST CHARGE</strong>. An Active Package card carries none of this &mdash; it only tells you what is left to use, not what will be billed next, which is why it helps to know <a href=\"#/a/how-do-subscriptions-renew\">how subscriptions renew</a> if you are on one.</p> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">CHECK THIS BEFORE A RENEWAL</strong> NEXT CHARGE tells you the date; CARD ON FILE tells you whether the card is still right. If you want to change plan before that date, see <a href=\"#/a/can-i-upgrade-or-downgrade-later\">how to upgrade or downgrade</a>.</div>","r":["how-do-subscriptions-renew","where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances","when-does-my-package-expire-and-what-happens-to-unused-job-ads"]},{"u":"when-does-my-package-expire-and-what-happens-to-unused-job-ads","t":"When does my package expire, and what happens to unused job ads?","c":9,"s":"What You Have","b":"<p><strong>Every package expires on its own EXPIRY date</strong>, shown with a STATUS countdown such as \"358 D Left\" on <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">your Packages page</a>.</p> <h2>Read EXPIRY together with REMAINING</h2> <p>Whichever runs out first is what stops you from posting or unlocking. A package showing 0/3 is finished even with 24 days still left on it. One showing 42/120 with only 2 days left will lose the unused 42 the moment it expires &mdash; the countdown does not pause just because a balance remains.</p> <div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">UNUSED JOB ADS DO NOT ROLL OVER AUTOMATICALLY</strong> If a package is close to expiring and you still have ads sitting on it, use them before the date or speak to your Account Manager. This matters most if you hold more than one package &mdash; check <a href=\"#/a/which-package-gets-used-when-i-post-a-job\">which package gets used first when you post a job</a> so the one expiring soonest does not go to waste. For every other deadline in one place, see <a href=\"#/a/every-deadline-and-expiry-in-one-table\">the full table of deadlines and expiries</a>.</div>","r":["where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances","what-s-the-difference-between-active-packages-and-subscriptions","which-package-gets-used-when-i-post-a-job"]},{"u":"which-package-gets-used-when-i-post-a-job","t":"Which package gets used when I post a job?","c":9,"s":"What You Have","b":"<p><strong>The job card itself tells you which package was used</strong> &mdash; the <a href=\"#/a/what-are-the-coloured-tags-on-my-job-cards\">coloured tag on the card</a> names the package, so you do not need to open the Packages page just to check.</p> <p>A job that qualifies as free comes out of your <strong>FREE JOB ADS</strong> balance &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/which-job-ads-are-free\">which job ads are free</a> if you are not sure whether yours counts. Every other job ad draws down a paid package instead.</p> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">IF YOU HAVE SEVERAL PACKAGES</strong> Ask your Account Manager which one is drawn down first. Knowing the order lets you post deliberately from the package <a href=\"#/a/when-does-my-package-expire-and-what-happens-to-unused-job-ads\">closest to expiring</a>, instead of watching it lapse with ads still unused.</div>","r":["what-are-the-coloured-tags-on-my-job-cards","which-job-ads-are-free"]},{"u":"which-plan-is-right-for-me","t":"Which plan is right for me?","c":9,"s":"Choosing A Plan","b":"<p><strong>Plans are sized by how many people you hire each month</strong> &mdash; pick the one closest to your typical hiring volume, not your busiest month.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Built for</th><th>Chats/month</th><th>Job ads/month</th><th>Free similar ads</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td><strong>Starter Hire</strong></td><td>1&ndash;2 hires a month</td><td>100</td><td>4</td><td>16</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>Growth Hire</strong> (most popular)</td><td>3&ndash;6 hires a month</td><td>300</td><td>10</td><td>40</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>Pro Hire</strong></td><td>7&ndash;14 hires a month</td><td>700</td><td>20</td><td>80</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>Custom Package</strong></td><td>15+ hires a month</td><td colspan=\"2\">Set for your team</td><td>Dedicated account manager</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Job ads on every plan are auto-reposted, and each plan carries the same set of features beyond the numbers above &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-do-i-get-in-every-plan\">what you get in every plan</a> for the full list.</p> <h2>Current prices</h2> <p>Prices are shown in <strong>Shop &rarr; Buy Job Ads</strong> in your dashboard, with a monthly and an annual option &mdash; annual works out cheaper per month. For a written quote, use <strong>Get Free Quotation</strong> or email <strong>sales@ajobthing.com</strong>.</p> <div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">PICK THE PLAN CLOSEST TO A NORMAL MONTH</strong> Unused chats do not carry forward to the next month, so oversizing your plan wastes them. You <a href=\"#/a/can-i-upgrade-or-downgrade-later\">can move up to a bigger plan any time</a> if your hiring grows.</div>","r":["what-do-i-get-in-every-plan","can-i-upgrade-or-downgrade-later"]},{"u":"what-do-i-get-in-every-plan","t":"What do I get in every plan?","c":9,"s":"Choosing A Plan","b":"<p><strong>The core features are the same across Starter, Growth and Pro</strong> &mdash; the numbers differ by plan, but what is included does not.</p> <ul> <li>Your chats reset every month on your renewal date</li> <li>Your job ads get reposted for you automatically</li> <li><strong>10 free credits for every job ad</strong> &mdash; usable within 30 days of posting; see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on\">what credits can be spent on</a> if you are not sure what this covers</li> <li>2 candidate suggestions every day</li> <li>Posting across <a href=\"https://www.maukerja.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Maukerja</a>, <a href=\"https://www.ricebowl.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ricebowl</a>, EpiCareer and partner job sites</li> <li>Your logo and company profile shown on every ad</li> <li>All applicants in one place</li> <li>Advice if your ad is quiet</li> <li>Support on WhatsApp, email, phone or live chat</li> <li><a href=\"#/a/what-is-ajobthing-care\">AJT Care</a></li> <li>Extra applicants at 5 credits each once your monthly chats run out</li> </ul> <p>An optional paid add-on &mdash; a monthly job ad boost &mdash; sits on top of any plan. Ask your Account Manager for the price on your specific plan before you add it, and see <a href=\"#/a/which-plan-is-right-for-me\">how the plans compare</a> if you are still deciding which one to start on.</p>","r":["which-plan-is-right-for-me","what-is-ajobthing-care"]},{"u":"what-is-ajobthing-care","t":"What is AJobThing Care?","c":9,"s":"Choosing A Plan","b":"<p><strong>AJobThing Care is the after-sales support bundled into every hiring plan</strong> &mdash; AJobThing describes it as \"dedicated after-sales support, hiring guidance, and personalized assistance,\" part of what you get alongside the numbers in <a href=\"#/a/what-do-i-get-in-every-plan\">what every plan includes</a>.</p> <p>AJobThing's own wording on the guarantee: \"Post your job ad with AJobThing Care and get guaranteed hiring success &mdash; or we replace your ad free.\"</p> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">USE IT BEFORE YOU REPOST</strong> If a job ad is quiet, talk to your Account Manager about <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants\">why it is not getting applicants</a> before reposting it yourself &mdash; reposting spends another job ad and loses the applicants already on the old one.</div> <p>Because \"guaranteed\" and \"replace free\" can mean different things depending on your package, ask AJobThing to confirm the exact replacement conditions that apply to you before you rely on the guarantee.</p>","v":[{"i":"BxNdFKCbwJM","t":"AJobThing Care","d":"2:00","w":"What AJobThing Care covers."}],"r":["what-do-i-get-in-every-plan","why-is-my-job-ad-not-getting-applicants"]},{"u":"how-do-the-candidate-profile-packages-compare","t":"How do the candidate profile packages compare?","c":9,"s":"Choosing A Plan","b":"<p><strong>The three fixed candidate profile packages trade off price per profile against how long you have to use it.</strong></p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Profiles</th><th>Package validity</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>AI Candidate Search 20</td><td>20</td><td>10 days</td></tr> <tr><td>AI Candidate Search 80</td><td>80</td><td>3 months</td></tr> <tr><td>AI Candidate Search 320</td><td>320</td><td>6 months</td></tr> <tr><td>Custom</td><td>Custom</td><td>Custom</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>The bigger the pack, the less each profile costs and the longer the package validity lasts. Every package includes unlocking from Candidate Search and Recommended Profiles, resume and contact details, <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-talent-pool\">My Talent Pool</a>, Chat, Invite to Apply, and a 90-day profile unlock expiry &mdash; a separate clock from package validity, explained in <a href=\"#/a/what-s-the-difference-between-unlock-expiry-and-package-validity\">unlock expiry versus package validity</a>.</p> <div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">TWO THINGS BEFORE YOU BUY</strong> Prices <a href=\"#/a/is-8-sst-added-to-the-price\">exclude 8% SST</a>, and package validity is only 10 days on the smallest pack &mdash; check you can realistically use 20 profiles in that window.</div> <p>Current prices are on <strong>Shop &rarr; Buy Candidate Profiles</strong> in your dashboard, or ask for a written quote with <strong>Get Free Quotation</strong>.</p>","r":["what-s-the-difference-between-unlock-expiry-and-package-validity","is-8-sst-added-to-the-price"]},{"u":"can-i-upgrade-or-downgrade-later","t":"Can I upgrade or downgrade later?","c":9,"s":"Choosing A Plan","b":"<p><strong>Yes &mdash; you can move up any time, and move down at your next renewal.</strong> AJobThing's rule: \"You can move up whenever your hiring grows. If you move down, the change starts at your next renewal.\"</p> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>To upgrade, pick a bigger plan in <strong>Shop &rarr; Buy Job Ads</strong>, or ask your Account Manager &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/which-plan-is-right-for-me\">how the plans compare</a> if you have not decided which one.</li> <li>To downgrade, tell your Account Manager <em>before</em> your renewal date, since the change only takes effect at the next cycle, not immediately.</li> <li>Find that date under <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">Billing &rarr; Packages</a> &rarr; <strong>Subscriptions</strong>, on the <strong>NEXT CHARGE</strong> line &mdash; the same tab that shows how <a href=\"#/a/how-do-subscriptions-renew\">subscriptions renew</a>.</li> </ol>","r":["what-s-the-difference-between-active-packages-and-subscriptions","which-plan-is-right-for-me","what-do-i-get-in-every-plan"]},{"u":"is-8-sst-added-to-the-price","t":"Is 8% SST added to the price?","c":9,"s":"Payment & Invoices","b":"<p><strong>Yes, on candidate profile packages</strong> &mdash; the pricing page carries the line <strong>*Excludes 8% SST</strong> under the prices shown, meaning 8% is added on top of what you see.</p> <p>This applies to the <a href=\"#/a/how-do-the-candidate-profile-packages-compare\">candidate profile packages</a> specifically, since that is where the source states it; for other purchases, check the price you are shown for the same kind of note before assuming SST is or is not included.</p> <p>For the exact figure your finance team needs, download the <strong>Invoice</strong> or <strong>Receipt</strong> from <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-download-my-invoice-or-receipt\">Billing &rarr; Payment History</a>, or ask for a proforma invoice before you buy so there are no surprises on the final amount.</p>","r":["how-do-i-download-my-invoice-or-receipt","how-do-i-change-the-billing-details-on-my-invoice","my-payment-failed-or-nothing-appeared-was-i-charged"]},{"u":"how-do-i-download-my-invoice-or-receipt","t":"How do I download my invoice or receipt?","c":9,"s":"Payment & Invoices","b":"<p><strong>Open Billing &rarr; Payment History</strong> &mdash; every invoice and receipt you have generated is listed there.</p> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Go to <strong>Billing &rarr; Payment History</strong>.</li> <li>Find the row for the purchase, showing its Date, User Name, Item, Cost and Type.</li> <li>Download the document you need from the three columns: <strong>Proforma Invoice</strong> (for finance to raise payment before you buy), <strong>Invoice</strong> (the tax invoice) or <strong>Receipt</strong> (proof of payment).</li> </ol> <div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">SET YOUR BILLING ADDRESS FIRST</strong> It is pulled from <strong>Settings &rarr; Billing Address</strong>. Fix it before you download &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-change-the-billing-details-on-my-invoice\">how to change the billing details on your invoice</a> &mdash; or finance will send the document back.</div> <p>Need to know whether the amount includes tax? Check <a href=\"#/a/is-8-sst-added-to-the-price\">whether 8% SST is added to the price</a> before you pass the invoice on.</p>","r":["how-do-i-change-the-billing-details-on-my-invoice","is-8-sst-added-to-the-price","my-payment-failed-or-nothing-appeared-was-i-charged"]},{"u":"how-do-i-change-the-billing-details-on-my-invoice","t":"How do I change the billing details on my invoice?","c":9,"s":"Payment & Invoices","b":"<p><strong>Update it under Company Branding &rarr; Settings, in the Billing Address section.</strong> The address stored there is what appears on your invoices and receipts.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <strong>Company Branding &rarr; Settings</strong> &mdash; the same place covered in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get\">what is on the Settings page</a>.</li>\n<li>Under <strong>Billing Address</strong>, either tick <strong>Billing address is same as Company Address</strong> or type in a different address.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Save</strong>. Each block on that page saves separately, so save the one you edited.</li>\n<li>Go back to <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-download-my-invoice-or-receipt\">Payment History</a> and download the invoice again &mdash; it will now carry the updated address.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What to check with your finance team</h2>\n<p>Confirm the registered entity name, address and any tax reference they need on the document before your next purchase, so the invoice is right first time. Your company name itself cannot be changed by you &mdash; that one needs AJobThing support; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-edit-my-company-details-and-what-can-t-i-change\">how to edit your company details, and what you can't change</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">DO THIS BEFORE YOU BUY</strong> so the first invoice you ever download is already correct, instead of needing a reissue. If a document you have already downloaded shows the wrong details, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">contact AJobThing</a> rather than editing it yourself.</div>","r":["how-do-i-download-my-invoice-or-receipt","is-8-sst-added-to-the-price","my-payment-failed-or-nothing-appeared-was-i-charged"]},{"u":"my-payment-failed-or-nothing-appeared-was-i-charged","t":"My payment failed or nothing appeared — was I charged?","c":9,"s":"Payment & Invoices","b":"<p><strong>First work out where it went wrong</strong> &mdash; the fix depends on which of two things happened.</p> <ul> <li><strong>An error appeared on the payment gateway screen</strong> &mdash; that is between you and your bank; contact them.</li> <li><strong>Payment seemed to go through but no credits or job ads appeared</strong> in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">your packages and balances</a> &mdash; contact AJobThing.</li> </ul> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Screenshot the error and note the time it happened.</li> <li>Check <strong>Billing &rarr; Payment History</strong> to see whether the payment was actually recorded.</li> <li>Contact AJobThing with the screenshot: <a href=\"https://wa.me/60189666610\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhatsApp +6018-966 6610</a>.</li> </ol> <div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">DO NOT PAY AGAIN</strong> until support confirms the first attempt genuinely failed &mdash; a duplicate charge falls under the same <a href=\"#/a/what-s-the-refund-and-cancellation-policy\">refund and cancellation policy</a> as any other purchase.</div>","r":["what-s-the-refund-and-cancellation-policy","is-8-sst-added-to-the-price","how-do-i-download-my-invoice-or-receipt"]},{"u":"what-s-the-refund-and-cancellation-policy","t":"What's the refund and cancellation policy?","c":9,"s":"Payment & Invoices","b":"<p><strong>All payments are final and non-refundable</strong> &mdash; AJobThing's Terms state: \"All payments are final and non-refundable. Any exception is at AJobThing's sole discretion.\"</p> <p>Job ads and accounts removed or suspended for breaking the rules get no refund either &mdash; a non-compliant ad is \"removed immediately, without refund,\" so it pays to know <a href=\"#/a/what-content-is-not-allowed-in-a-job-ad\">what content is not allowed in a job ad</a> before you post.</p> <h2>Cancelling a subscription</h2>\n<p>There is no self-service cancel button. To cancel a recurring subscription, contact your Account Manager <strong>before the NEXT CHARGE date</strong> shown on the subscription card in <strong>Billing &rarr; Packages &rarr; Subscriptions</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-subscriptions-renew\">how subscriptions renew</a>. Cancelling stops future charges; it does not refund a payment already taken, and it does not extend a package you have not used.</p>\n<h2>Protect yourself before you buy</h2> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Ask for a written quotation and check exactly what is included.</li> <li>Check the package validity &mdash; a package you cannot use in time, as covered in <a href=\"#/a/when-does-my-package-expire-and-what-happens-to-unused-job-ads\">when packages expire</a>, is a real loss with no refund to fall back on.</li> <li>Read the job ad rules so an ad is never removed after you have already paid for it.</li> </ol> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">IF SOMETHING GENUINELY WENT WRONG</strong> &mdash; a double charge, or <a href=\"#/a/my-payment-failed-or-nothing-appeared-was-i-charged\">a package that never arrived after payment</a> &mdash; contact your Account Manager with the transaction from Payment History. Exceptions are entirely discretionary, so raise it as early as possible.</div>","r":["my-payment-failed-or-nothing-appeared-was-i-charged","is-8-sst-added-to-the-price","how-do-i-download-my-invoice-or-receipt"]},{"u":"how-do-subscriptions-renew","t":"How do subscriptions renew?","c":9,"s":"Payment & Invoices","b":"<p><strong>A subscription charges the card on file automatically, on its own billing cycle</strong> &mdash; you do not need to do anything for it to renew.</p> <p>Open <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">Billing &rarr; Packages</a> &rarr; <strong>Subscriptions</strong> to see the price and how often it is billed, <strong>NEXT CHARGE</strong> (when your card is charged next), <strong>CARD ON FILE</strong> (the card brand and last four digits) and <strong>LAST CHARGE</strong> (when you were last billed). This is the tab that is distinct from <a href=\"#/a/what-s-the-difference-between-active-packages-and-subscriptions\">Active Packages</a>, which only shows what you have left to use.</p> <p>To <strong>cancel</strong>, stop or change a subscription, contact your Account Manager <em>before</em> the NEXT CHARGE date &mdash; there is no self-service cancel button, and a charge that has already gone through is covered by <a href=\"#/a/what-s-the-refund-and-cancellation-policy\">the refund and cancellation policy</a> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-i-upgrade-or-downgrade-later\">upgrading or downgrading later</a> for how a plan change is timed against that date.</p>","r":["what-s-the-difference-between-active-packages-and-subscriptions","can-i-upgrade-or-downgrade-later"]},{"u":"what-is-a-walk-in-campaign","t":"What is a Walk-In campaign?","c":10,"s":"Running A Campaign","b":"<p><strong>A Walk-In campaign is a hiring day you advertise, where jobseekers come to your venue and are interviewed on the spot</strong> &mdash; instead of applying online and waiting for a reply.</p>\n<p>It gets its own <strong>Walk-In</strong> tab on <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">your public Company Page</a>, and jobseekers browsing on <a href=\"https://www.maukerja.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Maukerja</a> or <a href=\"https://www.ricebowl.my/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ricebowl</a> can see the date, place and roles before turning up.</p>\n<h2>When it works best</h2>\n<p>High-volume, front-line hiring &mdash; retail, F&amp;B, warehouse, security &mdash; where you need many people at once and speed matters more than a long screening process. It also suits roles where candidates are more likely to show up than to complete an online application.</p>\n<h2>When a job ad is the better tool</h2>\n<p>For a single specialist role, or where you need to screen resumes before meeting anyone, a normal job ad and <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-search-for-candidates-myself\">Candidate Search</a> give you more control. Many employers run both: an ad for the pipeline, a walk-in for volume.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>The campaign is only as good as its details.</strong> Jobseekers decide whether to travel based on what you write, so the date, venue and roles need to be unambiguous &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-do-i-need-to-prepare-for-a-walk-in\">what to prepare for a Walk-In</a> before you start, then <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-create-a-walk-in-campaign\">create the campaign</a>.</div>","v":[{"i":"-N-QdMgww0c","t":"Smart Walk-In Interview by AJobThing","d":"1:45","w":"What a Walk-In campaign is and how it runs."}],"r":["how-do-i-create-a-walk-in-campaign","what-do-i-need-to-prepare-for-a-walk-in","how-do-i-manage-my-walk-in-campaigns"]},{"u":"how-do-i-create-a-walk-in-campaign","t":"How do I create a Walk-In campaign?","c":10,"s":"Running A Campaign","b":"<p><strong>Open Walk-In &rarr; Create Campaign</strong> and work through the form. Have your venue, date and role list settled first &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-do-i-need-to-prepare-for-a-walk-in\">what to prepare for a Walk-In</a>.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Company Name/Brand</strong> &mdash; which brand is hosting.</li>\n<li><strong>Campaign Name</strong> &mdash; the title jobseekers see.</li>\n<li><strong>Campaign Details</strong> &mdash; required in English, optional in Mandarin and Bahasa Melayu. There is a <strong>Tips &amp; Example</strong> link.</li>\n<li><strong>Job Positions For Hire this Location</strong> &mdash; the roles you are interviewing for.</li>\n<li><strong>Email Address</strong> for the campaign.</li>\n<li><strong>Campaign Date</strong> and <strong>Campaign Time</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Contact</strong> number, and tick the box to let jobseekers WhatsApp that number.</li>\n<li><strong>Campaign Location</strong> &mdash; search it, use your current location, or type it manually.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Continue</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Two date rules</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Choose a date from tomorrow onwards.</li>\n<li>Each campaign can last up to 3 months.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>After you submit</h2>\n<p>The campaign appears under <strong>Current Campaigns</strong> and on the <strong>Walk-In</strong> tab of <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">your Company Page</a>. Check it there as a jobseeker would, and keep an eye on it from <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-manage-my-walk-in-campaigns\">Manage Campaign</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Put the building, floor and what to bring in Campaign Details.</strong> Most no-shows are people who could not find the room. If you ticked the WhatsApp box, make sure someone is watching that number on the day.</div>","v":[{"i":"-N-QdMgww0c","t":"Smart Walk-In Interview by AJobThing","d":"1:45","w":"What a Walk-In campaign is and how it runs."}],"r":["what-do-i-need-to-prepare-for-a-walk-in","how-do-i-manage-my-walk-in-campaigns"]},{"u":"what-do-i-need-to-prepare-for-a-walk-in","t":"What do I need to prepare for a Walk-In?","c":10,"s":"Running A Campaign","b":"<p><strong>Settle these eight things before you open the form</strong> &mdash; the campaign form asks for all of them, and a walk-in advertised with vague details is what produces empty interview rooms.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Which brand is hosting</li>\n<li>A campaign name jobseekers will recognise</li>\n<li>Campaign details in English &mdash; date, venue including floor and unit, what to bring, who to ask for at reception</li>\n<li>The list of roles you are interviewing for</li>\n<li>An email address for the campaign</li>\n<li>The date (tomorrow onwards) and the time</li>\n<li>A contact number, and whether jobseekers may WhatsApp it</li>\n<li>The exact address</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Prepare the day itself, not just the listing</h2>\n<p>A walk-in brings people in bulk, so decide in advance who is interviewing, how many rooms you have, and what happens to someone you want to hire on the spot. Have your <a href=\"#/a/what-are-message-templates\">message templates</a> ready for following up afterwards, and brief reception, since they are the first person every candidate meets.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Add Mandarin and Bahasa Melayu versions if you can.</strong> Walk-in roles are often the ones where English is not the candidate's strongest language, and the details that matter most &mdash; what to bring, which floor &mdash; are exactly the ones you cannot afford to have misread.</div>\n<p>With all of this ready, go to <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-create-a-walk-in-campaign\">create the campaign</a>. If you also want an ongoing pipeline for the same roles, run a normal job ad alongside it &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-post-a-job-step-by-step\">how to post a job, step by step</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-create-a-walk-in-campaign","what-is-a-walk-in-campaign","how-do-i-manage-my-walk-in-campaigns"]},{"u":"how-do-i-manage-my-walk-in-campaigns","t":"How do I manage my Walk-In campaigns?","c":10,"s":"Running A Campaign","b":"<p><strong>Open Walk-In &rarr; Manage Campaign.</strong> Everything you have created, running or finished, is on that one page.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Go to <strong>Walk-In &rarr; Manage Campaign</strong>.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Current Campaigns</strong> for anything active or upcoming.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Past Campaigns</strong> to look back at what you have run.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Create Campaign</strong> alongside to add another &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-create-a-walk-in-campaign\">how to create a Walk-In campaign</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>With none set up you will see: \"There is no active or upcoming Walk-In Interview Campaigns.\" That message means nothing has been created yet, not that a campaign has failed.</p>\n<h2>What to check while a campaign is live</h2>\n<p>Open it and read it as a jobseeker would, on the <strong>Walk-In</strong> tab of <a href=\"#/a/what-is-my-company-page-and-where-do-jobseekers-see-it\">your Company Page</a> &mdash; particularly the date, time and venue details. If the date moves or the venue changes, update the campaign rather than relying on candidates to ask, and make sure the contact number you gave is being answered.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>For campaign questions, the page gives <strong>+6018-966 6610</strong> and <strong>sales@ajobthing.com</strong>.</strong> Quote the campaign name and date when you get in touch. For anything else, see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">how to contact AJobThing</a>.</div>\n<p>After the day, anyone you decide to take forward can be tracked through your normal pipeline &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant\">shortlist, reject or hire</a> and <a href=\"#/a/i-have-hired-someone-what-now\">I have hired someone, what now</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-create-a-walk-in-campaign","what-is-a-walk-in-campaign","what-do-i-need-to-prepare-for-a-walk-in"]},{"u":"how-do-i-invite-a-team-member","t":"How do I invite a team member?","c":11,"s":"Your Team","b":"<p><strong>Add colleagues under Team Management.</strong> Each person gets their own login, which is how the account stays traceable and how cover works when someone is away.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <strong>Team Management</strong>.</li>\n<li>Check <strong>Current Limit</strong> &mdash; it shows how many user seats are allocated and how many are free; see <a href=\"#/a/is-there-a-limit-on-team-members\">is there a limit on team members</a>.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Add User</strong> and fill in their details.</li>\n<li>Give them the role that matches what they need to do &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-the-roles-and-what-can-each-one-do\">what the roles can each do</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The list then shows each person's name, email, job title, status and <strong>Last Login</strong> &mdash; useful for spotting a seat nobody is using before you ask for more.</p>\n<h2>Why it is worth doing properly</h2>\n<p>Adding colleagues is what lets them see and reply to candidate chats, so nobody is left waiting when one person is on leave &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-my-colleagues-see-the-same-applicants-and-chats\">can my colleagues see the same applicants and chats</a>. It also keeps spending attributable, since <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">Credit History</a> records a user name against every credit spent.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Never hand out your own password instead.</strong> A shared login cannot be removed for one person, and it hides who did what &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-two-people-share-one-login\">can two people share one login</a>. If a colleague only needs the application emails, add them as an additional recipient on the job ad instead: <a href=\"#/a/who-gets-told-when-someone-applies\">who gets told when someone applies</a>.</div>","r":["what-are-the-roles-and-what-can-each-one-do","can-two-people-share-one-login"]},{"u":"is-there-a-limit-on-team-members","t":"Is there a limit on team members?","c":11,"s":"Your Team","b":"<p><strong>Yes &mdash; your account has a set number of user seats, shown in Team Management.</strong> The <strong>Current Limit</strong> block tells you how many are allocated and how many are still available.</p>\n<h2>What the page shows</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Current Limit</strong> &mdash; seats allocated and seats available.</li>\n<li><strong>Team Members</strong> &mdash; your total users.</li>\n<li><strong>Active (24h)</strong> &mdash; logins and active users in the last day.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The exact number of seats depends on your plan, so read it from the <strong>Current Limit</strong> block on your own account rather than assuming a standard figure &mdash; your package details in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">Billing &rarr; Packages</a> reflect what you hold.</p>\n<h2>If you have run out of seats</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Check <strong>Last Login</strong> on the team list for seats held by people who have left or never used the account.</li>\n<li>Remove anyone who no longer needs access, which frees a seat.</li>\n<li>If you still need more, ask your Account Manager whether additional seats can be added to your plan &mdash; or <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">contact AJobThing</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Do not solve a seat shortage by sharing a login.</strong> It removes your record of who spent which credits and makes it impossible to revoke one person's access &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-two-people-share-one-login\">can two people share one login</a>. For someone who only needs to see applications, add them as an email recipient on the job ad instead: <a href=\"#/a/who-gets-told-when-someone-applies\">who gets told when someone applies</a>.</div>\n<p>To add people while seats are free, see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-team-member\">how to invite a team member</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-invite-a-team-member","what-are-the-roles-and-what-can-each-one-do","are-credits-shared-with-my-team"]},{"u":"what-are-the-roles-and-what-can-each-one-do","t":"What are the roles, and what can each one do?","c":11,"s":"Your Team","b":"<p><strong>Three built-in roles, separated mainly by whether the person can touch billing and users.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>What it can do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Owner</strong></td><td>Full system access. Cannot be removed from the account.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Admin</strong></td><td>Full access to all employer resources and user management.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Recruiter</strong></td><td>Everything except User Management and Billing.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>You can also build your own with <strong>Create Role</strong> on the Roles tab, which is worth doing when a colleague needs something narrower than Recruiter.</p>\n<h2>How to choose</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Start everyone on <strong>Recruiter</strong> &mdash; it covers posting ads, working applicants and chatting with candidates.</li>\n<li>Give <strong>Admin</strong> only to the one or two people who genuinely need to add users or handle <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-packages-and-balances\">packages and billing</a>.</li>\n<li>Review the list when someone changes job or leaves; <strong>Last Login</strong> in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-team-member\">Team Management</a> shows who is actually using the account.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Recruiter is the right default for most colleagues.</strong> Keep billing and user management with one or two people &mdash; credits and packages are real money, and they are shared across the whole account; see <a href=\"#/a/are-credits-shared-with-my-team\">are credits shared with my team</a>.</div>\n<p>Whatever role they hold, colleagues work from the same applicant list and can see candidate conversations &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-my-colleagues-see-the-same-applicants-and-chats\">can my colleagues see the same applicants and chats</a>.</p>","r":["how-do-i-invite-a-team-member","is-there-a-limit-on-team-members","are-credits-shared-with-my-team"]},{"u":"are-credits-shared-with-my-team","t":"Are credits shared with my team?","c":11,"s":"Your Team","b":"<p><strong>Yes.</strong> Credits, job ads and candidate profiles belong to the company account, not to one person &mdash; so anyone with access can spend them, and one colleague's spending reduces what is left for everyone.</p>\n<h2>How to keep control</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Give most people the Recruiter role</strong>, which excludes Billing &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/what-are-the-roles-and-what-can-each-one-do\">what the roles can each do</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Give each person their own login, never a shared one</strong> &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-two-people-share-one-login\">can two people share one login</a>. Without this, the audit trail is worthless.</li>\n<li><strong>Check Billing &rarr; Credit History</strong> regularly. The <strong>User Name</strong> column shows who spent what, and the <strong>Item</strong> and <strong>Job</strong> columns show on what &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">where you see your credit balance and history</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Agree the rules out loud.</strong> Because contacting a candidate <a href=\"#/a/what-does-it-cost-to-contact-a-candidate\">costs 5 credits whether or not they reply</a>, it is worth telling the team to shortlist for free in a <a href=\"#/a/what-is-a-project-and-how-do-i-use-one\">Project</a> first.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\"><strong>Watch the balance as a team, not individually.</strong> Batches expire on their own dates &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/do-my-credits-expire\">do my credits expire</a> &mdash; so someone should be checking for anything marked <strong>(Expiring soon)</strong> before it lapses.</div>\n<p>Job ads and candidate profiles are separate shared balances with their own rules &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/credits-coins-job-ads-and-candidate-profiles-what-is-the-difference\">the difference between them</a>. If a balance looks wrong, Credit History is the place that explains it.</p>","r":["what-are-the-roles-and-what-can-each-one-do","where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history"]},{"u":"how-do-i-change-my-name-email-contact-number-or-password","t":"How do I change my name, email, contact number or password?","c":11,"s":"Account & Alerts","b":"<p><strong>All four are in Account Details</strong> &mdash; this page is your personal login, not your company's public information.</p>\n<h2>Name, email or contact number</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Open <strong>Account Details</strong>.</li>\n<li>Edit your name, email address or contact number.</li>\n<li>Save, then complete verification &mdash; email and phone each show <strong>Verified</strong> once confirmed.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Password</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>In <strong>Account Details</strong>, open <strong>Change Password</strong>.</li>\n<li>Enter your old password, then the new one twice. Leave it blank if you are not changing it.</li>\n<li>Save.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you cannot remember your current password, use the reset link on the login page instead &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-reset-a-forgotten-password\">how to reset a forgotten password</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"warn\"><strong>This is your personal login only.</strong> Your company name is separate and can only be changed by AJobThing support &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-edit-my-company-details-and-what-can-t-i-change\">how to edit your company details, and what you can't change</a>.</div>\n<p>Changing your login email does not change where application alerts go if a different address is set for them &mdash; check <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get\">Notifications &amp; Alerts</a> and each ad's <a href=\"#/a/who-gets-told-when-someone-applies\">Notification Settings</a> afterwards, or you may stop receiving alerts you expect. See <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get\">what is on the Settings page</a> for the company-level equivalents.</p>","r":["how-do-i-edit-my-company-details-and-what-can-t-i-change","how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get","where-do-i-see-all-my-notifications"]},{"u":"how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get","t":"How do I choose which alerts I get?","c":11,"s":"Account & Alerts","b":"<p><strong>Open Company Branding &rarr; Settings &rarr; Notifications &amp; Alerts</strong> and tick the channels you want. These are your account-wide defaults for new application alerts.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li>Go to <strong>Company Branding &rarr; Settings &rarr; Notifications &amp; Alerts</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tick the channels you want: <strong>WhatsApp</strong>, <strong>Email</strong>, <strong>SMS</strong>.</li>\n<li>Choose how often the emails come &mdash; for example <strong>Realtime (as soon they apply)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Check the address and number under <strong>Contact Information</strong> on the same page are current.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Save</strong> <em>in this block</em>. The Settings page has three blocks &mdash; Notifications &amp; Alerts, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-change-the-billing-details-on-my-invoice\">Billing Address</a> and Contact Information &mdash; and each saves separately, so editing one and saving another leaves your change unapplied. That is the most common reason a setting appears not to have taken effect.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Choosing sensibly</h2>\n<p>Realtime suits a role you are filling this week; a batched frequency keeps your inbox usable on a long-running ad. WhatsApp is worth adding for urgent roles, since it reaches you away from your desk. Nothing is lost whichever you pick &mdash; every application is in <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-everyone-who-applied\">Manage Applicants</a> regardless of the alert.</p>\n<h2>The three layers of notification</h2>\n<p>It helps to know these are separate, because that is usually why an expected alert did not arrive:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Account-wide defaults</strong> &mdash; the channels on this page, applying to every ad.</li>\n<li><strong>Per-job alerts</strong> &mdash; set in <strong>Notification Settings</strong> while writing the job ad, where you can add up to five extra recipients. A per-ad setting can differ from your account default; see <a href=\"#/a/who-gets-told-when-someone-applies\">who gets told when someone applies</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The notification bell</strong> &mdash; the in-platform log, which runs whether or not an email or WhatsApp reached you; see <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-all-my-notifications\">where you see all your notifications</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\">If the bell shows activity that never reached your inbox, the problem is the alert channel rather than the platform &mdash; work through <a href=\"#/a/i-m-not-receiving-application-emails\">I'm not receiving application emails</a>. For chat replies specifically, see <a href=\"#/a/will-i-be-told-when-a-candidate-replies\">will I be told when a candidate replies</a>.</div>\n<p>Your own name, login email and password are not on this page &mdash; they live under Account Details; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-change-my-name-email-contact-number-or-password\">how to change your name, email, contact number or password</a>.</p>","r":["who-gets-told-when-someone-applies","i-m-not-receiving-application-emails"]},{"u":"where-do-i-see-all-my-notifications","t":"Where do I see all my notifications?","c":11,"s":"Account & Alerts","b":"<p><strong>Click the bell in the top bar.</strong> It is the running log of account activity, independent of whether an email or WhatsApp alert reached you.</p>\n<p>It lists entries such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Job Published</strong> &mdash; \"We have published your job ad - &lt;title&gt;\"</li>\n<li><strong>Job Draft</strong> &mdash; \"Draft for job &lt;title&gt; has been saved.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each entry carries a date and time, so you can see exactly when something happened.</p>\n<h2>What it is most useful for</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Confirming an ad went live.</strong> A <strong>Job Published</strong> entry is proof the ad left <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live\">Pending</a> &mdash; quicker than refreshing the job list.</li>\n<li><strong>Checking a draft saved</strong> when you had to stop mid-form &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/can-i-save-a-job-ad-and-finish-later\">can I save a job ad and finish later</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Working out the timeline</strong> when you need to tell support when something happened &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-report-a-problem\">how to report a problem</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tip\">This list is not the same as your email or WhatsApp alerts, which are set in <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get\">Notifications &amp; Alerts</a>. If the bell shows activity that never reached your inbox, the problem is with the alert channel, not the platform &mdash; see <a href=\"#/a/i-m-not-receiving-application-emails\">I'm not receiving application emails</a>.</div>\n<p>For applicants and chats waiting on you, the counters on your <a href=\"#/a/what-do-the-numbers-on-my-dashboard-mean\">Dashboard</a> are the quicker check.</p>","r":["why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live","how-do-i-change-my-name-email-contact-number-or-password","how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get"]},{"u":"a-page-is-stuck-loading-or-shows-empty-boxes","t":"A page is stuck loading or shows empty boxes","c":12,"s":"Common Problems","b":"<p>A stuck or empty-looking page is almost always a loading delay or a browser issue, not lost data.</p> <p>Job lists, <a href=\"#/a/where-do-i-see-my-credit-balance-and-history\">credit history</a> and payment history all load in the background, and this is slower on accounts with a lot of activity. A browser extension can also block part of a page from rendering, which is one reason switching browsers helps rule things out.</p> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Wait a few more seconds before assuming something is wrong.</li> <li>Reload the page.</li> <li>Narrow down what you're loading — use the status tabs on the <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-the-job-ads-application-page\">Job Ads &amp; Application page</a>, or show fewer items per page.</li> <li>Try a different browser, or an incognito window, to rule out an extension.</li> </ol> <p>If the page loads normally afterward, it was a temporary delay. If it still won't load after all four steps, that's worth reporting rather than retrying indefinitely.</p> <div class=\"tip\"><strong class=\"lb\">REPORT IT</strong> Use Send Feedback, choose the Problem Type, and attach a screenshot of the error — below 2MB, .JPEG, .JPG, .PNG or .GIF only. See <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-report-a-problem\">how to report a problem</a> for what to include so support can act on it faster.</div>","r":["how-do-i-report-a-problem","chat-says-not-authorized","the-ai-button-is-greyed-out-or-says-i-ve-hit-my-limit"]},{"u":"chat-says-not-authorized","t":"Chat says \"Not authorized\"","c":12,"s":"Common Problems","b":"<p><strong>\"Not authorized\"</strong> means you opened the chat window directly, instead of launching it from inside your dashboard.</p> <p>The full message reads: \"Not authorized. Open chat from your ajobthing dashboard to continue.\" <a href=\"#/a/what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message\">AJobThing Chat</a> only works when opened from a session that started at ajobthing.com — a bookmarked or shared inner chat link doesn't carry that login.</p> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Go to ajobthing.com and log in.</li> <li>Open <strong>Chat &rarr; Chat with jobseeker</strong> from the side menu, or click the chat icon in the top bar.</li> </ol> <p>This is the same route you would use to <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-start-a-new-chat-with-a-candidate\">start a new chat with a candidate</a>, so once you're in, everything should work as normal.</p> <div class=\"tip\">Do not bookmark the inner chat address. Bookmark the dashboard Chat page instead, and always reach a conversation by logging in first.</div> <p>If you still see \"Not authorized\" after logging in this way, <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">contact AJobThing</a> — it may be a session issue rather than a bookmark.</p>","r":["what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message","a-page-is-stuck-loading-or-shows-empty-boxes","the-ai-button-is-greyed-out-or-says-i-ve-hit-my-limit"]},{"u":"the-ai-button-is-greyed-out-or-says-i-ve-hit-my-limit","t":"The AI button is greyed out or says I've hit my limit","c":12,"s":"Common Problems","b":"<p>The AI button turns grey either because required fields aren't filled in yet, or because you've used up today's AI allowance.</p> <p><strong>Greyed out with a message to fill in details:</strong> the form is telling you \"Please fill up the job details and location first\" — <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-use-ai-to-fill-in-my-job-ad\">using AI to fill in your job ad</a> needs at least the basic job details and location before it has anything to work from.</p> <p><strong>\"I've hit my limit\":</strong> each AI helper has its own daily count, shown next to the button as, for example, <a href=\"#/a/what-does-3-3-left-today-mean\">3/3 left today</a>. Once that count reaches zero, the button offers <strong>Fill in Manually</strong> instead.</p> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Check whether the job details and location are already filled in — if not, add them first.</li> <li>If the button mentions a limit, check the count next to it to see how many uses you have left today.</li> <li>Once the count is at zero, use <strong>Fill in Manually</strong> to type the fields yourself, or come back tomorrow and try the AI helper again.</li> </ol> <p>Importing a job from a file or URL runs on <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-import-a-job-from-a-file-or-url\">its own separate daily allowance</a>, so hitting one limit does not affect the other.</p> <div class=\"tip\">If the button stays greyed out even after the job details and location are complete, that's not expected — report it rather than assuming it's a limit issue.</div>","r":["how-do-i-use-ai-to-fill-in-my-job-ad","what-does-3-3-left-today-mean"]},{"u":"a-candidate-s-phone-number-or-email-doesn-t-work","t":"A candidate's phone number or email doesn't work","c":12,"s":"Common Problems","b":"<p>A phone number or email that doesn't work usually means either a formatting issue or an outdated profile, not a fault on your end.</p> <p>Candidates enter their own contact details on <a href=\"#/a/what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile\">their profile</a>, so a mistyped number or an email they no longer check will fail the same way it would for anyone else.</p> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Check the number format — Malaysian mobiles need the right prefix.</li> <li>Try the other channel. If the phone fails, email them, or message them through <a href=\"#/a/what-is-ajobthing-chat-and-who-can-i-message\">Chat</a>.</li> <li>Check <strong>Last Active</strong> on their profile. A profile that hasn't been active in a while often carries old contact details.</li> </ol> <p>If all three checks come up empty, it's most likely a data issue with that specific profile rather than something on AJobThing's side.</p> <div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">NOT REFUNDED</strong> Contact details are not refunded if they turn out to be out of date — see the <a href=\"#/a/what-s-the-refund-and-cancellation-policy\">refund and cancellation policy</a>. Check <strong>Last Active</strong> before you spend credits on someone.</div>","r":["what-is-on-a-candidate-s-profile","what-s-the-refund-and-cancellation-policy"]},{"u":"how-do-i-contact-ajobthing","t":"How do I contact AJobThing?","c":12,"s":"Getting Help","b":"<p>The fastest way to reach AJobThing depends on what you need — general help goes through <a href=\"https://wa.me/60189666610\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhatsApp +6018-966 6610</a>, while other requests have their own dedicated email address.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>What you need</th><th>Where to go</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>General help</td><td>WhatsApp or call <strong>+6018-966 6610</strong></td></tr> <tr><td>General questions</td><td>hello@ajobthing.com</td></tr> <tr><td>Packages and quotations</td><td>sales@ajobthing.com</td></tr> <tr><td>A job ad stuck in Pending</td><td><strong>View Pending Reason</strong> on the job card — the window shows the number to call. See <a href=\"#/a/why-is-my-job-ad-pending-and-when-will-it-go-live\">why a job ad is Pending</a> for what happens next.</td></tr> <tr><td>Data protection</td><td>dataprivacy@ajobthing.com</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Match what you need against the rows above — pricing questions go to sales, day-to-day issues go to general help.</li> <li>Inside the dashboard, use <strong>Contact Us</strong> in the side menu to request a call, a WhatsApp message or an email from a hiring consultant instead of hunting for the details yourself.</li> <li>Check your dashboard side menu for your Account Manager's name and contact — most accounts have one assigned.</li> </ol> <p>Expect a reply from a person through whichever channel you use, whether that's a hiring consultant or your Account Manager. If your issue is really a bug or something not behaving as expected, use <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-report-a-problem\">how to report a problem</a> instead, so it reaches the team that fixes it.</p>","r":["how-do-i-report-a-problem"]},{"u":"how-do-i-report-a-problem","t":"How do I report a problem?","c":12,"s":"Getting Help","b":"<p>Use <strong>Send Feedback</strong> inside the dashboard to report a problem, rather than emailing or messaging support directly.</p> <p>This routes your report to whoever handles that Problem Type, along with your screenshot and details — useful for things like a <a href=\"#/a/a-page-is-stuck-loading-or-shows-empty-boxes\">page stuck loading</a> or <a href=\"#/a/chat-says-not-authorized\">Chat showing \"Not authorized\"</a>.</p> <ol class=\"steps\"> <li>Choose the <strong>Problem Type</strong> that matches what you experienced.</li> <li>Write what happened, in your own words.</li> <li>Attach a screenshot — below 2MB, .JPEG, .JPG, .PNG or .GIF only.</li> </ol> <h2>What gets it solved fastest</h2> <p>Include the page you were on, what you clicked, what you expected to happen, what happened instead, the exact error wording, and a screenshot. The more specific the report, the less back-and-forth it takes to fix.</p> <p>If your question isn't a bug but a general query about your account or billing, it's usually faster to <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-contact-ajobthing\">contact AJobThing</a> directly instead.</p>","r":["how-do-i-contact-ajobthing"]},{"u":"what-costs-credits-what-costs-a-job-ad-what-s-free","t":"What costs credits, what costs a job ad, what's free","c":13,"s":"Cheat Sheets","b":"<p>Every action on AJobThing falls into one of three buckets: it <strong>costs credits</strong>, it <strong>uses your job ad or chat quota</strong>, or it's <strong>free</strong>.</p> <p><a href=\"#/a/what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on\">Credits</a> pay for candidate-side actions — reaching out to someone or asking for more of their information.</p> <h2>Costs credits</h2> <table> <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Start a chat with a candidate from Candidate Search</td><td>5 credits</td></tr> <tr><td>Start a chat with a <a href=\"#/a/what-are-recommended-candidates\">Recommended Candidate</a></td><td>5 credits</td></tr> <tr><td>Request Resume on a profile</td><td>5 credits</td></tr> <tr><td>Chat with an extra applicant after your monthly chats run out</td><td>5 credits</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <h2>Uses your plan</h2> <ul> <li>Publishing a job ad — <a href=\"#/a/which-job-ads-are-free\">uses one job ad from your quota</a>, unless it is an eligible free type</li> <li>Starting a conversation with an applicant — 1 chat from your monthly total</li> <li>Boosters — paid add-ons on top of your plan</li> </ul> <h2>Free</h2> <p>Searching candidates, opening a profile, reading experience and education, Save to Project, saving a draft, previewing an ad, and editing your Company Page all cost nothing. Answering Company FAQ questions is better than free — it <em>earns</em> you 2 credits each.</p> <p>Similar job ads are free and do not count toward your monthly job ad limit.</p> <div class=\"note\">This table covers the actions currently on the platform. If something you did isn't listed here, your credit history will show exactly what it was charged as.</div>","r":["what-are-credits-and-what-can-i-spend-them-on","every-deadline-and-expiry-in-one-table","before-you-click-what-cannot-be-undone"]},{"u":"every-deadline-and-expiry-in-one-table","t":"Every deadline and expiry in one table","c":13,"s":"Cheat Sheets","b":"<p>This table collects every fixed timeframe on AJobThing in one place, so you don't have to check each feature separately.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>How long</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Job ad stays published</td><td>30 days, unless a promotion extends it</td></tr> <tr><td>Job ad auto-extend</td><td>Renews after 30 days, no repost needed</td></tr> <tr><td>Free credits with a job ad</td><td>10 credits, valid 30 days</td></tr> <tr><td>Profile unlock expiry</td><td>90 days</td></tr> <tr><td>Candidate package validity</td><td>10 days (20 pack) / 3 months (80) / 6 months (320)</td></tr> <tr><td>Company verification</td><td>Up to 3 working days</td></tr> <tr><td>Job ad review</td><td>Published within 24 hours</td></tr> <tr><td>Credit expiry warning email</td><td>7 days before expiry</td></tr> <tr><td>Monthly chats</td><td>Reset on your renewal date, do not carry forward</td></tr> <tr><td>Data breach notification</td><td>Within 48 hours of becoming aware</td></tr> <tr><td>Delete data of candidates you did not hire</td><td>Within 6 months of the hiring decision</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>A few of these are worth planning around: your job ad's 30-day run is explained further in <a href=\"#/a/how-long-does-a-job-ad-stay-live\">how long a job ad stays live</a>, and unused credits follow their own schedule, covered in <a href=\"#/a/do-my-credits-expire\">do my credits expire</a>. A profile you unlock stays accessible for its own window too — see <a href=\"#/a/how-long-does-an-unlocked-profile-last\">how long an unlocked profile lasts</a> before deciding when to unlock someone.</p> <div class=\"tip\">Set a reminder a few days before a deadline that matters to you — renewal, unlock expiry, or a job ad ending — rather than relying on catching every notification.</div>","r":["do-my-credits-expire","how-long-does-a-job-ad-stay-live"]},{"u":"before-you-click-what-cannot-be-undone","t":"Before you click: what cannot be undone","c":13,"s":"Cheat Sheets","b":"<p>Most actions on AJobThing are reversible, or just for your own reference — a handful genuinely cannot be undone, either because they reach a real person or because they spend money.</p> <h2>These reach a real person immediately</h2> <ul> <li>Setting an applicant to <strong>Shortlisted</strong>, <strong>Rejected</strong> or <strong>Hired</strong> — see <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant\">how to shortlist, reject or hire an applicant</a> before you decide</li> <li>Starting a chat — the candidate gets a request from your company, and <a href=\"#/a/what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged\">a chat has its own cost and timing</a></li> <li>Sending an <a href=\"#/a/how-do-i-invite-a-candidate-to-an-interview\">interview invitation</a></li> <li><strong>Bulk chat</strong> — one click, many candidates, all at once</li> </ul> <h2>These spend money or change your account</h2> <ul> <li><strong>Mark Filled / Hired</strong> closes the job to new applications</li> <li>Buying a plan, package or booster</li> <li><strong>Delete</strong> on an Employee Story</li> </ul> <h2>Safe to explore</h2> <p>Save as Draft, Job Ad Preview, Bookmark, Hide, Internal Comment, Save to Project, and every AI suggestion are all safe to click around — nothing is committed until you click <strong>Confirm</strong>.</p> <div class=\"warn\"><strong class=\"lb\">TAKE CARE</strong> Bulk chat needs the most care of everything on this list. Read your message twice before sending it to a whole list — once it's sent, every candidate on that list has already received it.</div>","r":["how-do-i-shortlist-reject-or-hire-an-applicant","what-does-a-chat-cost-and-when-am-i-charged"]}],"alias":{"how-do-i-change-my-email-or-password":"how-do-i-change-my-name-email-contact-number-or-password","how-will-i-be-notified-about-new-applicants":"how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get","will-this-job-ad-be-free-or-use-one-of-my-paid-job-ads":"which-job-ads-are-free","what-does-n-a-mean-instead-of-a-number":"what-are-the-coloured-tags-on-my-job-cards","what-does-n-total-ads-mean-on-a-job-card":"what-are-the-coloured-tags-on-my-job-cards","what-is-on-the-settings-page":"how-do-i-choose-which-alerts-i-get","numbers-on-different-screens-don-t-match":"what-do-the-numbers-on-my-dashboard-mean","can-a-recruitment-agency-post-jobs-for-clients":"what-can-i-do-with-a-candidate-s-details"},"calias":{"policies":